const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"Westminster Abbey.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Dead March. Enter the funeral of King Henry the Fifth, attended on by the Duke of Bedford, Regent of France; the Duke of Gloucester, Protector; the Duke of Exeter, the Earl of Warwick, the Bishop of Winchester, Heralds, &c.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["Hung be the heavens with black, yield day to night!","Comets, importing change of times and states,","Brandish your crystal tresses in the sky,","And with them scourge the bad revolting stars","That have consented unto Henry's death!","King Henry the Fifth, too famous to live long!","England ne'er lost a king of so much worth."],"id":2,"m":"Let the skies be hung with black and day give way to night! Comets, which foretell changes in times and states, wave your bright tails across the sky and whip the wicked, rebellious stars that agreed to Henry's death! King Henry the Fifth, too famous to live long! England never lost a king so worthy."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["England ne'er had a king until his time.","Virtue he had, deserving to command:","His brandish'd sword did blind men with his beams:","His arms spread wider than a dragon's wings;","His sparkling eyes, replete with wrathful fire,","More dazzled and drove back his enemies","Than mid-day sun fierce bent against their faces.","What should I say? his deeds exceed all speech:","He ne'er lift up his hand but conquered."],"id":3,"m":"England never had a true king until him. He had virtue fit to command; his flashing sword blinded men with its light; his arms spread wider than a dragon's wings; his sparkling eyes, full of angry fire, dazzled and drove back his enemies more than the midday sun blazing in their faces. What can I say? His deeds are beyond words. He never raised his hand without conquering."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["We mourn in black: why mourn we not in blood?","Henry is dead and never shall revive:","Upon a wooden coffin we attend,","And death's dishonourable victory","We with our stately presence glorify,","Like captives bound to a triumphant car.","What! shall we curse the planets of mishap","That plotted thus our glory's overthrow?","Or shall we think the subtle-witted French","Conjurers and sorcerers, that afraid of him","By magic verses have contriv'd his end?"],"id":4,"m":"We mourn in black; why don't we mourn in blood? Henry is dead and will never come back to life. We walk beside a wooden coffin and, with our grand presence, glorify death's shameful victory, like prisoners tied to a conqueror's chariot. What! Shall we curse the unlucky planets that plotted the end of our glory? Or shall we think the cunning French are conjurers and sorcerers who, afraid of him, killed him with magic spells?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["He was a king bless'd of the King of kings;","Unto the French the dreadful judgment-day","So dreadful will not be as was his sight.","The battles of the Lord of hosts he fought:","The Church's prayers made him so prosperous."],"id":5,"m":"He was a king blessed by the King of kings. To the French, Judgement Day itself will not be as terrifying as the sight of him was. He fought the battles of the Lord of hosts; the Church's prayers made him so successful."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["The church! where is it? Had not churchmen pray'd,","His thread of life had not so soon decay'd:","None do you like but an effeminate prince,","Whom, like a school-boy, you may over-awe."],"id":6,"m":"The Church! Where is it? If churchmen hadn't prayed, his life would not have ended so soon. You like nothing but a weak, soft prince whom you can bully like a schoolboy."},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["Gloucester, whate'er we like, thou art Protector,","And lookest to command the Prince and realm.","Thy wife is proud; she holdeth thee in awe,","More than God or religious churchmen may."],"id":7,"m":"Gloucester, whatever we like, you are Protector, and you aim to rule the Prince and the kingdom. Your wife is proud; she has more control over you than God or the clergy do."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Name not religion, for thou lov'st the flesh,","And ne'er throughout the year to church thou go'st,","Except it be to pray against thy foes."],"id":8,"m":"Don't talk about religion, for you love the pleasures of the flesh, and you never go to church all year except to pray against your enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["Cease, cease these jars and rest your minds in peace:","Let's to the altar: heralds, wait on us:","Instead of gold, we'll offer up our arms;","Since arms avail not, now that Henry's dead.","Posterity, await for wretched years,","When at their mothers' moist eyes babes shall suck,","Our isle be made a marish of salt tears,","And none but women left to wail the dead.","Henry the Fifth, thy ghost I invocate:","Prosper this realm, keep it from civil broils,","Combat with adverse planets in the heavens!","A far more glorious star thy soul will make","Than Julius Caesar or bright—"],"id":9,"m":"Stop, stop these quarrels and calm yourselves. Let's go to the altar. Heralds, attend us. Instead of gold, we'll offer up our weapons, since weapons are no use now Henry is dead. Future ages, expect miserable years, when babies will suck at their mothers' tear-wet eyes, our island will become a marsh of salt tears, and only women will be left to mourn the dead. Henry the Fifth, I call on your spirit: make this kingdom prosper, keep it from civil wars, fight the hostile planets in the heavens! Your soul will make a far more glorious star than Julius Caesar or bright—"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":10},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["My honourable lords, health to you all!","Sad tidings bring I to you out of France,","Of loss, of slaughter, and discomfiture:","Guienne, Champagne, Rheims, Orleans,","Paris, Guysors, Poictiers, are all quite lost."],"id":11,"m":"My honourable lords, health to you all! I bring sad news from France of loss, slaughter and defeat. Guienne, Champagne, Rheims, Orleans, Paris, Gisors and Poitiers are all lost."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["What say'st thou, man, before dead Henry's corse?","Speak softly; or the loss of those great towns","Will make him burst his lead and rise from death."],"id":12,"m":"What are you saying, man, in front of dead Henry's body? Speak quietly, or the loss of those great towns will make him burst his lead coffin and rise from the dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Is Paris lost? Is Rouen yielded up","If Henry were recall'd to life again,","These news would cause him once more yield the ghost."],"id":13,"m":"Is Paris lost? Has Rouen surrendered? If Henry were brought back to life, this news would make him die again."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["How were they lost? What treachery was us'd?"],"id":14,"m":"How were they lost? What treachery was used?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["No treachery; but want of men and money.","Amongst the soldiers this is muttered,","That here you maintain several factions,","And whilst a field should be dispatch'd and fought,","You are disputing of your generals:","One would have lingering wars with little cost;","Another would fly swift, but wanteth wings;","A third thinks, without expense at all,","By guileful fair words peace may be obtain'd.","Awake, awake, English nobility!","Let not sloth dim your honours new-begot:","Cropp'd are the flower-de-luces in your arms;","Of England's coat one half is cut away."],"id":15,"m":"No treachery, but a shortage of men and money. The soldiers are muttering that here at home you keep up rival factions, and while a battle should be fought and won, you are arguing about your generals. One wants a long war that costs little; another wants to move fast but has no means to; a third thinks peace can be won by clever, smooth words, without any spending at all. Wake up, wake up, English nobles! Don't let idleness tarnish your newly won honours. The French lilies have been cut from your coat of arms; half of England's shield has been hacked away."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Were our tears wanting to this funeral,","These tidings would call forth their flowing tides."],"id":16,"m":"If our tears were lacking at this funeral, this news would call them out in floods."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["Me they concern; Regent I am of France.","Give me my steeled coat. I'll fight for France.","Away with these disgraceful wailing robes!","Wounds will I lend the French instead of eyes,","To weep their intermissive miseries."],"id":17,"m":"This news concerns me: I am Regent of France. Give me my steel armour. I'll fight for France. Away with these shameful mourning clothes! I'll give the French wounds to weep with instead of eyes, to cry over their own endless sufferings."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter to them another Messenger.","id":18},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Lords, view these letters full of bad mischance.","France is revolted from the English quite,","Except some petty towns of no import:","The Dauphin Charles is crowned king in Rheims;","The Bastard of Orleans with him is join'd;","Reignier, Duke of Anjou, doth take his part;","The Duke of Alencon flieth to his side."],"id":19,"m":"Lords, read these letters full of bad news. France has rebelled completely against the English, except for a few unimportant towns. The Dauphin Charles has been crowned king in Rheims; the Bastard of Orleans has joined him; Reignier, Duke of Anjou, supports him; the Duke of Alencon is hurrying to his side."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["The Dauphin crowned king! all fly to him!","O, whither shall we fly from this reproach?"],"id":20,"m":"The Dauphin crowned king! Everyone running to him! Oh, where can we run to escape this shame?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["We will not fly, but to our enemies' throats.","Bedford, if thou be slack, I'll fight it out."],"id":21,"m":"We won't run anywhere but at our enemies' throats. Bedford, if you are slow, I'll fight it out myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["Gloucester, why doubt'st thou of my forwardness?","An army have I muster'd in my thoughts,","Wherewith already France is overrun."],"id":22,"m":"Gloucester, why do you doubt my eagerness? I have already raised an army in my mind and with it overrun France."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter another Messenger.","id":23},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["My gracious lords, to add to your laments,","Wherewith you now bedew King Henry's hearse,","I must inform you of a dismal fight","Betwixt the stout Lord Talbot and the French."],"id":24,"m":"My gracious lords, to add to the grief with which you now wet King Henry's coffin, I must tell you of a terrible battle between brave Lord Talbot and the French."},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["What! wherein Talbot overcame? is't so?"],"id":25,"m":"What! Talbot won? Is that it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["O, no; wherein Lord Talbot was o'erthrown:","The circumstance I'll tell you more at large.","The tenth of August last this dreadful lord,","Retiring from the siege of Orleans,","Having full scarce six thousand in his troop,","By three and twenty thousand of the French","Was round encompassed and set upon.","No leisure had he to enrank his men;","He wanted pikes to set before his archers;","Instead whereof sharp stakes pluck'd out of hedges","They pitched in the ground confusedly,","To keep the horsemen off from breaking in.","More than three hours the fight continued;","Where valiant Talbot above human thought","Enacted wonders with his sword and lance:","Hundreds he sent to hell, and none durst stand him;","Here, there, and every where, enrag'd he slew:","The French exclaim'd, the devil was in arms;","All the whole army stood agaz'd on him.","His soldiers spying his undaunted spirit","A Talbot! a Talbot! cried out amain,","And rush'd into the bowels of the battle.","Here had the conquest fully been seal'd up,","If Sir John Fastolfe had not play'd the coward.","He, being in the vaward, plac'd behind","With purpose to relieve and follow them,","Cowardly fled, not having struck one stroke.","Hence grew the general wreck and massacre;","Enclosed were they with their enemies:","A base Walloon, to win the Dauphin's grace,","Thrust Talbot with a spear into the back;","Whom all France with their chief assembled strength","Durst not presume to look once in the face."],"id":26,"m":"Oh no; Lord Talbot was defeated. I'll tell you the details. On the tenth of August last, as this fearsome lord was withdrawing from the siege of Orleans with barely six thousand men, he was surrounded and attacked by twenty-three thousand French. He had no time to draw up his men in ranks. He had no pikes to set in front of his archers, so instead they hammered sharp stakes pulled from hedges into the ground in a rough line to keep the cavalry from breaking in. The fight went on for more than three hours, and brave Talbot performed superhuman wonders with sword and lance. He sent hundreds to hell, and nobody dared stand against him. He killed in a rage here, there and everywhere. The French cried that the devil himself was fighting; the whole army stood staring at him. His soldiers, seeing his fearless spirit, shouted \"A Talbot! A Talbot!\" and charged into the heart of the battle. The victory would have been certain if Sir John Fastolfe had not acted the coward. He was in the vanguard, placed behind them to support and follow, but he ran away like a coward without striking a single blow. That caused the general disaster and slaughter: they were surrounded by enemies. A low-born Walloon, to win the Dauphin's favour, stabbed Talbot in the back with a spear, a man whom the whole of France with all its forces never dared look in the face."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["Is Talbot slain? then I will slay myself,","For living idly here in pomp and ease,","Whilst such a worthy leader, wanting aid,","Unto his dastard foemen is betray'd."],"id":27,"m":"Is Talbot dead? Then I'll kill myself for living here idle in luxury and comfort while such a worthy leader, without help, is handed over to his cowardly enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["O no, he lives; but is took prisoner,","And Lord Scales with him, and Lord Hungerford:","Most of the rest slaughter'd or took likewise."],"id":28,"m":"Oh no, he's alive, but taken prisoner, and Lord Scales and Lord Hungerford with him. Most of the rest were killed or captured too."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["His ransom there is none but I shall pay:","I'll hale the Dauphin headlong from his throne:","His crown shall be the ransom of my friend;","Four of their lords I'll change for one of ours.","Farewell, my masters; to my task will I;","Bonfires in France forthwith I am to make","To keep our great Saint George's feast withal:","Ten thousand soldiers with me I will take,","Whose bloody deeds shall make an Europe quake."],"id":29,"m":"I alone will pay his ransom. I'll drag the Dauphin headfirst from his throne; his crown will be the ransom for my friend. I'll exchange four of their lords for one of ours. Goodbye, gentlemen; I'll get to my work. I must go to France at once to light bonfires for our great Saint George's feast. I'll take ten thousand soldiers whose bloody deeds will make all Europe tremble."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["So you had need; for Orleans is besieg'd;","The English army is grown weak and faint:","The Earl of Salisbury craveth supply,","And hardly keeps his men from mutiny,","Since they, so few, watch such a multitude."],"id":30,"m":"You'll need them, for Orleans is under siege. The English army has grown weak and tired. The Earl of Salisbury is begging for reinforcements and can barely keep his men from mutiny, since so few of them have to watch over so many."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Remember, lords, your oaths to Henry sworn,","Either to quell the Dauphin utterly,","Or bring him in obedience to your yoke."],"id":31,"m":"Remember, lords, the oaths you swore to Henry: either to crush the Dauphin completely or bring him under your rule."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["I do remember it, and here take my leave","To go about my preparation."],"id":32,"m":"I do remember, and I'll say goodbye now to go and make my preparations."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":33},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["I'll to the Tower with all the haste I can,","To view the artillery and munition;","And then I will proclaim young Henry king."],"id":34,"m":"I'll go to the Tower as fast as I can to inspect the artillery and weapons, and then I'll proclaim young Henry king."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":35},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["To Eltham will I, where the young King is,","Being ordain'd his special governor;","And for his safety there I'll best devise."],"id":36,"m":"I'll go to Eltham, where the young King is, since I've been appointed his special guardian, and I'll arrange his safety there as best I can."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":37},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["Each hath his place and function to attend:","I am left out; for me nothing remains.","But long I will not be Jack out of office:","The King from Eltham I intend to steal,","And sit at chiefest stern of public weal."],"id":38,"m":"Everyone has his place and job to do; I am left out, nothing is left for me. But I won't be out of office for long. I intend to snatch the King away from Eltham and sit at the helm of the state."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":39},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"France. Before Orleans.","id":40},{"t":"sd","text":"Sound a Flourish. Enter Charles, Alencon, and Reignier, marching with Drum and Soldiers.","id":41},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Mars his true moving, even as in the heavens","So in the earth, to this day is not known:","Late did he shine upon the English side;","Now we are victors; upon us he smiles.","What towns of any moment but we have?","At pleasure here we lie near Orleans;","Otherwhiles the famish'd English, like pale ghosts,","Faintly besiege us one hour in a month."],"id":42,"m":"Even now nobody knows the true movement of Mars, the god of war, on earth any more than in the heavens. Lately he shone on the English; now we are winning and he smiles on us. What important towns are there that we don't hold? We camp at our ease here near Orleans, while every so often the starving English, like pale ghosts, weakly besiege us for an hour a month."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALENCON","lines":["They want their porridge and their fat bull beeves","Either they must be dieted like mules,","And have their provender tied to their mouths,","Or piteous they will look, like drowned mice."],"id":43,"m":"They miss their porridge and their fat beef. Either they'll have to be fed like mules, with nosebags tied to their mouths, or they'll look pitiful, like drowned mice."},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["Let's raise the siege: why live we idly here?","Talbot is taken, whom we wont to fear:","Remaineth none but mad-brain'd Salisbury;","And he may well in fretting spend his gall,","Nor men nor money hath he to make war."],"id":44,"m":"Let's break the siege. Why are we sitting here idle? Talbot, whom we used to fear, is captured. Only mad Salisbury is left, and he can fret away his anger for all the good it will do; he has neither men nor money to make war."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Sound, sound alarum! we will rush on them.","Now for the honour of the forlorn French!","Him I forgive my death that killeth me","When he sees me go back one foot or flee."],"id":45,"m":"Sound the alarm! We'll charge them. Now for the honour of the desperate French! I forgive any man who kills me if he sees me take one step back or run."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":46},{"t":"sd","text":"Here alarum; they are beaten back by the English, with great loss. Re-enter Charles, Alencon, and Reignier.","id":47},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Who ever saw the like? what men have I!","Dogs! cowards! dastards! I would ne'er have fled,","But that they left me 'midst my enemies."],"id":48,"m":"Who ever saw anything like it? What men I have! Dogs! Cowards! Sneaks! I would never have run away if they hadn't left me surrounded by enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["Salisbury is a desperate homicide;","He fighteth as one weary of his life.","The other lords, like lions wanting food,","Do rush upon us as their hungry prey."],"id":49,"m":"Salisbury is a reckless killer; he fights like a man tired of life. The other lords, like starving lions, rush at us as if we were their prey."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALENCON","lines":["Froissart, a countryman of ours, records,","England all Olivers and Rowlands bred","During the time Edward the Third did reign.","More truly now may this be verified;","For none but Samsons and Goliases","It sendeth forth to skirmish. One to ten!","Lean raw-bon'd rascals! who would e'er suppose","They had such courage and audacity?"],"id":50,"m":"Froissart, a Frenchman, writes that in Edward the Third's reign England bred nothing but heroes like Oliver and Roland. That can be said even more truly now, for it sends out nothing but Samsons and Goliaths to fight. One against ten! Thin, bony wretches! Who would have thought they had such courage and daring?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Let's leave this town; for they are hare-brain'd slaves,","And hunger will enforce them to be more eager:","Of old I know them; rather with their teeth","The walls they'll tear down than forsake the siege."],"id":51,"m":"Let's leave this town, for they are wild, reckless men, and hunger will make them fiercer. I know them of old: they'll tear down the walls with their teeth rather than give up the siege."},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["I think by some odd gimmors or device","Their arms are set like clocks, still to strike on;","Else ne'er could they hold out so as they do.","By my consent, we'll even let them alone."],"id":52,"m":"I think their arms are set by some odd gears or device, like clocks, to keep on striking; otherwise they could never hold out as they do. If you ask me, we'll just leave them alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALENCON","lines":["Be it so."],"id":53,"m":"So be it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Bastard of Orleans.","id":54},{"t":"sp","who":"BASTARD","lines":["Where's the Prince Dauphin? I have news for him."],"id":55,"m":"Where's the Prince Dauphin? I have news for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Bastard of Orleans, thrice welcome to us."],"id":56,"m":"Bastard of Orleans, three times welcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASTARD","lines":["Methinks your looks are sad, your cheer appall'd:","Hath the late overthrow wrought this offence?","Be not dismay'd, for succour is at hand:","A holy maid hither with me I bring,","Which by a vision sent to her from heaven","Ordained is to raise this tedious siege,","And drive the English forth the bounds of France.","The spirit of deep prophecy she hath,","Exceeding the nine sibyls of old Rome:","What's past and what's to come she can descry.","Speak, shall I call her in? Believe my words,","For they are certain and unfallible."],"id":57,"m":"You look sad, and your faces are pale with fear. Has the recent defeat caused this? Don't lose heart, for help is near. I have brought a holy maid with me who, through a vision sent to her from heaven, has been chosen to break this long siege and drive the English out of France. She has the gift of deep prophecy, greater than the nine sibyls of ancient Rome. She can see what has happened and what is to come. Tell me, shall I call her in? Believe me, my words are true and certain."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Go, call her in. [Exit Bastard]","But first, to try her skill,","Reignier, stand thou as Dauphin in my place;","Question her proudly; let thy looks be stern:","By this means shall we sound what skill she hath."],"id":58,"m":"Go, call her in. (The Bastard leaves) But first, to test her skill, Reignier, take my place as Dauphin. Question her haughtily and look stern. This way we'll find out what skill she has."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter the Bastard of Orleans, with Joan La Pucelle.","id":59},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["Fair maid, is 't thou wilt do these wondrous feats?"],"id":60,"m":"Lovely girl, are you the one who will do these wonderful deeds?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Reignier is 't thou that thinkest to beguile me?","Where is the Dauphin? Come, come from behind;","I know thee well, though never seen before.","Be not amazed, there's nothing hid from me.","In private will I talk with thee apart.","Stand back, you lords, and give us leave awhile."],"id":61,"m":"Reignier, are you the one who thinks he can trick me? Where is the Dauphin? Come, come out from behind there. I know you well, though I've never seen you before. Don't be amazed; nothing is hidden from me. I will talk with you privately. Stand back, lords, and leave us alone a while."},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["She takes upon her bravely at first dash."],"id":62,"m":"She acts boldly right from the start."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Dauphin, I am by birth a shepherd's daughter,","My wit untrain'd in any kind of art.","Heaven and our Lady gracious hath it pleased","To shine on my contemptible estate:","Lo, whilst I waited on my tender lambs","And to sun's parching heat display'd my cheeks,","God's mother deigned to appear to me,","And in a vision full of majesty","Will'd me to leave my base vocation,","And free my country from calamity:","Her aid she promised and assured success:","In complete glory she reveal'd herself;","And, whereas I was black and swart before,","With those clear rays which she infused on me","That beauty am I bless'd with which you may see.","Ask me what question thou canst possible,","And I will answer unpremeditated:","My courage try by combat, if thou dar'st,","And thou shalt find that I exceed my sex.","Resolve on this, thou shalt be fortunate,","If thou receive me for thy warlike mate."],"id":63,"m":"Dauphin, I was born a shepherd's daughter, my mind untrained in any skill. Heaven and our gracious Lady have chosen to shine on my lowly state. While I was tending my young lambs, my cheeks exposed to the scorching sun, the mother of God appeared to me in a majestic vision and told me to leave my humble work and free my country from disaster. She promised her help and guaranteed success. She revealed herself in full glory, and whereas before I was dark and sunburnt, the bright rays she poured on me gave me the beauty you can see. Ask me any question you like and I'll answer without preparing. Test my courage in combat if you dare, and you'll find I go beyond what is expected of my sex. Be sure of this: you'll be lucky if you accept me as your partner in war."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Thou hast astonish'd me with thy high terms;","Only this proof I'll of thy valour make,","In single combat thou shalt buckle with me,","And if thou vanquishest, thy words are true;","Otherwise I renounce all confidence."],"id":64,"m":"You have amazed me with your bold words. I'll test your courage in just one way: you will fight me in single combat. If you win, your words are true; if not, I'll reject all trust in you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["I am prepared: here is my keen-edg'd sword,","Deck'd with five flower-de-luces on each side,","The which at Touraine, in Saint Katharine's church-yard,","Out of a great deal of old iron I chose forth."],"id":65,"m":"I am ready. Here is my sharp sword, decorated with five fleurs-de-lis on each side, which I chose out of a great heap of old iron in Saint Katharine's churchyard in Touraine."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Then come, o' God's name; I fear no woman."],"id":66,"m":"Then come on, in God's name. I'm not afraid of any woman."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["And while I live, I'll ne'er fly from a man."],"id":67,"m":"And as long as I live, I'll never run from a man."},{"t":"sd","text":"Here they fight, and Joan La Pucelle overcomes.","id":68},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Stay, stay thy hands; thou art an Amazon,","And fightest with the sword of Deborah."],"id":69,"m":"Stop, stop! You are an Amazon, and you fight with the sword of Deborah."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Christ's Mother helps me, else I were too weak."],"id":70,"m":"Christ's mother helps me, or I would be too weak."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Whoe'er helps thee, 'tis thou that must help me:","Impatiently I burn with thy desire;","My heart and hands thou hast at once subdued.","Excellent Pucelle, if thy name be so,","Let me thy servant and not sovereign be:","'Tis the French Dauphin sueth to thee thus."],"id":71,"m":"Whoever helps you, it's you who must help me. I burn impatiently with desire for you; you have conquered my heart and my hands at once. Excellent Pucelle, if that's your name, let me be your servant, not your king. It's the French Dauphin who begs you like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["I must not yield to any rites of love,","For my profession's sacred from above:","When I have chased all thy foes from hence,","Then will I think upon a recompense."],"id":72,"m":"I must not give way to any love-making, for my calling is holy, from heaven. When I have chased all your enemies away, then I'll think about a reward."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Meantime look gracious on thy prostrate thrall."],"id":73,"m":"Meanwhile, look kindly on your humbled slave."},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["My lord, methinks, is very long in talk."],"id":74,"m":"My lord is talking for a very long time, I think."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALENCON","lines":["Doubtless he shrives this woman to her smock;","Else ne'er could he so long protract his speech."],"id":75,"m":"No doubt he's hearing this woman's confession down to her underwear; otherwise he couldn't drag out his talk so long."},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["Shall we disturb him, since he keeps no mean?"],"id":76,"m":"Shall we interrupt him, since he doesn't know when to stop?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALENCON","lines":["He may mean more than we poor men do know:","These women are shrewd tempters with their tongues."],"id":77,"m":"He may mean more than we poor men know. These women are clever temptresses with their tongues."},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["My lord, where are you? what devise you on?","Shall we give over Orleans, or no?"],"id":78,"m":"My lord, what are you up to? What are you planning? Shall we give up Orleans or not?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Why, no, I say; distrustful recreants!","Fight till the last gasp; I will be your guard."],"id":79,"m":"No, I say! You doubting cowards! Fight to the last breath; I will protect you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["What she says I'll confirm: we'll fight it out:"],"id":80,"m":"I'll back up what she says: we'll fight it out."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Assign'd am I to be the English scourge.","This night the siege assuredly I'll raise:","Expect Saint Martin's summer, halcyon days,","Since I have entered into these wars.","Glory is like a circle in the water,","Which never ceaseth to enlarge itself","Till by broad spreading it disperse to nought.","With Henry's death the English circle ends;","Dispersed are the glories it included.","Now am I like that proud insulting ship","Which Caesar and his fortune bare at once."],"id":81,"m":"I am appointed to be the scourge of the English. Tonight I will certainly break the siege. Expect a late summer of calm, peaceful days now that I have joined these wars. Glory is like a ripple in water, which keeps spreading wider until it spreads so far it vanishes. With Henry's death the English ripple ends; the glories it held are scattered. Now I am like that proud, triumphant ship that carried Caesar and his good fortune together."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Was Mahomet inspired with a dove?","Thou with an eagle art inspired then.","Helen, the mother of great Constantine,","Nor yet Saint Philip's daughters, were like thee.","Bright star of Venus, fall'n down on the earth,","How may I reverently worship thee enough?"],"id":82,"m":"Was Mahomet inspired by a dove? Then you are inspired by an eagle. Neither Helen, the mother of great Constantine, nor Saint Philip's daughters were your equal. Bright star of Venus fallen to earth, how can I worship you enough?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALENCON","lines":["Leave off delays, and let us raise the siege."],"id":83,"m":"Stop delaying, and let's break the siege."},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["Woman, do what thou canst to save our honors;","Drive them from Orleans and be immortalized."],"id":84,"m":"Woman, do what you can to save our honour. Drive them from Orleans and be famous forever."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Presently we'll try: come, let's away about it:","No prophet will I trust, if she prove false."],"id":85,"m":"We'll try at once. Come, let's get on with it. I'll trust no prophet again if she proves false."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":86},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"III","loc":"London. Before the Tower.","id":87},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Duke of Gloucester, with his Serving-men in blue coats.","id":88},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["I am come to survey the Tower this day:","Since Henry's death, I fear, there is conveyance.","Where be these warders that they wait not here?","Open the gates; 'tis Gloucester that calls."],"id":89,"m":"I've come to inspect the Tower today. Since Henry's death I'm afraid there's been some theft. Where are the guards? Why aren't they on duty? Open the gates; it's Gloucester calling."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST WARDER","lines":["[Within] Who's there that knocks so imperiously?"],"id":90,"m":"(From inside) Who's knocking so arrogantly?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVING-MAN","lines":["It is the noble Duke of Gloucester."],"id":91,"m":"It's the noble Duke of Gloucester."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND WARDER","lines":["[Within] Whoe'er he be, you may not be let in."],"id":92,"m":"(From inside) Whoever he is, you can't come in."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVING-MAN","lines":["Villains, answer you so the lord protector?"],"id":93,"m":"You villains, is that how you answer the Lord Protector?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST WARDER","lines":["[Within] The Lord protect him! so we answer him:","We do no otherwise than we are will'd."],"id":94,"m":"(From inside) The Lord protect him! That's our answer. We're only doing as we're told."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Who willed you? or whose will stands but mine?","There's none protector of the realm but I.","Break up the gates, I'll be your warrantize:","Shall I be flouted thus by dunghill grooms?"],"id":95,"m":"Who told you? Whose orders count except mine? There's no protector of the kingdom but me. Break down the gates; I'll take responsibility. Am I to be mocked like this by filthy servants?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Gloucester's men rush at the Tower Gates, and Woodvile the Lieutenant speaks within.","id":96},{"t":"sp","who":"WOODVILE","lines":["What noise is this? what traitors have we here?"],"id":97,"m":"What's this noise? What traitors are these?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Lieutenant, is it you whose voice I hear?","Open the gates; here's Gloucester that would enter."],"id":98,"m":"Lieutenant, is that your voice? Open the gates; it's Gloucester who wants to come in."},{"t":"sp","who":"WOODVILE","lines":["Have patience, noble duke; I may not open;","The Cardinal of Winchester forbids:","From him I have express commandment","That thou nor none of thine shall be let in."],"id":99,"m":"Be patient, noble Duke; I can't open them. The Cardinal of Winchester forbids it. He has given me strict orders that neither you nor any of your men are to be let in."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Faint-hearted Woodvile, prizest him 'fore me?","Arrogant Winchester, that haughty prelate","Whom Henry, our late sovereign, ne'er could brook?","Thou art no friend to God or to the King.","Open the gates, or I'll shut thee out shortly."],"id":100,"m":"Cowardly Woodvile, do you value him above me? Arrogant Winchester, that proud churchman whom Henry, our late king, could never stand? You are no friend to God or the King. Open the gates, or I'll soon throw you out."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVING-MEN","lines":["Open the gates unto the lord protector,","Or we'll burst them open, if that you come not quickly."],"id":101,"m":"Open the gates to the Lord Protector, or we'll burst them open if you don't come quickly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter to the Protector at the Tower Gates Winchester and his men in tawny coats.","id":102},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["How now, ambitious Humphry! what means this?"],"id":103,"m":"What now, ambitious Humphrey! What does this mean?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Peel'd priest, dost thou command me to be shut out?"],"id":104,"m":"Bald-headed priest, are you ordering me to be shut out?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["I do, thou most usurping proditor,","And not protector, of the king or realm."],"id":105,"m":"I am, you complete traitor, the usurper, not the protector, of the King and kingdom."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Stand back, thou manifest conspirator,","Thou that contrivedst to murder our dead lord;","Thou that givest whores indulgences to sin:","I'll canvass thee in thy broad cardinal's hat,","If thou proceed in this thy insolence."],"id":106,"m":"Stand back, you obvious conspirator, you who plotted to murder our dead king, you who sell pardons to whores so they can sin! I'll toss you in your broad cardinal's hat if you keep up this insolence."},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["Nay, stand thou back; I will not budge a foot:","This be Damascus, be thou cursed Cain,","To slay thy brother Abel, if thou wilt."],"id":107,"m":"No, you stand back; I won't move an inch. Let this be Damascus, where Cain killed Abel; be cursed Cain and kill your brother if you want."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["I will not slay thee, but I'll drive thee back:","Thy scarlet robes as a child's bearing-cloth","I'll use to carry thee out of this place."],"id":108,"m":"I won't kill you, but I'll drive you back. I'll use your scarlet robes like a baby's christening blanket to carry you out of here."},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["Do what thou darest; I beard thee to thy face."],"id":109,"m":"Do your worst; I defy you to your face."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["What! am I dared and bearded to my face?","Draw, men, for all this privileged place;","Blue coats to tawny coats. Priest, beware your beard;","I mean to tug it and to cuff you soundly:","Under my feet I stamp thy cardinal's hat:","In spite of pope or dignities of church,","Here by the cheeks I'll drag thee up and down."],"id":110,"m":"What! Am I defied and insulted to my face? Draw, men, even though this is protected ground. Blue coats against tawny coats! Priest, watch your beard; I mean to pull it and give you a good beating. I'll stamp your cardinal's hat under my feet. In spite of the Pope or any church dignitaries, I'll drag you up and down by the cheeks."},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["Gloucester, thou wilt answer this before the","pope."],"id":111,"m":"Gloucester, you'll answer to the Pope for this."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Winchester goose, I cry, a rope! a rope!","Now beat them hence; why do you let them stay?","Thee I'll chase hence, thou wolf in sheep's array.","Out, tawny coats! out, scarlet hypocrite!"],"id":112,"m":"Winchester goose! I call for a rope, a rope! Now drive them away; why do you let them stay? I'll chase you away, you wolf in sheep's clothing. Out, tawny coats! Out, scarlet hypocrite!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Here Gloucester's men beat out the Cardinal's men, and enter in the hurly-burly the Mayor of London and his Officers.","id":113},{"t":"sp","who":"MAYOR","lines":["Fie, lords! that you, being supreme magistrates,","Thus contumeliously should break the peace!"],"id":114,"m":"Shame, lords! You are the highest officers of the state, and you insult the law by breaking the peace like this!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Peace, mayor! thou know'st little of my wrongs:","Here's Beaufort, that regards nor God nor king,","Hath here distrain'd the Tower to his use."],"id":115,"m":"Quiet, Mayor! You know little of the wrongs done to me. Here's Beaufort, who respects neither God nor King, and has seized the Tower for his own use."},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["Here's Gloucester, a foe to citizens,","One that still motions war and never peace,","O'ercharging your free purses with large fines,","That seeks to overthrow religion,","Because he is protector of the realm,","And would have armour here out of the Tower,","To crown himself king and suppress the prince."],"id":116,"m":"Here's Gloucester, an enemy to citizens, a man who always proposes war and never peace, who loads your purses with heavy taxes, who wants to overthrow the Church because he is Protector of the kingdom, and who wants weapons out of the Tower to crown himself king and push aside the Prince."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["I will not answer thee with words, but blows."],"id":117,"m":"I won't answer you with words, but with blows."},{"t":"sd","text":"Here they skirmish again.","id":118},{"t":"sp","who":"MAYOR","lines":["Nought rests for me in this tumultuous strife","But to make open proclamation:","Come, officer; as loud as e'er thou canst:","Cry."],"id":119,"m":"There's nothing for me to do in this violent quarrel but make a public proclamation. Come, officer, as loud as you can: cry it out."},{"t":"sp","who":"OFFICER","lines":["All manner of men assembled here in arms","this day against God's peace and the king's, we charge","and command you, in his highness' name, to repair to","your several dwelling-places; and not to wear, handle, or","use any sword, weapon, or dagger, henceforward, upon","pain of death."],"id":120,"m":"All men gathered here in arms today against the peace of God and the King: we order and command you, in his Highness's name, to go back to your homes and not to wear, handle or use any sword, weapon or dagger from now on, on pain of death."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Cardinal, I'll be no breaker of the law;","But we shall meet, and break our minds at large."],"id":121,"m":"Cardinal, I won't break the law; but we'll meet and speak our minds in full."},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["Gloucester, we will meet; to thy cost, be sure;","Thy heart-blood I will have for this day's work."],"id":122,"m":"Gloucester, we'll meet, and it will cost you, be sure. I'll have your heart's blood for this day's work."},{"t":"sp","who":"MAYOR","lines":["I'll call for clubs, if you will not away.","This Cardinal's more haughty than the devil."],"id":123,"m":"I'll call for the constables with their clubs if you won't leave. This Cardinal is more arrogant than the devil."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Mayor, farewell: thou dost but what thou mayst."],"id":124,"m":"Mayor, goodbye. You're only doing what you must."},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["Abominable Gloucester, guard thy head;","For I intend to have it ere long."],"id":125,"m":"Hateful Gloucester, guard your head, for I intend to have it before long."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt, severally, Gloucester and Winchester with their Serving-men.","id":126},{"t":"sp","who":"MAYOR","lines":["See the coast clear'd, and then we will depart.","Good God, these nobles should such stomachs bear!","I myself fight not once in forty year."],"id":127,"m":"Make sure the area is clear, and then we'll leave. Good God, how these nobles carry grudges! I don't fight once in forty years."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":128},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"IV","loc":"Orleans.","id":129},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter, on the walls, a Master Gunner and his Boy.","id":130},{"t":"sp","who":"MASTER GUNNER","lines":["Sirrah, thou know'st how Orleans is besieged,","And how the English have the suburbs won."],"id":131,"m":"Boy, you know how Orleans is under siege and how the English have captured the suburbs."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["Father, I know; and oft have shot at them,","Howe'er unfortunate I miss'd my aim."],"id":132,"m":"Father, I know, and I've often shot at them, though unluckily I missed."},{"t":"sp","who":"MASTER GUNNER","lines":["But now thou shalt not. Be thou ruled by me:","Chief master-gunner am I of this town;","Something I must do to procure me grace.","The prince's espials have informed me","How the English, in the suburbs close intrench'd,","Wont through a secret grate of iron bars","In yonder tower to overpeer the city,","And thence discover how with most advantage","They may vex us with shot or with assault.","To intercept this inconvenience,","A piece of ordnance 'gainst it I have placed;","And even these three days have I watch'd,","If I could see them.","Now do thou watch, for I can stay no longer.","If thou spy'st any, run and bring me word;","And thou shalt find me at the governor's."],"id":133,"m":"But now you won't miss. Do as I say. I'm the chief gunner of this town; I must do something to win favour. The Prince's spies have told me that the English, dug in close in the suburbs, are in the habit of looking over the city through a secret iron grating in that tower, to work out how best to attack us with gunfire or assault. To stop this nuisance, I've aimed a cannon at it, and for three days now I've watched to see if I could spot them. Now you keep watch, for I can't stay any longer. If you see anyone, run and tell me; you'll find me at the governor's."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":134},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["Father, I warrant you; take you no care;","I'll never trouble you, if I may spy them."],"id":135,"m":"Father, I promise you; don't worry. I won't bother you if I can spot them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":136},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter, on the turrets, the Lords Salisbury and Talbot, Sir William Glansdale, Sir Thomas Gargrave, and others.","id":137},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["Talbot, my life, my joy, again return'd!","How wert thou handled being prisoner?","Or by what means got'st thou to be releas'd?","Discourse, I prithee, on this turret's top."],"id":138,"m":"Talbot, my life, my joy, back again! How were you treated as a prisoner? How did you manage to get released? Tell me about it, please, here on this turret."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["The Duke of Bedford had a prisoner","Call'd the brave Lord Ponton de Santrailles;","For him was I exchanged and ransomed.","But with a baser man of arms by far","Once in contempt they would have barter'd me:","Which I disdaining scorn'd, and craved death","Rather than I would be so vile-esteem'd.","In fine, redeem'd I was as I desired.","But, O! the treacherous Fastolfe wounds my heart,","Whom with my bare fists I would execute,","If I now had him brought into my power."],"id":139,"m":"The Duke of Bedford had a prisoner called the brave Lord Ponton de Santrailles; I was exchanged and ransomed for him. Once, to insult me, they wanted to swap me for a far lower-ranking soldier, which I scornfully refused, asking for death rather than be valued so cheaply. In the end I was released as I wanted. But oh, treacherous Fastolfe wounds my heart! I would kill him with my bare hands if I had him in my power now."},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["Yet tell'st thou not how thou wert entertain'd."],"id":140,"m":"You still haven't told me how you were treated."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["With scoffs and scorns and contumelious taunts.","In open market-place produced they me,","To be a public spectacle to all:","Here, said they, is the terror of the French,","The scarecrow that affrights our children so.","Then broke I from the officers that led me,","And with my nails digg'd stones out of the ground","To hurl at the beholders of my shame;","My grisly countenance made others fly;","None durst come near for fear of sudden death.","In iron walls they deem'd me not secure;","So great fear of my name 'mongst them was spread","That they supposed I could rend bars of steel,","And spurn in pieces posts of adamant:","Wherefore a guard of chosen shot I had,","That walk'd about me every minute while;","And if I did but stir out of my bed,","Ready they were to shoot me to the heart."],"id":141,"m":"With mockery, scorn and insulting taunts. They put me on show in the open market-place as a public spectacle for everyone. \"Here,\" they said, \"is the terror of the French, the bogeyman who frightens our children.\" Then I broke away from the officers who led me and dug stones out of the ground with my nails to throw at the people watching my shame. My grim face made others run; nobody dared come near for fear of instant death. They didn't think I was safe even behind iron walls; the fear of my name spread so far among them that they believed I could break steel bars and kick unbreakable posts to pieces. So I had a guard of picked marksmen who walked around me every minute, and if I so much as got out of bed they were ready to shoot me through the heart."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Boy with a linstock.","id":142},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["I grieve to hear what torments you endured,","But we will be revenged sufficiently.","Now it is supper-time in Orleans:","Here, through this grate, I count each one,","And view the Frenchmen how they fortify:","Let us look in; the sight will much delight thee.","Sir Thomas Gargrave and Sir William Glansdale,","Let me have your express opinions","Where is best place to make our battery next."],"id":143,"m":"I'm sorry to hear what torments you suffered, but we'll get revenge enough. It's supper time in Orleans now. Through this grating I can count each one of them and see how the French are fortifying. Let's look in; the sight will cheer you. Sir Thomas Gargrave and Sir William Glansdale, give me your opinions on the best place for our next bombardment."},{"t":"sp","who":"GARGRAVE","lines":["I think, at the north gate; for there stand lords."],"id":144,"m":"I think the north gate, for the lords are there."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLANSDALE","lines":["And I, here, at the bulwark of the bridge."],"id":145,"m":"And I think here, at the fortification on the bridge."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["For aught I see, this city must be famish'd,","Or with light skirmishes enfeebled."],"id":146,"m":"As far as I can see, this city must be starved out or worn down with small attacks."},{"t":"sd","text":"Here they shoot. Salisbury and Gargrave fall.","id":147},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["O Lord, have mercy on us, wretched sinners!"],"id":148,"m":"O Lord, have mercy on us, miserable sinners!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GARGRAVE","lines":["O Lord, have mercy on me, woful man!"],"id":149,"m":"O Lord, have mercy on me, a wretched man!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["What chance is this that suddenly hath cross'd us?","Speak, Salisbury: at least, if thou canst speak:","How farest thou, mirror of all martial men?","One of thy eyes and thy cheek's side struck off!","Accursed tower! accursed fatal hand","That hath contrived this woful tragedy!","In thirteen battles Salisbury o'ercame;","Henry the Fifth he first train'd to the wars;","Whilst any trump did sound, or drum struck up,","His sword did ne'er leave striking in the field.","Yet liv'st thou, Salisbury? though thy speech doth fail,","One eye thou hast, to look to heaven for grace:","The sun with one eye vieweth all the world.","Heaven, be thou gracious to none alive,","If Salisbury wants mercy at thy hands!","Bear hence his body; I will help to bury it,","Sir Thomas Gargrave, hast thou any life?","Speak unto Talbot; nay, look up to him.","Salisbury, cheer thy spirit with this comfort,","Thou shalt not die whiles—","He beckons with his hand and smiles on me,","As who should say 'When I am dead and gone,","Remember to avenge me on the French.'","Plantagenet, I will; and like thee, Nero,","Play on the lute, beholding the towns burn;","Wretched shall France be only in thy name."],"id":150,"m":"What disaster is this that has suddenly struck us? Speak, Salisbury, if you can speak at all. How are you, model of all soldiers? One of your eyes and the side of your face blown off! Cursed tower! Cursed deadly hand that caused this sad tragedy! Salisbury won thirteen battles; he trained Henry the Fifth in war. While any trumpet sounded or drum beat, his sword never stopped striking on the battlefield. Are you still alive, Salisbury? Though your voice fails, you have one eye left to look to heaven for mercy. The sun sees the whole world with one eye. Heaven, show mercy to no one alive if you deny it to Salisbury! Carry his body away; I'll help bury it. Sir Thomas Gargrave, are you alive? Speak to Talbot; no, look up at him. Salisbury, take comfort in this: you won't die while— He beckons and smiles at me, as if to say, \"When I am dead and gone, remember to take revenge on the French for me.\" Plantagenet, I will; and like Nero I'll play the lute while watching the towns burn. Your name alone will bring misery to France."},{"t":"sd","text":"Here an alarum, and it thunders and lightens.","id":151},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","cont":1,"lines":["What stir is this? what tumult's in the heavens?","Whence cometh this alarum and the noise?"],"id":152,"m":"What's this commotion? What uproar is this in the skies? Where is this alarm and noise coming from?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":153},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["My lord, my lord, the French have gather'd head:","The Dauphin, with one Joan la Pucelle join'd,","A holy prophetess new risen up,","Is come with a great power to raise the siege."],"id":154,"m":"My lord, my lord, the French have gathered their forces. The Dauphin, joined by one Joan la Pucelle, a holy prophetess who has newly appeared, has come with a great army to break the siege."},{"t":"sd","text":"Here SALISBURY lifteth himself up and groans.","id":155},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Hear, hear how dying Salisbury doth groan!","It irks his heart he cannot be revenged.","Frenchmen, I'll be a Salisbury to you:","Pucelle or puzzel, dolphin or dogfish,","Your hearts I'll stamp out with my horse's heels,","And make a quagmire of your mingled brains.","Convey me Salisbury into his tent,","And then we'll try what these dastard Frenchmen dare."],"id":156,"m":"Listen, listen how dying Salisbury groans! It pains his heart that he can't take revenge. Frenchmen, I'll be a Salisbury to you. Pucelle or slut, dauphin or dogfish, I'll stamp out your hearts with my horse's hooves and turn your mixed-up brains into a swamp. Carry Salisbury to his tent for me, and then we'll see what these cowardly Frenchmen dare to do."},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Exeunt.","id":157},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"V","loc":"Orleans. Before the walls.","id":158},{"t":"sd","text":"Here an alarum again: and Talbot pursueth the Dauphin, and driveth him: then enter Joan La Pucelle, driving Englishmen before her, and exit after them: then re-enter Talbot.","id":159},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Where is my strength, my valor, and my force?","Our English troops retire, I cannot stay them:","A woman clad in armour chaseth them."],"id":160,"m":"Where is my strength, my courage and my force? Our English troops are retreating; I can't stop them. A woman dressed in armour is chasing them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter La Pucelle.","id":161},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","cont":1,"lines":["Here, here she comes. I'll have a bout with thee;","Devil or devil's dam, I'll conjure thee:","Blood will I draw on thee, thou art a witch,","And straightway give thy soul to him thou servest."],"id":162,"m":"Here, here she comes. I'll have a fight with you. Devil or devil's mother, I'll drive the spirit out of you. I'll draw your blood, you witch, and send your soul straight to the master you serve."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Come, come, 'tis only I that must disgrace thee."],"id":163,"m":"Come on, come on; I'm the one who will shame you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Here they fight.","id":164},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Heavens, can you suffer hell so to prevail?","My breast I'll burst with straining of my courage,","And from my shoulders crack my arms asunder,","But I will chastise this high-minded strumpet."],"id":165,"m":"Heavens, can you let hell win like this? I'll burst my chest straining my courage and crack my arms from my shoulders, but I'll punish this arrogant whore."},{"t":"sd","text":"They fight again.","id":166},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Talbot, farewell; thy hour is not yet come:","I must go victual Orleans forthwith."],"id":167,"m":"Talbot, goodbye; your time hasn't come yet. I must go and bring supplies into Orleans at once."},{"t":"sd","text":"A short alarum: then enter the town with soldiers.","id":168},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","cont":1,"lines":["O'ertake me, if thou canst; I scorn thy strength.","Go, go, cheer up thy hungry-starved men;","Help Salisbury to make his testament:","This day is ours, as many more shall be."],"id":169,"m":"Catch me if you can; I scorn your strength. Go, go and cheer up your starving men. Help Salisbury make his will. This day is ours, and many more will be."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":170},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["My thoughts are whirled like a potter's wheel;","I know not where I am, nor what I do;","A witch, by fear, not force, like Hannibal,","Drives back our troops and conquers as she lists.","So bees with smoke and doves with noisome stench","Are from their hives and houses driven away.","They call'd us for our fierceness English dogs;","Now, like to whelps, we crying run away."],"id":171,"m":"My thoughts are spinning like a potter's wheel; I don't know where I am or what I'm doing. A witch, like Hannibal, drives back our troops by fear, not force, and wins as she pleases. That's how bees are driven from their hives by smoke and doves from their dovecotes by foul smells. They used to call us English dogs because we were so fierce; now we run away whimpering like puppies."},{"t":"sd","text":"A short alarum.","id":172},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","cont":1,"lines":["Hark, countrymen! either renew the fight,","Or tear the lions out of England's coat;","Renounce your soil, give sheep in lions' stead:","Sheep run not half so treacherous from the wolf,","Or horse or oxen from the leopard,","As you fly from your oft-subdued slaves."],"id":173,"m":"Listen, countrymen! Either start fighting again or tear the lions off England's coat of arms. Give up your country and put sheep in place of lions. Sheep don't run from the wolf, or horses and oxen from the leopard, half as disloyally as you run from the slaves you have so often beaten."},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Here another skirmish.","id":174},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","cont":1,"lines":["It will not be: retire into your trenches:","You all consented unto Salisbury's death,","For none would strike a stroke in his revenge.","Pucelle is ent'red into Orleans,","In spite of us or aught that we could do.","O, would I were to die with Salisbury!","The shame hereof will make me hide my head."],"id":175,"m":"It's no use. Retreat to your trenches. You all agreed to Salisbury's death, since none of you would strike a blow to avenge him. Pucelle has got into Orleans in spite of us and everything we could do. Oh, I wish I had died with Salisbury! The shame of this will make me hide my face."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Talbot. Alarum; retreat; flourish.","id":176},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"VI","loc":"Orleans. On the walls.","id":177},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter, on the walls, La Pucelle, Charles, Reignier, Alencon, and Soldiers.","id":178},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Advance our waving colours on the walls;","Rescued is Orleans from the English:","Thus Joan la Pucelle hath perform'd her word."],"id":179,"m":"Raise our waving flags on the walls. Orleans is rescued from the English. So Joan la Pucelle has kept her word."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Divinest creature, Astraea's daughter,","How shall I honour thee for this success?","Thy promises are like Adonis' gardens","That one day bloom'd and fruitful were the next.","France, triumph in thy glorious prophetess!","Recover'd is the town of Orleans.","More blessed hap did ne'er befall our state."],"id":180,"m":"Most divine creature, daughter of Astraea, goddess of justice, how shall I honour you for this success? Your promises are like the gardens of Adonis, which flowered one day and bore fruit the next. France, rejoice in your glorious prophetess! The town of Orleans is won back. Nothing more fortunate ever happened to our country."},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["Why ring not out the bells aloud throughout the town?","Dauphin, command the citizens make bonfires","And feast and banquet in the open streets,","To celebrate the joy that God hath given us."],"id":181,"m":"Why aren't the bells ringing out loud all through the town? Dauphin, order the citizens to light bonfires and feast and celebrate in the open streets to mark the joy God has given us."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALENCON","lines":["All France will be replete with mirth and joy,","When they shall hear how we have play'd the men."],"id":182,"m":"All France will be full of happiness and joy when they hear how bravely we have fought."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["'Tis Joan, not we, by whom the day is won;","For which I will divide my crown with her;","And all the priests and friars in my realm","Shall in procession sing her endless praise.","A statelier pyramis to her I'll rear","Than Rhodope's of Memphis ever was;","In memory of her when she is dead,","Her ashes, in an urn more precious","Than the rich-jewel'd coffer of Darius,","Transported shall be at high festivals","Before the kings and queens of France.","No longer on Saint Denis will we cry,","But Joan la Pucelle shall be France's saint.","Come in, and let us banquet royally","After this golden day of victory."],"id":183,"m":"It's Joan, not us, who won the day, and for that I'll share my crown with her, and all the priests and friars in my kingdom will sing her praises forever in processions. I'll build her a grander pyramid than Rhodope's in Memphis. In memory of her, when she is dead, her ashes will be carried at great festivals before the kings and queens of France in an urn more precious than the jewelled chest of Darius. We'll no longer pray to Saint Denis; Joan la Pucelle will be the saint of France. Come in, and let's feast like kings after this golden day of victory."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Exeunt.","id":184},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"Before Orleans.","id":185},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Sergeant of a band, with two Sentinels.","id":186},{"t":"sp","who":"SERGEANT","lines":["Sirs, take your places and be vigilant:","If any noise or soldier you perceive","Near to the walls, by some apparent sign","Let us have knowledge at the court of guard."],"id":187,"m":"Men, take your places and stay alert. If you notice any noise or soldier near the walls, let us know at the guardhouse with some clear signal."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENTINEL","lines":["Sergeant, you shall. [Exit Sergeant]","Thus are poor servitors,","When others sleep upon their quiet beds,","Constrain'd to watch in darkness, rain and cold."],"id":188,"m":"Sergeant, we will. (The Sergeant leaves) This is how poor servants have to keep watch in darkness, rain and cold while others sleep in their quiet beds."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Talbot, Bedford, Burgundy, and forces, with scaling-ladders, their drums beating a dead march.","id":189},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Lord Regent, and redoubted Burgundy,","By whose approach the regions of Artois,","Wallon and Picardy are friends to us,","This happy night the Frenchmen are secure,","Having all day caroused and banqueted:","Embrace we then this opportunity,","As fitting best to quittance their deceit","Contriv'd by art and baleful sorcery."],"id":190,"m":"Lord Regent, and honoured Burgundy, whose coming has made the regions of Artois, Walloon Flanders and Picardy our friends: tonight the French feel safe after feasting and drinking all day. Let's take this chance, as the best way to pay back their trickery, plotted with magic and evil sorcery."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["Coward of France, how much he wrongs his fame,","Despairing of his own arm's fortitude,","To join with witches and the help of hell!"],"id":191,"m":"The coward of France! How he damages his reputation, giving up on the strength of his own arm and joining with witches and the help of hell!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["Traitors have never other company.","But what 's that Pucelle whom they term so pure?"],"id":192,"m":"Traitors never keep any other company. But who is this Pucelle they call so pure?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["A maid, they say."],"id":193,"m":"A virgin, they say."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["A maid! and be so martial!"],"id":194,"m":"A virgin! And so warlike!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["Pray God she prove not masculine ere long,","If underneath the standard of the French","She carry armour as she hath begun."],"id":195,"m":"Pray God she doesn't turn out to be with a man's child soon, if she keeps bearing arms under the French flag as she has started."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Well, let them practice and converse with spirits:","God is our fortress, in whose conquering name","Let us resolve to scale their flinty bulwarks."],"id":196,"m":"Well, let them plot and deal with spirits. God is our fortress, and in his conquering name let's be determined to climb their stone walls."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["Ascend, brave Talbot; we will follow thee."],"id":197,"m":"Climb, brave Talbot; we'll follow you."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Not all together: better far, I guess,","That we do make our entrance several ways;","That, if it chance the one of us do fail,","The other yet may rise against their force."],"id":198,"m":"Not all together. Much better, I think, that we break in at different places, so that if one of us happens to fail, the other can still rise against their forces."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["Agreed: I'll to yond corner."],"id":199,"m":"Agreed. I'll go to that corner."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["And I to this."],"id":200,"m":"And I to this one."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["And here will Talbot mount, or make his grave.","Now, Salisbury, for thee, and for the right","Of English Henry, shall this night appear","How much in duty I am bound to both."],"id":201,"m":"And here Talbot will climb, or make his grave here. Now, Salisbury, for you and for the rights of English Henry, tonight will show how much I owe to both of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SENTINEL","lines":["Arm! arm! the enemy doth make assault!"],"id":202,"m":"To arms! To arms! The enemy is attacking!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Cry: 'St George,' 'A Talbot.'.","id":203},{"t":"sd","text":"The French leap over the walls in their shirts. Enter, several ways, the Bastard of Orleans, Alencon, and Reignier, half ready, and half unready.","id":204},{"t":"sp","who":"ALENCON","lines":["How now, my lords! what, all unready so?"],"id":205,"m":"What's this, my lords? What, all half-dressed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BASTARD","lines":["Unready! aye, and glad we 'scap'd so well."],"id":206,"m":"Half-dressed! Yes, and glad we escaped so well."},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["'Twas time, I trow, to wake and leave our beds,","Hearing alarums at our chamber-doors."],"id":207,"m":"It was time, I think, to wake up and leave our beds, hearing alarms at our bedroom doors."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALENCON","lines":["Of all exploits since first I follow'd arms,","Ne'er heard I of a warlike enterprise","More venturous or desperate than this."],"id":208,"m":"Of all the exploits I've heard of since I first became a soldier, I never heard of a military venture more daring or desperate than this."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASTARD","lines":["I think this Talbot be a fiend of hell."],"id":209,"m":"I think this Talbot is a devil from hell."},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["If not of hell, the heavens, sure, favor him."],"id":210,"m":"If not from hell, then the heavens must surely favour him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALENCON","lines":["Here cometh Charles: I marvel how he sped."],"id":211,"m":"Here comes Charles. I wonder how he got on."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASTARD","lines":["Tut, holy Joan was his defensive guard."],"id":212,"m":"Pooh, holy Joan was protecting him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Charles and La Pucelle.","id":213},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Is this thy cunning, thou deceitful dame?","Didst thou at first, to flatter us withal,","Make us partakers of a little gain,","That now our loss might be ten times so much?"],"id":214,"m":"Is this your skill, you deceitful woman? Did you first give us a little gain, to flatter us, so that our loss now might be ten times as great?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Wherefore is Charles impatient with his friend?","At all times will you have my power alike?","Sleeping or waking must I still prevail,","Or will you blame and lay the fault on me?","Improvident soldiers! had your watch been good,","This sudden mischief never could have fall'n."],"id":215,"m":"Why is Charles impatient with his friend? Do you expect my power to be the same at all times? Must I always win, asleep or awake, or else you'll blame me and put the fault on me? Careless soldiers! If you had kept good watch, this sudden disaster could never have happened."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Duke of Alencon, this was your default,","That, being captain of the watch to-night,","Did look no better to that weighty charge."],"id":216,"m":"Duke of Alencon, this was your fault: you were captain of the watch tonight and didn't look after that important duty better."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALENCON","lines":["Had all your quarters been as safely kept","As that whereof I had the government,","We had not been thus shamefully surprised."],"id":217,"m":"If all your areas had been as well guarded as the one I was in charge of, we wouldn't have been so shamefully surprised."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASTARD","lines":["Mine was secure."],"id":218,"m":"Mine was safe."},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["And so was mine, my lord."],"id":219,"m":"And so was mine, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["And, for myself, most part of all this night,","Within her quarter and mine own precinct","I was employ'd in passing to and fro,","About relieving of the sentinels:","Then how or which way should they first break in?"],"id":220,"m":"And as for me, I spent most of the night going back and forth within her area and my own, relieving the sentries. So how, or which way, could they have broken in first?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Question, my lords, no further of the case,","How or which way: 'tis sure they found some place","But weakly guarded, where the breach was made.","And now there rests no other shift but this;","To gather our soldiers, scatter'd and dispersed,","And lay new platforms to endamage them."],"id":221,"m":"Don't question any further, my lords, how or which way. It's certain they found some weakly guarded place and broke in there. And now there's nothing left to do but gather our scattered soldiers and make new plans to damage them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Enter an English Soldier, crying 'A Talbot! a Talbot!' They fly, leaving their clothes behind.","id":222},{"t":"sp","who":"SOLDIER","lines":["I'll be so bold to take what they have left.","The cry of Talbot serves me for a sword;","For I have loaden me with many spoils,","Using no other weapon but his name."],"id":223,"m":"I'll make so bold as to take what they've left. The cry of \"Talbot\" is as good as a sword to me, for I've loaded myself with loot using no weapon but his name."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":224},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"Orleans. Within the town.","id":225},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Talbot, Bedford, Burgundy, a Captain, and others.","id":226},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["The day begins to break, and night is fled,","Whose pitchy mantle over-veil'd the earth.","Here sound retreat, and cease our hot pursuit."],"id":227,"m":"Day is beginning to break and night has fled, whose pitch-black cloak covered the earth. Sound the retreat here and stop our fierce pursuit."},{"t":"sd","text":"Retreat sounded.","id":228},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Bring forth the body of old Salisbury,","And here advance it in the market-place,","The middle centre of this cursed town.","Now have I paid my vow unto his soul;","For every drop of blood was drawn from him","There hath at least five Frenchmen died to-night.","And that hereafter ages may behold","What ruin happen'd in revenge of him,","Within their chiefest temple I'll erect","A tomb, wherein his corpse shall be interr'd;","Upon the which, that every one may read,","Shall be engraved the sack of Orleans,","The treacherous manner of his mournful death","And what a terror he had been to France.","But, lords, in all our bloody massacre,","I muse we met not with the Dauphin's grace,","His new-come champion, virtuous Joan of Arc,","Nor any of his false confederates."],"id":229,"m":"Bring out the body of old Salisbury and set it up here in the market-place, the very centre of this cursed town. Now I have kept my vow to his soul: for every drop of blood drawn from him, at least five Frenchmen have died tonight. And so that future ages may see what destruction happened to avenge him, I'll build a tomb in their main church where his body will be buried, and on it, for everyone to read, will be carved the sack of Orleans, the treacherous way he died and what a terror he had been to France. But, lords, in all our bloody slaughter I'm surprised we didn't meet his Grace the Dauphin, his new champion, the virtuous Joan of Arc, or any of his treacherous allies."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["'Tis thought, Lord Talbot, when the fight began,","Rous'd on the sudden from their drowsy beds,","They did amongst the troops of armed men","Leap o'er the walls for refuge in the field."],"id":230,"m":"It's thought, Lord Talbot, that when the fighting started, woken suddenly from their sleepy beds, they jumped over the walls among the soldiers to take refuge in the fields."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["Myself, as far as I could well discern","For smoke and dusky vapors of the night,","Am sure I scared the Dauphin and his trull,","When arm in arm they both came swiftly running,","Like to a pair of loving turtle-doves","That could not live asunder day or night.","After that things are set in order here,","We'll follow them with all the power we have."],"id":231,"m":"As far as I could make out through the smoke and the dark mists of the night, I'm sure I frightened off the Dauphin and his whore as they came running arm in arm, like a pair of loving turtle-doves who couldn't live apart day or night. Once things are in order here, we'll follow them with all the forces we have."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":232},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["All hail, my lords! Which of this princely train","Call ye the warlike Talbot, for his acts","So much applauded through the realm of France?"],"id":233,"m":"Greetings, my lords! Which of this princely company is the warlike Talbot, whose deeds are so praised throughout France?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Here is the Talbot: who would speak with him?"],"id":234,"m":"Here is Talbot. Who wants to speak to him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["The virtuous lady, Countess of Auvergne,","With modesty admiring thy renown,","By me entreats, great lord, thou wouldst vouchsafe","To visit her poor castle where she lies,","That she may boast she hath beheld the man","Whose glory fills the world with loud report."],"id":235,"m":"The virtuous lady, the Countess of Auvergne, modestly admiring your fame, begs you through me, great lord, to be so kind as to visit her humble castle where she lives, so that she can boast she has seen the man whose glory fills the world with loud praise."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["Is it even so? Nay, then I see our wars","Will turn into a peaceful comic sport,","When ladies crave to be encount'red with.","You may not, my lord, despise her gentle suit."],"id":236,"m":"Is that so? Well then, I see our wars will turn into a peaceful comedy when ladies ask to be met in combat. You can't turn down her gentle request, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Ne'er trust me then; for when a world of men","Could not prevail with all their oratory,","Yet hath a woman's kindness over-ruled:","And therefore tell her I return great thanks,","And in submission will attend on her.","Will not your honors bear me company?"],"id":237,"m":"Never trust me if I do. When a world of men couldn't win me over with all their eloquence, a woman's kindness has. So tell her I give her many thanks and will humbly visit her. Will your lordships come with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["No, truly; it is more than manners will:","And I have heard it said, unbidden guests","Are often welcomest when they are gone."],"id":238,"m":"No, really; that would be more than good manners allow. And I've heard it said that uninvited guests are often most welcome when they've gone."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Well then, alone, since there 's no remedy,","I mean to prove this lady's courtesy.","Come hither, Captain. [Whispers] You perceive my mind?"],"id":239,"m":"Well then, I'll go alone, since there's no help for it, to test this lady's courtesy. Come here, Captain. (Whispering) You understand what I want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["I do, my lord, and mean accordingly."],"id":240,"m":"I do, my lord, and I'll act on it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":241},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"Auvergne. The Countess's castle.","id":242},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Countess and her Porter.","id":243},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Porter, remember what I gave in charge;","And when you have done so, bring the keys to me."],"id":244,"m":"Porter, remember what I told you to do, and when you've done it, bring the keys to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTER","lines":["Madam, I will."],"id":245,"m":"I will, madam."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":246},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["The plot is laid: if all things fall out right,","I shall as famous be by this exploit","As Scythian Tomyris by Cyrus' death.","Great is the rumor of this dreadful knight,","And his achievements of no less account:","Fain would mine eyes be witness with mine ears,","To give their censure of these rare reports."],"id":247,"m":"The plot is set. If everything goes right, I'll be as famous for this exploit as the Scythian queen Tomyris was for killing Cyrus. Great is the fame of this fearsome knight, and his deeds are just as great. I would like my eyes to witness what my ears have heard and judge these extraordinary reports for themselves."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Messenger and Talbot.","id":248},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Madam,","according as your ladyship desired,","By message craved, so is Lord Talbot come."],"id":249,"m":"Madam, as your ladyship wished and asked by message, Lord Talbot has come."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["And he is welcome. What! is this the man?"],"id":250,"m":"And he is welcome. What! Is this the man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Madam, it is."],"id":251,"m":"Madam, it is."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Is this the scourge of France?","Is this the Talbot, so much fear'd abroad","That with his name the mothers still their babes?","I see report is fabulous and false:","I thought I should have seen some Hercules,","A second Hector, for his grim aspect,","And large proportion of his strong-knit limbs.","Alas, this is a child, a silly dwarf!","It cannot be this weak and writhled shrimp","Should strike such terror to his enemies."],"id":252,"m":"Is this the scourge of France? Is this the Talbot so feared abroad that mothers quiet their babies with his name? I see the reports are made up and false. I thought I would see some Hercules, a second Hector, with a grim face and big, powerful limbs. But this is a child, a feeble dwarf! This weak, wrinkled shrimp can't possibly strike such terror into his enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Madam, I have been bold to trouble you;","But since your ladyship is not at leisure,","I'll sort some other time to visit you."],"id":253,"m":"Madam, I have been bold to trouble you, but since your ladyship isn't free, I'll find some other time to visit you."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["What means he now? Go ask him whither he goes."],"id":254,"m":"What does he mean now? Go and ask him where he's going."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Stay, my Lord Talbot; for my lady craves","To know the cause of your abrupt departure."],"id":255,"m":"Wait, my Lord Talbot, for my lady wants to know why you are leaving so suddenly."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Marry, for that she's in a wrong belief,","I go to certify her Talbot's here."],"id":256,"m":"Well, since she is mistaken, I'm going to prove to her that Talbot is here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Porter with keys.","id":257},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["If thou be he, then art thou prisoner."],"id":258,"m":"If you are he, then you are a prisoner."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Prisoner! to whom?"],"id":259,"m":"Prisoner! Whose?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["To me, blood-thirsty lord;","And for that cause I train'd thee to my house.","Long time thy shadow hath been thrall to me,","For in my gallery thy picture hangs:","But now the substance shall endure the like,","And I will chain these legs and arms of thine,","That hast by tyranny these many years","Wasted our country, slain our citizens,","And sent our sons and husbands captivate."],"id":260,"m":"Mine, bloodthirsty lord, and that's why I lured you to my house. Your image has long been my prisoner, for your portrait hangs in my gallery. But now the real you will suffer the same, and I'll chain these legs and arms of yours, which have for so many years cruelly ruined our country, killed our citizens and taken our sons and husbands captive."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Ha, ha, ha!"],"id":261,"m":"Ha, ha, ha!"},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Laughest thou, wretch? Thy mirth shall turn to moan."],"id":262,"m":"Are you laughing, wretch? Your laughter will turn to groans."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["I laugh to see your ladyship so fond","To think that you have aught but Talbot's shadow","Whereon to practice your severity."],"id":263,"m":"I'm laughing to see your ladyship so foolish as to think you have anything but Talbot's shadow to be cruel to."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Why, art not thou the man?"],"id":264,"m":"Why, aren't you the man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["I am indeed."],"id":265,"m":"I am indeed."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Then have I substance too."],"id":266,"m":"Then I have the real thing too."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["No, no, I am but shadow of myself:","You are deceived, my substance is not here;","For what you see is but the smallest part","And least proportion of humanity:","I tell you, madam, were the whole frame here,","It is of such a spacious lofty pitch,","Your roof were not sufficient to contain 't."],"id":267,"m":"No, no, I am only a shadow of myself. You are mistaken; my real substance is not here. What you see is only the smallest part and tiniest fraction of my being. I tell you, madam, if the whole of me were here, it is so vast and tall that your roof couldn't hold it."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["This is a riddling merchant for the nonce;","He will be here, and yet he is not here:","How can these contrarieties agree?"],"id":268,"m":"This one's a dealer in riddles, all right. He will be here, and yet he isn't here. How can these contradictions fit together?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["That will I show you presently."],"id":269,"m":"I'll show you right now."},{"t":"sd","text":"Winds his horn. Drums strike up: a peal of ordnance. Enter Soldiers.","id":270},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","cont":1,"lines":["How say you, madam? are you now persuaded","That Talbot is but shadow of himself?","These are his substance, sinews, arms and strength,","With which he yoketh your rebellious necks,","Razeth your cities and subverts your towns,","And in a moment makes them desolate."],"id":271,"m":"What do you say, madam? Are you convinced now that Talbot is only a shadow of himself? These are his substance, his muscles, arms and strength, with which he yokes your rebellious necks, flattens your cities, overthrows your towns and in a moment leaves them in ruins."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Victorious Talbot! pardon my abuse:","I find thou art no less than fame hath bruited,","And more than may be gather'd by thy shape.","Let my presumption not provoke thy wrath;","For I am sorry that with reverence","I did not entertain thee as thou art."],"id":272,"m":"Victorious Talbot! Forgive my insults. I find you are no less than fame reported, and more than your appearance suggests. Don't let my arrogance make you angry; I am sorry I did not receive you with the respect you deserve."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Be not dismay'd, fair lady; nor misconstrue","The mind of Talbot, as you did mistake","The outward composition of his body.","What you have done hath not offended me;","Nor other satisfaction do I crave,","But only, with your patience, that we may","Taste of your wine and see what cates you have;","For soldiers' stomachs always serve them well."],"id":273,"m":"Don't be upset, fair lady, and don't misjudge Talbot's mind as you misjudged his outward body. What you did hasn't offended me. I ask no other compensation than that, with your permission, we may taste your wine and see what delicacies you have, for soldiers always have good appetites."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["With all my heart, and think me honored","To feast so great a warrior in my house."],"id":274,"m":"With all my heart, and I consider myself honoured to feast so great a warrior in my house."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":275},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"IV","loc":"London. The Temple-garden.","id":276},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Earls of Somerset, Suffolk, and Warwick; Richard Plantagenet, Vernon, and another Lawyer.","id":277},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["Great lords and gentlemen,","what means this silence?","Dare no man answer in a case of truth?"],"id":278,"m":"Great lords and gentlemen, why this silence? Doesn't anyone dare answer on a matter of truth?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Within the Temple-hall we were too loud;","The garden here is more convenient."],"id":279,"m":"We were too loud inside the Temple hall; the garden here is more suitable."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["Then say at once if I maintain'd the truth;","Or else was wrangling Somerset in the error?"],"id":280,"m":"Then say straight out whether I argued the truth, or whether quarrelsome Somerset was wrong."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Faith, I have been a truant in the law,","And never yet could frame my will to it;","And therefore frame the law unto my will."],"id":281,"m":"Honestly, I've played truant from the law and could never bend my will to it, so I bend the law to fit my will."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Judge you, my Lord of Warwick, then, between us."],"id":282,"m":"Then you judge between us, my Lord of Warwick."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Between two hawks, which flies the higher pitch;","Between two dogs, which hath the deeper mouth;","Between two blades, which bears the better temper:","Between two horses, which doth bear him best;","Between two girls, which hath the merriest eye;","I have perhaps some shallow spirit of judgment:","But in these nice sharp quillets of the law,","Good faith, I am no wiser than a daw."],"id":283,"m":"Between two hawks, which flies higher; between two dogs, which has the deeper bark; between two swords, which is better tempered; between two horses, which carries itself best; between two girls, which has the merriest eye: on these I may have some shallow judgement. But in these fine, sharp quibbles of the law, honestly, I'm no wiser than a jackdaw."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["Tut, tut, here is a mannerly forbearance:","The truth appears so naked on my side","That any purblind eye may find it out."],"id":284,"m":"Come, come, this is just polite dodging. The truth is so plainly on my side that a half-blind eye could see it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["And on my side it is so well apparell'd,","So clear, so shining and so evident,","That it will glimmer through a blind man's eye."],"id":285,"m":"And on my side it is so well dressed, so clear, so shining and so obvious that it would shine through a blind man's eye."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["Since you are tongue-tied and so loath to speak,","In dumb significants proclaim your thoughts:","Let him that is a true-born gentleman","And stands upon the honor of his birth,","If he suppose that I have pleaded truth,","From off this brier pluck a white rose with me."],"id":286,"m":"Since you are tongue-tied and so unwilling to speak, declare your thoughts by silent signs. Let whoever is a true-born gentleman, proud of the honour of his birth, and thinks I have argued the truth, pick a white rose from this bush with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Let him that is no coward nor no flatterer,","But dare maintain the party of the truth,","Pluck a red rose from off this thorn with me."],"id":287,"m":"Let whoever is no coward or flatterer, but dares to support the side of truth, pick a red rose from this thorn bush with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["I love no colours, and without all colour","Of base insinuating flattery","I pluck this white rose with Plantagenet."],"id":288,"m":"I don't like pretences, and without any colour of low, sly flattery I pick this white rose with Plantagenet."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["I pluck this red rose with young Somerset,","And say withal I think he held the right."],"id":289,"m":"I pick this red rose with young Somerset, and I say too that I think he was right."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["Stay, lords and gentlemen, and pluck no more,","Till you conclude that he, upon whose side","The fewest roses are cropp'd from the tree","Shall yield the other in the right opinion."],"id":290,"m":"Wait, lords and gentlemen, and pick no more until you agree that the one whose side has the fewest roses picked from the tree will admit the other is right."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Good Master Vernon, it is well objected:","If I have fewest, I subscribe in silence."],"id":291,"m":"Good Master Vernon, that's a fair point. If I have the fewest, I'll accept it in silence."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["And I."],"id":292,"m":"And so will I."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["Then for the truth and plainness of the case,","I pluck this pale and maiden blossom here,","Giving my verdict on the white rose side."],"id":293,"m":"Then for the truth and plain facts of the case, I pick this pale, virgin flower here, giving my verdict on the white rose side."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Prick not your finger as you pluck it off,","Lest bleeding, you do paint the white rose red,","And fall on my side so, against your will."],"id":294,"m":"Don't prick your finger as you pick it, in case you bleed and paint the white rose red, and so come over to my side against your will."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["If I, my lord, for my opinion bleed,","Opinion shall be surgeon to my hurt","And keep me on the side where still I am."],"id":295,"m":"If I bleed for my opinion, my lord, my opinion will be the surgeon for my wound and keep me on the side where I still am."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Well, well, come on: who else?"],"id":296,"m":"Well, well, come on: who else?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAWYER","lines":["Unless my study and my books be false,","The argument you held was wrong in you;"],"id":297,"m":"Unless my study and my books are wrong, the argument you put forward was mistaken. (To Somerset)"},{"t":"sd","text":"To Somerset.","id":298},{"t":"sp","who":"LAWYER","cont":1,"lines":["In sign whereof I pluck a white rose too."],"id":299,"m":"As a sign of which, I pick a white rose too."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["Now, Somerset, where is your argument?"],"id":300,"m":"Now, Somerset, where is your argument?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Here in my scabbard, meditating that","Shall dye your white rose in a bloody red."],"id":301,"m":"Here in my scabbard, thinking about something that will dye your white rose a bloody red."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["Meantime your cheeks do counterfeit our roses;","For pale they look with fear, as witnessing","The truth on our side."],"id":302,"m":"Meanwhile your cheeks are imitating our roses, for they look pale with fear, as if admitting the truth is on our side."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["No, Plantagenet,","'Tis not for fear but anger that thy cheeks","Blush for pure shame to counterfeit our roses,","And yet thy tongue will not confess thy error."],"id":303,"m":"No, Plantagenet, it's not fear but anger that makes your cheeks blush with pure shame and imitate our roses; and yet your tongue won't admit your mistake."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["Hath not thy rose a canker, Somerset?"],"id":304,"m":"Hasn't your rose got a canker-worm in it, Somerset?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Hath not thy rose a thorn, Plantagenet?"],"id":305,"m":"Hasn't your rose got a thorn, Plantagenet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["Ay, sharp and piercing, to maintain his truth;","Whiles thy consuming canker eats his falsehood."],"id":306,"m":"Yes, sharp and piercing, to defend its truth, while your devouring canker eats away its falseness."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Well, I'll find friends to wear my bleeding roses,","That shall maintain what I have said is true,","Where false Plantagenet dare not be seen."],"id":307,"m":"Well, I'll find friends to wear my bleeding roses who will defend what I have said is true, where false Plantagenet doesn't dare show his face."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["Now, by this maiden blossom in my hand,","I scorn thee and thy fashion, peevish boy."],"id":308,"m":"By this virgin flower in my hand, I scorn you and your kind, you silly boy."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Turn not thy scorns this way, Plantagenet."],"id":309,"m":"Don't aim your scorn this way, Plantagenet."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["Proud Pole, I will, and scorn both him and thee."],"id":310,"m":"I will, proud Pole, and scorn both him and you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["I'll turn my part thereof into thy throat."],"id":311,"m":"I'll ram my share of it back down your throat."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Away, away, good William de la Pole!","We grace the yeoman by conversing with him."],"id":312,"m":"Come away, come away, good William de la Pole! We honour this commoner by talking to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Now, by God's will, thou wrong'st him, Somerset;","His grandfather was Lionel Duke of Clarence,","Third son to the third Edward King of England:","Spring crestless yeomen from so deep a root?"],"id":313,"m":"By God's will, you wrong him, Somerset. His grandfather was Lionel Duke of Clarence, third son of Edward the Third, King of England. Do commoners without coats of arms grow from such a deep root?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["He bears him on the place's privilege,","Or durst not, for his craven heart, say thus."],"id":314,"m":"He's relying on the protection of this place, or his cowardly heart wouldn't dare say this."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["By Him that made me, I'll maintain my words","On any plot of ground in Christendom.","Was not thy father, Richard Earl of Cambridge,","For treason executed in our late king's days?","And, by his treason, stand'st not thou attainted,","Corrupted, and exempt from ancient gentry?","His trespass yet lives guilty in thy blood;","And, till thou be restored, thou art a yeoman."],"id":315,"m":"By the God who made me, I'll stand by my words on any patch of ground in Christendom. Wasn't your father, Richard Earl of Cambridge, executed for treason in our late king's time? And because of his treason, aren't you attainted, your blood corrupted and stripped of your ancient noble rank? His crime still lives on as guilt in your blood, and until you are restored, you are a commoner."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["My father was attached, not attainted,","Condemn'd to die for treason, but no traitor;","And that I'll prove on better men than Somerset,","Were growing time once ripen'd to my will.","For your partaker Pole and you yourself,","I'll note you in my book of memory,","To scourge you for this apprehension:","Look to it well and say you are well warn'd."],"id":316,"m":"My father was arrested, not attainted; condemned to die for treason, but no traitor. And I'll prove that on better men than Somerset when time ripens to suit my purpose. As for your ally Pole and you yourself, I'll write you down in my book of memory to punish you for this idea. Watch out, and remember you've been warned."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Ay, thou shalt find us ready for thee still;","And know us by these colors for thy foes,","For these my friends in spite of thee shall wear."],"id":317,"m":"Yes, you'll find us always ready for you, and you'll know us as your enemies by these colours, which my friends will wear in spite of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["And, by my soul, this pale and angry rose,","As cognizance of my blood-drinking hate,","Will I for ever and my faction wear,","Until it wither with me to my grave,","Or flourish to the height of my degree."],"id":318,"m":"And by my soul, I and my party will wear this pale and angry rose forever as the badge of my bloodthirsty hatred, until it withers with me into my grave or flourishes to the height of my rank."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Go forward, and be chok'd with thy ambition!","And so farewell until I meet thee next."],"id":319,"m":"Go on, and choke on your ambition! And so goodbye until I meet you next."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":320},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Have with thee, Pole. Farewell, ambitious Richard."],"id":321,"m":"I'm with you, Pole. Goodbye, ambitious Richard."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":322},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["How I am braved and must perforce endure it!"],"id":323,"m":"How I am defied, and I must put up with it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["This blot that they object against your house","Shall be wiped out in the next parliament","Call'd for the truce of Winchester and Gloucester;","And if thou be not then created York,","I will not live to be accounted Warwick.","Meantime, in signal of my love to thee,","Against proud Somerset and William Pole,","Will I upon thy party wear this rose:","And here I prophesy: this brawl to-day,","Grown to this faction in the Temple-garden,","Shall send between the red rose and the white","A thousand souls to death and deadly night."],"id":324,"m":"This stain they accuse your family of will be wiped out at the next parliament, called to make a truce between Winchester and Gloucester; and if you are not made Duke of York then, I won't live to be called Warwick. Meanwhile, as a sign of my love for you against proud Somerset and William Pole, I'll wear this rose on your side. And here I make a prophecy: this quarrel today, grown into this faction in the Temple garden, will send a thousand souls to death and endless night between the red rose and the white."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["Good Master Vernon, I am bound to you,","That you on my behalf would pluck a flower."],"id":325,"m":"Good Master Vernon, I'm grateful to you for picking a flower on my side."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["In your behalf still will I wear the same."],"id":326,"m":"I'll always wear it on your side."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAWYER","lines":["And so will I."],"id":327,"m":"And so will I."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["Thanks, gentle sir.","Come, let us four to dinner: I dare say","This quarrel will drink blood another day."],"id":328,"m":"Thank you, kind sir. Come, let's all four go to dinner. I dare say this quarrel will drink blood another day."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":329},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"V","loc":"The Tower of London.","id":330},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Mortimer, brought in a chair, and Jailers.","id":331},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["Kind keepers of my weak decaying age,","Let dying Mortimer here rest himself.","Even like a man new haled from the rack,","So fare my limbs with long imprisonment;","And these gray locks, the pursuivants of death,","Nestor-like aged in an age of care,","Argue the end of Edmund Mortimer.","These eyes, like lamps whose wasting oil is spent,","Wax dim, as drawing to their exigent;","Weak shoulders, overborne with burdening grief,","And pithless arms, like to a wither'd vine","That droops his sapless branches to the ground:","Yet are these feet, whose strengthless stay is numb,","Unable to support this lump of clay,","Swift-winged with desire to get a grave,","As witting I no other comfort have.","But tell me, keeper, will my nephew come?"],"id":332,"m":"Kind keepers of my weak, failing old age, let dying Mortimer rest here. My limbs feel like a man's just pulled off the rack, after my long imprisonment; and these grey hairs, the messengers of death, aged like Nestor's in an age of trouble, show that Edmund Mortimer's end is near. These eyes, like lamps whose oil is used up, grow dim as they come to their end. My weak shoulders are bowed with heavy grief, and my feeble arms are like a withered vine drooping its dried-up branches to the ground. Yet these feet, numb and too weak to hold up this lump of clay, are swift with desire for a grave, knowing I have no other comfort. But tell me, keeper, will my nephew come?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST JAILER","lines":["Richard Plantagenet, my lord, will come:","We sent unto the Temple, unto his chamber;","And answer was return'd that he will come."],"id":333,"m":"Richard Plantagenet will come, my lord. We sent to his rooms at the Temple, and the answer came back that he will come."},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["Enough: my soul shall then be satisfied.","Poor gentleman! his wrong doth equal mine.","Since Henry Monmouth first began to reign,","Before whose glory I was great in arms,","This loathsome sequestration have I had;","And even since then hath Richard been obscured,","Deprived of honour and inheritance.","But now the arbitrator of despairs,","Just Death, kind umpire of men's miseries,","With sweet enlargement doth dismiss me hence:","I would his troubles likewise were expired,","That so he might recover what was lost."],"id":334,"m":"Enough: then my soul will be satisfied. Poor gentleman! His wrongs equal mine. Ever since Henry of Monmouth began to reign (before his glory I was a great soldier), I have been kept in this hateful confinement, and ever since then Richard has been kept down, robbed of honour and inheritance. But now Death, who settles all despair, the just and kind judge of men's miseries, releases me from here with sweet freedom. I wish his troubles were over too, so that he could recover what was lost."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Richard Plantagenet.","id":335},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST JAILER","lines":["My lord, your loving nephew now is come."],"id":336,"m":"My lord, your loving nephew has come."},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["Richard Plantagenet, my friend, is he come?"],"id":337,"m":"Richard Plantagenet, my friend, has he come?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["Aye, noble uncle, thus ignobly used,","Your nephew, late despised Richard, comes."],"id":338,"m":"Yes, noble uncle: your nephew, so shamefully treated, the lately despised Richard, has come."},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["Direct mine arms I may embrace his neck,","And in his bosom spend my latter gasp:","O, tell me when my lips do touch his cheeks,","That I may kindly give one fainting kiss.","And now declare, sweet stem from York's great stock,","Why didst thou say of late thou wert despised?"],"id":339,"m":"Guide my arms so that I can embrace his neck and breathe my last on his chest. Oh, tell me when my lips touch his cheeks, so I can lovingly give one faint kiss. And now tell me, sweet branch of York's great family tree, why did you say just now that you were despised?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["First, lean thine aged back against mine arm;","And, in that case, I'll tell thee my disease.","This day, in argument upon a case,","Some words there grew 'twixt Somerset and me;","Among which terms he used his lavish tongue","And did upbraid me with my father's death:","Which obloquy set bars before my tongue,","Else with the like I had requited him.","Therefore, good uncle, for my father's sake,","In honor of a true Plantagenet","And for alliance sake, declare the cause","My father, Earl of Cambridge, lost his head."],"id":340,"m":"First, lean your old back against my arm, and like that I'll tell you what is troubling me. Today, while arguing a case, some words arose between Somerset and me, and among them he let his tongue run wild and taunted me with my father's death. That insult silenced me, or I would have paid him back in kind. So, good uncle, for my father's sake, in honour of a true Plantagenet and for the sake of our family ties, tell me why my father, the Earl of Cambridge, lost his head."},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["That cause, fair nephew, that imprison'd me","And hath detain'd me all my flowering youth","Within a loathsome dungeon, there to pine,","Was cursed instrument of his decease."],"id":341,"m":"Fair nephew, the same cause that imprisoned me and kept me all through my youth in a hateful dungeon to waste away was the cursed instrument of his death."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["Discover more at large what cause that was,","For I am ignorant and cannot guess."],"id":342,"m":"Explain more fully what that cause was, for I don't know and can't guess."},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["I will, if that my fading breath permit,","And death approach not ere my tale be done.","Henry the Fourth, grandfather to this king,","Deposed his nephew Richard, Edward's son,","The first-begotten and the lawful heir","Of Edward king, the third of that descent;","During whose reign the Percies of the north,","Finding his usurpation most unjust,","Endeavour'd my advancement to the throne.","The reason moved these warlike lords to this","Was, for that—young King Richard thus removed,","Leaving no heir begotten of his body—","I was the next by birth and parentage;","For by my mother I derived am","From Lionel Duke of Clarence, third son","To King Edward the Third; whereas he","From John of Gaunt doth bring his pedigree,","Being but fourth of that heroic line.","But mark: as in this haughty great attempt","They labored to plant the rightful heir,","I lost my liberty and they their lives.","Long after this, when Henry the Fifth,","Succeeding his father Bolingbroke, did reign,","Thy father, Earl of Cambridge, then derived","From famous Edmund Langley, Duke of York,","Marrying my sister that thy mother was,","Again in pity of my hard distress.","Levied an army, weening to redeem","And have install'd me in the diadem:","But, as the rest, so fell that noble earl","And was beheaded. Thus the Mortimers,","In whom the title rested, were suppress'd."],"id":343,"m":"I will, if my fading breath allows and death does not come before my story is told. Henry the Fourth, grandfather of this king, deposed his cousin Richard, son of Edward the Black Prince, the eldest son and lawful heir of King Edward, the third of that name. During his reign the Percys of the north, finding his seizure of the crown most unjust, tried to put me on the throne. What moved these warlike lords to this was that, with young King Richard removed and leaving no children of his own, I was next in line by birth and descent. Through my mother I am descended from Lionel Duke of Clarence, the third son of King Edward the Third, whereas Henry traces his descent from John of Gaunt, only the fourth son of that heroic line. But note this: in that bold, great attempt to put the rightful heir on the throne, I lost my freedom and they lost their lives. Long after this, when Henry the Fifth reigned after his father Bolingbroke, your father, the Earl of Cambridge, descended from the famous Edmund Langley, Duke of York, who had married my sister, your mother, again took pity on my hard suffering and raised an army, hoping to free me and put me on the throne. But like the rest, that noble earl fell and was beheaded. So the Mortimers, in whom the right to the crown rested, were crushed."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["Of which, my lord, your honor is the last."],"id":344,"m":"And your lordship is the last of them."},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["True; and thou seest that I no issue have,","And that my fainting words do warrant death:","Thou art my heir; the rest I wish thee gather:","But yet be wary in thy studious care."],"id":345,"m":"True; and you see I have no children, and my failing words are a sign of death. You are my heir; I leave the rest for you to work out. But be careful in your eager efforts."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["Thy grave admonishments prevail with me:","But yet, methinks, my father's execution","Was nothing less than bloody tyranny."],"id":346,"m":"Your serious warnings carry weight with me. But still, I think my father's execution was nothing less than bloody tyranny."},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["With silence, nephew, be thou politic:","Strong-fixed is the house of Lancaster,","And like a mountain not to be removed.","But now thy uncle is removing hence;","As princes do their courts, when they are cloy'd","With long continuance in a settled place."],"id":347,"m":"Nephew, be shrewd and keep silent. The house of Lancaster is firmly fixed, like a mountain that can't be moved. But now your uncle is moving on, as princes move their courts when they are tired of staying too long in one place."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["O, uncle, would some part of my young years","Might but redeem the passage of your age!"],"id":348,"m":"Oh, uncle, if only some of my young years could buy back your lost years!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MORTIMER","lines":["Thou dost then wrong me, as that slaughterer doth","Which giveth many wounds when one will kill.","Mourn not, except thou sorrow for my good;","Only give order for my funeral:","And so farewell, and fair be all thy hopes,","And prosperous be thy life in peace and war!"],"id":349,"m":"Then you would wrong me, like a butcher who gives many wounds when one would kill. Don't mourn, unless you grieve for my good fortune. Just give orders for my funeral. And so goodbye; may all your hopes be fulfilled, and may your life prosper in peace and war!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Dies.","id":350},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["And peace, no war, befall thy parting soul!","In prison hast thou spent a pilgrimage,","And like a hermit overpass'd thy days.","Well, I will lock his counsel in my breast;","And what I do imagine let that rest.","Keepers, convey him hence; and I myself","Will see his burial better than his life."],"id":351,"m":"And may peace, not war, come to your departing soul! You have spent your life's journey in prison and passed your days like a hermit. Well, I'll lock his advice in my heart, and let what I'm thinking stay there. Keepers, carry him away; I myself will give him a better burial than he had a life."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Jailers, bearing out the body of Mortimer.","id":352},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","cont":1,"lines":["Here dies the dusky torch of Mortimer,","Choked with ambition of the meaner sort:","And for those wrongs, those bitter injuries,","Which Somerset hath offer'd to my house,","I doubt not but with honour to redress;","And therefore haste I to the parliament,","Either to be restored to my blood,","Or make my ill the advantage of my good."],"id":353,"m":"Here the dim torch of Mortimer goes out, smothered by the ambition of lesser men. And for those wrongs, those bitter injuries Somerset has done to my family, I have no doubt I'll put them right with honour. So I'm hurrying to parliament, either to be restored to my family rights or to turn my misfortune to my advantage."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":354},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"London. The Parliament-house.","id":355},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter King, Exeter, Gloucester, Warwick, Somerset, and Suffolk; the Bishop of Winchester, Richard Plantagenet, and others. Gloucester offers to put up a bill; Winchester snatches it, tears it.","id":356},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["Comest thou with deep premeditated lines,","With written pamphlets studiously devised,","Humphrey of Gloucester? If thou canst accuse,","Or aught intend'st to lay unto my charge.","Do it without invention, suddenly;","As I with sudden and extemporal speech","Purpose to answer what thou canst object."],"id":357,"m":"Have you come with carefully planned lines, with written papers studiously put together, Humphrey of Gloucester? If you can accuse me, or mean to charge me with anything, do it on the spot, without making things up, just as I intend to answer whatever you object with an on-the-spot, unprepared speech."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Presumptuous priest! this place commands my patience,","Or thou shouldst find thou hast dishonor'd me.","Think not, although in writing I preferr'd","The manner of thy vile outrageous crimes,","That therefore I have forged, or am not able","Verbatim to rehearse the method of my pen:","No, prelate; such is thy audacious wickedness,","Thy lewd, pestiferous and dissentious pranks,","As very infants prattle of thy pride.","Thou art a most pernicious usurer,","Froward by nature, enemy to peace;","Lascivious, wanton, more than well beseems","A man of thy profession and degree;","And for thy treachery, what's more manifest","In that thou laid'st a trap to take my life,","As well at London-bridge as at the Tower.","Beside, I fear me, if thy thoughts are sifted","The king, thy sovereign, is not quite exempt","From envious malice of thy swelling heart."],"id":358,"m":"Arrogant priest! This place demands my patience, or you'd find out what it means to dishonour me. Don't think that because I set out your vile, outrageous crimes in writing, I invented them or can't repeat word for word what I wrote. No, churchman: your shameless wickedness, your vile, poisonous, quarrelsome tricks are such that even babies chatter about your pride. You are a most harmful moneylender, stubborn by nature, an enemy of peace, lustful and loose-living more than is fitting for a man of your calling and rank. As for your treachery, what's more obvious than the fact that you set a trap to kill me, both at London Bridge and at the Tower? Besides, I fear that if your thoughts were examined closely, the King, your sovereign, would not be entirely safe from the envious hatred of your swollen heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["Gloucester, I do defy thee. Lords, vouchsafe","To give me hearing what I shall reply.","If I were covetous, ambitious, or perverse,","As he will have me, how am I so poor?","Or how haps it I seek not to advance","Or raise myself, but keep my wonted calling?","And for dissension, who preferreth peace","More than I do?—except I be provoked.","No, my good lords, it is not that offends;","It is not that that hath incensed the duke:","It is, because no one should sway but he;","No one but he should be about the king;","And that engenders thunder in his breast,","And makes him roar these accusations forth.","But he shall know I am as good—"],"id":359,"m":"Gloucester, I defy you. Lords, be good enough to hear my reply. If I were greedy, ambitious or wicked, as he claims, how is it that I'm so poor? And how is it that I don't try to promote or raise myself, but stay in my usual calling? As for quarrelling, who prefers peace more than I do, unless I'm provoked? No, my good lords, that isn't what offends him; that isn't what has angered the Duke. It's that nobody should rule but him, nobody but him should be close to the King. That's what breeds thunder in his chest and makes him roar out these accusations. But he'll learn I am as good—"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["As good!","Thou bastard of my grandfather!"],"id":360,"m":"As good! You bastard son of my grandfather!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["Aye, lordly sir; for what are you, I pray,","But one imperious in another's throne?"],"id":361,"m":"Yes, my lordly sir; for what are you, I'd like to know, but a man ruling arrogantly from another's throne?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Am I not protector, saucy priest?"],"id":362,"m":"Am I not Protector, you cheeky priest?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["And am not I a prelate of the church?"],"id":363,"m":"And am I not a bishop of the Church?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Yes, as an outlaw in a castle keeps","And useth it to patronage his theft."],"id":364,"m":"Yes, the way an outlaw holds a castle and uses it to protect his stolen goods."},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["Unreverent Gloster!"],"id":365,"m":"Disrespectful Gloucester!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Thou art reverent","Touching thy spiritual function, not thy life."],"id":366,"m":"You are respected for your religious office, not for your life."},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["Rome shall remedy this."],"id":367,"m":"Rome will put this right."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Roam thither, then."],"id":368,"m":"Then roam off there."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["My lord, it were your duty to forbear."],"id":369,"m":"My lord, it would be your duty to hold back."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Ay, see the bishop be not overborne."],"id":370,"m":"Yes, make sure the bishop isn't overpowered."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Methinks my lord should be religious,","And know the office that belongs to such."],"id":371,"m":"I think my lord should be religious and know the duties that go with such an office."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Methinks his lordship should be humbler;","It fitteth not a prelate so to plead."],"id":372,"m":"I think his lordship should be humbler; it doesn't suit a bishop to argue like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Yes, when his holy state is touch'd so near."],"id":373,"m":"It does, when his holy office is attacked so directly."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["State holy or unhallow'd, what of that?","Is not his grace protector to the king?"],"id":374,"m":"Holy office or unholy, what does that matter? Isn't his Grace the King's Protector?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["[Aside] Plantagenet, I see, must hold his tongue,","Lest it be said, 'Speak, sirrah, when you should:","Must your bold verdict enter talk with lords?'","Else would I have a fling at Winchester."],"id":375,"m":"(Aside) I see Plantagenet must hold his tongue, or else they'll say, \"Speak when you're spoken to, fellow. Must your bold opinion butt into lords' talk?\" Otherwise I'd have a go at Winchester."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Uncles of Gloucester and of Winchester,","The special watchmen of our English weal,","I would prevail, if prayers might prevail,","To join your hearts in love and amity.","O, what a scandal is it to our crown,","That two such noble peers as ye should jar!","Believe me, lords, my tender years can tell","Civil dissension is a viperous worm","That gnaws the bowels of the commonwealth."],"id":376,"m":"Uncles of Gloucester and Winchester, special guardians of England's welfare, I would persuade you, if prayers could do it, to join your hearts in love and friendship. Oh, what a scandal it is to our crown that two such noble lords should quarrel! Believe me, lords, even at my young age I can tell that civil strife is a poisonous worm that gnaws at the guts of the state."},{"t":"sd","text":"A noise within, 'Down with the tawny-coats!'.","id":377},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":1,"lines":["What tumult's this?"],"id":378,"m":"What's this uproar?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["An uproar, I dare warrant,","Begun through malice of the bishop's men."],"id":379,"m":"A riot, I'll bet, started out of spite by the bishop's men."},{"t":"sd","text":"A noise again, 'Stones! stones!' Enter Mayor.","id":380},{"t":"sp","who":"MAYOR","lines":["O, my good lords, and virtuous Henry,","Pity the city of London, pity us!","The bishop and the Duke of Gloucester's men,","Forbidden late to carry any weapon,","Have fill'd their pockets full of pebble stones,","And banding themselves in contrary parts","Do pelt so fast at one another's pate","That many have their giddy brains knock'd out:","Our windows are broke down in every street,","And we for fear compell'd to shut our shops."],"id":381,"m":"Oh, my good lords, and virtuous Henry, have pity on the city of London, pity us! The bishop's and the Duke of Gloucester's men, recently forbidden to carry any weapons, have filled their pockets with pebbles and, ganging up on opposite sides, pelt each other's heads so hard that many have had their dizzy brains knocked out. Windows are smashed in every street, and we're forced by fear to shut our shops."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Serving-men, in skirmish, with bloody pates.","id":382},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["We charge you, on allegiance to ourself,","To hold your slaughtering hands and keep the peace.","Pray, uncle Gloucester, mitigate this strife."],"id":383,"m":"We order you, by your loyalty to us, to stop your murderous fighting and keep the peace. Please, uncle Gloucester, calm this quarrel."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVING-MAN","lines":["Nay, if we be forbidden stones,","we'll fall to it with our teeth."],"id":384,"m":"No, if we're forbidden stones, we'll go at it with our teeth."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVING-MAN","lines":["Do what ye dare, we are as resolute."],"id":385,"m":"Do what you dare; we're just as determined."},{"t":"sd","text":"Skirmish again.","id":386},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["You of my household, leave this peevish broil","And set this unaccustom'd fight aside."],"id":387,"m":"You of my household, stop this silly brawl and put aside this disorderly fighting."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVING-MAN","lines":["My lord, we know your grace to be a man","Just and upright; and, for your royal birth,","Inferior to none but to his Majesty:","And ere that we will suffer such a prince,","So kind a father of the commonweal,","To be disgraced by an inkhorn mate,","We and our wives and children all will fight,","And have our bodies slaughter'd by thy foes."],"id":388,"m":"My lord, we know your Grace is a just and upright man, and by royal birth second to none but his Majesty. Rather than let such a prince, so kind a father to the country, be disgraced by a pen-pushing scribbler, we and our wives and children will all fight and have our bodies slaughtered by your enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVING-MAN","lines":["Aye, and the very parings of our nails","Shall pitch a field when we are dead."],"id":389,"m":"Yes, and even the clippings of our nails will set up a battle when we're dead."},{"t":"sd","text":"Begin again.","id":390},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Stay, stay, I say!","And if you love me, as you say you do,","Let me persuade you to forbear awhile."],"id":391,"m":"Stop, stop, I say! And if you love me, as you say you do, let me persuade you to hold back a while."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["O, how this discord doth afflict my soul!","Can you, my Lord of Winchester, behold","My sighs and tears and will not once relent?","Who should be pitiful, if you be not?","Or who should study to prefer a peace,","If holy churchmen take delight in broils?"],"id":392,"m":"Oh, how this quarrelling hurts my soul! My Lord of Winchester, can you watch my sighs and tears and not give way at all? Who should show pity, if not you? Or who should work for peace, if holy churchmen enjoy brawls?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Yield, my lord protector; yield, Winchester;","Except you mean with obstinate repulse","To slay your sovereign and destroy the realm.","You see what mischief and what murder too","Hath been enacted through your enmity;","Then be at peace, except ye thirst for blood."],"id":393,"m":"Give way, Lord Protector; give way, Winchester; unless you mean to kill your king and destroy the kingdom by stubborn refusal. You see what harm, and murder too, your hostility has caused. So make peace, unless you are thirsty for blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["He shall submit, or I will never yield."],"id":394,"m":"He must submit first, or I'll never give way."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Compassion on the king commands me stoop;","Or I would see his heart out, ere the priest","Should ever get that privilege of me."],"id":395,"m":"Pity for the King makes me give in; otherwise I'd see his heart cut out before the priest ever got that advantage over me."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Behold, my Lord of Winchester, the duke","Hath banish'd moody discontented fury,","As by his smoothed brows it doth appear:","Why look you still so stem and tragical?"],"id":396,"m":"Look, my Lord of Winchester, the Duke has driven out his sullen, discontented anger, as his smoothed brow shows. Why do you still look so stern and grim?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Here, Winchester, I offer thee my hand."],"id":397,"m":"Here, Winchester, I offer you my hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Fie, uncle Beaufort! I have heard you preach","That malice was a great and grievous sin;","And will not you maintain the thing you teach,","But prove a chief offender in the same?"],"id":398,"m":"Shame, uncle Beaufort! I've heard you preach that malice was a great and terrible sin. Won't you practise what you teach, instead of proving a chief offender in it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Sweet king! the bishop hath a kindly gird.","For shame, my lord of Winchester, relent!","What, shall a child instruct you what to do?"],"id":399,"m":"Sweet King! He has given the bishop a gentle dig. For shame, my Lord of Winchester, give way! What, must a child teach you what to do?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["Well, Duke of Gloucester, I will yield to thee;","Love for thy love and hand for hand I give."],"id":400,"m":"Well, Duke of Gloucester, I'll give way to you. I give love for your love and my hand for yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["[Aside] Aye, but, I fear me, with a hollow heart.—","See here, my friends and loving countrymen;","This token serveth for a flag of truce","Betwixt ourselves and all our followers:","So help me God, as I dissemble not!"],"id":401,"m":"(Aside) Yes, but I'm afraid with a hollow heart. (Aloud) Look here, my friends and loving countrymen: this handshake serves as a flag of truce between ourselves and all our followers. So help me God, I'm not pretending!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["[Aside] So help me God, as I intend it not!"],"id":402,"m":"(Aside) So help me God, I don't mean it at all!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["O loving uncle, kind Duke of Gloucester,","How joyful am I made by this contract!","Away, my masters! trouble us no more;","But join in friendship, as your lords have done."],"id":403,"m":"Oh loving uncle, kind Duke of Gloucester, how happy this agreement makes me! Away, men! Trouble us no more, but be friends, as your lords have become."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVING-MAN","lines":["Content: I'll to the surgeon's."],"id":404,"m":"Agreed. I'll go to the surgeon's."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVING-MAN","lines":["And so will I."],"id":405,"m":"And so will I."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVING-MAN","lines":["And I will see what physic the tavern affords."],"id":406,"m":"And I'll see what medicine the tavern has to offer."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Serving-men, Mayor, &C.","id":407},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Accept this scroll, most gracious sovereign;","Which in the right of Richard Plantagenet.","We do exhibit to your majesty."],"id":408,"m":"Accept this document, most gracious King, which we present to your Majesty on behalf of Richard Plantagenet's rights."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Well urged, my Lord of Warwick: for, sweet prince,","An if your Grace mark every circumstance,","You have great reason to do Richard right:","Especially for those occasions","At Eltham place I told your majesty."],"id":409,"m":"Well argued, my Lord of Warwick. For, sweet Prince, if your Grace considers every circumstance, you have good reason to do Richard justice, especially for those reasons I told your Majesty at Eltham Palace."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["And those occasions, uncle, were of force;","Therefore, my loving lords, our pleasure is","That Richard be restored to his blood."],"id":410,"m":"And those reasons were strong, uncle. So, my loving lords, it is our wish that Richard be restored to his family rights."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Let Richard be restored to his blood;","So shall his father's wrongs be recompensed."],"id":411,"m":"Let Richard be restored to his family rights; so his father's wrongs will be made up for."},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["As will the rest, so willeth Winchester."],"id":412,"m":"As the rest wish, so does Winchester."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["If Richard will be true, not that alone","But all the whole inheritance I give","That doth belong unto the house of York,","From whence you spring by lineal descent."],"id":413,"m":"If Richard will be loyal, I give him not only that but the whole inheritance that belongs to the house of York, from which you are directly descended."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["Thy humble servant vows obedience","And humble service till the point of death."],"id":414,"m":"Your humble servant swears obedience and humble service to the point of death."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Stoop then and set your knee against my foot;","And, in reguerdon of that duty done,","I girt thee with the valiant sword of York:","Rise, Richard, like a true Plantagenet,","And rise created princely Duke of York."],"id":415,"m":"Kneel then and set your knee against my foot; and as a reward for that act of loyalty, I fasten on you the brave sword of York. Rise, Richard, like a true Plantagenet, and rise made the princely Duke of York."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLANTAGENET","lines":["And so thrive Richard as thy foes may fall!","And as my duty springs, so perish they","That grudge one thought against your majesty!"],"id":416,"m":"May Richard thrive as surely as your enemies fall! And as my loyalty grows, so may anyone perish who holds a single grudging thought against your Majesty!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Welcome, high prince, the mighty Duke of York!"],"id":417,"m":"Welcome, high prince, the mighty Duke of York!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["[Aside] Perish, base prince, ignoble Duke of York!"],"id":418,"m":"(Aside) Perish, low prince, dishonourable Duke of York!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Now will it best avail your majesty","To cross the seas and to be crown'd in France:","The presence of a king engenders love","Amongst his subjects and his loyal friends,","As it disanimates his enemies."],"id":419,"m":"Now it would serve your Majesty best to cross the sea and be crowned in France. A king's presence breeds love among his subjects and loyal friends, just as it disheartens his enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["When Gloucester says the word, King Henry goes;","For friendly counsel cuts off many foes."],"id":420,"m":"When Gloucester says the word, King Henry goes; for friendly advice cuts off many enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Your ships already are in readiness."],"id":421,"m":"Your ships are already prepared."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sennet. Flourish. Exeunt all but Exeter.","id":422},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Aye, we may march in England or in France,","Not seeing what is likely to ensue.","This late dissension grown betwixt the peers","Burns under feigned ashes of forged love,","And will at last break out into a flame;","As fest'red members rot but by degree,","Till bones and flesh and sinews fall away,","So will this base and envious discord breed.","And now I fear that fatal prophecy","Which in the time of Henry named the fifth","Was in the mouth of every sucking babe;","That Henry born at Monmouth should win all","And Henry born at Windsor lose all:","Which is so plain, that Exeter doth wish","His days may finish ere that hapless time."],"id":423,"m":"Yes, we may march in England or in France without seeing what is likely to follow. This recent quarrel between the lords is burning under the false ashes of pretended love and will finally burst into flame. Just as infected limbs rot slowly until bones, flesh and sinews fall away, so this low, envious quarrel will spread. And now I fear that deadly prophecy which in the time of Henry the Fifth was on the lips of every baby: that Henry born at Monmouth would win everything, and Henry born at Windsor would lose everything. It's so obviously coming true that Exeter wishes his life may end before that unlucky time."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":424},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"France. Before Rouen.","id":425},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter La Pucelle disguised, with four Soldiers with sacks upon their backs.","id":426},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["These are the city gates, the gates of Rouen,","Through which our policy must make a breach:","Take heed, be wary how you place your words;","Talk like the vulgar sort of market men","That come to gather money for their corn.","If we have entrance, as I hope we shall,","And that we find the slothful watch but weak,","I'll by a sign give notice to our friends,","That Charles the Dauphin may encounter them."],"id":427,"m":"These are the city gates, the gates of Rouen, through which our cunning must break in. Be careful how you choose your words. Talk like ordinary market traders who come to make money from their grain. If we get in, as I hope we will, and find the lazy guard weak, I'll give a signal to our friends so that Charles the Dauphin can attack them."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Our sacks shall be a mean to sack the city,","And we be lords and rulers over Rouen;","Therefore we'll knock. [Knocks]"],"id":428,"m":"Our sacks will be the means to sack the city, and we'll be lords and rulers of Rouen. So we'll knock. (Knocks)"},{"t":"sp","who":"WATCH","lines":["[Within] Qui est la?"],"id":429,"m":"(From inside) Who's there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Paysans, pauvres gens de France;","Poor market folks that come to sell their corn."],"id":430,"m":"Peasants, poor people of France; poor market folk who've come to sell their grain."},{"t":"sp","who":"WATCH","lines":["Enter, go in; the market bell is rung."],"id":431,"m":"Come in, go in; the market bell has rung."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Now, Rouen, I'll shake thy bulwarks to the ground."],"id":432,"m":"Now, Rouen, I'll shake your walls to the ground."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":433},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Charles, the Bastard of Orleans, Alencon, Reignier, and forces.","id":434},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Saint Denis bless this happy stratagem!","And once again we'll sleep secure in Rouen."],"id":435,"m":"Saint Denis bless this lucky trick! And once again we'll sleep safely in Rouen."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASTARD","lines":["Here enter'd Pucelle and her practisants;","Now she is there, how will she specify","Here is the best and safest passage in?"],"id":436,"m":"Pucelle and her fellow plotters went in here. Now that she's inside, how will she show us which is the best and safest way in?"},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["By thrusting out a torch from yonder tower;","Which, once discern'd, shows that her meaning is,","No way to that, for weakness, which she enter'd."],"id":437,"m":"By pushing out a torch from that tower over there; once we see it, it will mean that the way she went in is the weakest."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter La Pucelle, on the top, thrusting out a torch burning.","id":438},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Behold, this is the happy wedding torch","That joineth Rouen unto her countrymen,","But burning fatal to the Talbotites!"],"id":439,"m":"Look, this is the happy wedding torch that joins Rouen to her countrymen, but burns with death for Talbot's men!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":440},{"t":"sp","who":"BASTARD","lines":["See, noble Charles, the beacon of our friend;","The burning torch in yonder turret stands."],"id":441,"m":"Look, noble Charles, our friend's beacon: the burning torch is there in that turret."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Now shine it like a comet of revenge,","A prophet to the fall of all our foes!"],"id":442,"m":"Now let it shine like a comet of revenge, foretelling the fall of all our enemies!"},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["Defer no time, delays have dangerous ends;","Enter, and cry, 'The Dauphin!' presently,","And then do execution on the watch."],"id":443,"m":"Don't waste time; delays end in danger. Go in at once and shout \"The Dauphin!\", and then kill the guards."},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Exeunt.","id":444},{"t":"sd","text":"An alarum. Enter Talbot in an excursion.","id":445},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["France, thou shalt rue this treason with thy tears,","If Talbot but survive thy treachery.","Pucelle, that witch, that damned sorceress,","Hath wrought this hellish mischief unawares,","That hardly we escaped the pride of France."],"id":446,"m":"France, you'll pay for this treason with tears, if Talbot only survives your treachery. Pucelle, that witch, that damned sorceress, has done this hellish mischief by surprise, so that we barely escaped the might of France."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":447},{"t":"sd","text":"An alarum: excursions.","id":448},{"t":"sd","text":"Bedford, brought in sick in a chair. Enter Talbot and Burgundy without: within La Pucelle, Charles, Bastard, Alencon, and Reignier, on the walls.","id":449},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Good morrow, gallants! want ye corn for bread?","I think the Duke of Burgundy will fast","Before he'll buy again at such a rate:","'Twas full of darnel: do you like the taste?"],"id":450,"m":"Good morning, fine gentlemen! Do you need grain for bread? I think the Duke of Burgundy will go hungry before he buys again at such a price. It was full of weeds; do you like the taste?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["Scoff on, vile fiend and shameless courtezan!","I trust ere long to choke thee with thine own,","And make thee curse the harvest of that corn."],"id":451,"m":"Go on mocking, vile devil and shameless whore! I hope before long to choke you with your own grain and make you curse the harvest of that corn."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Your Grace may starve perhaps before that time."],"id":452,"m":"Your Grace may starve before then."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["O, let no words, but deeds, revenge this treason!"],"id":453,"m":"Oh, let deeds, not words, avenge this treason!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["What will you do, good graybeard? break a lance,","And run a tilt at death within a chair?"],"id":454,"m":"What will you do, good greybeard? Break a lance and charge at death in a chair?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Foul fiend of France, and hag of all despite,","Encompass'd with thy lustful paramours!","Becomes it thee to taunt his valiant age,","And twit with cowardice a man half dead?","Damsel, I'll have a bout with you again,","Or else let Talbot perish with this shame."],"id":455,"m":"Foul devil of France, spiteful hag, surrounded by your lustful lovers! Is it right for you to mock his brave old age and taunt a half-dead man with cowardice? Girl, I'll have another fight with you, or else let Talbot die with this shame."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Are ye so hot? yet, Pucelle, hold thy peace;","If Talbot do but thunder, rain will follow."],"id":456,"m":"Are you so heated? Still, Pucelle, keep quiet; if Talbot thunders, rain will follow."},{"t":"sd","text":"The English party whisper together in council.","id":457},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","cont":1,"lines":["God speed the parliament! who shall be the speaker?"],"id":458,"m":"God bless the parliament! Who will be the Speaker?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Dare ye come forth and meet us in the field?"],"id":459,"m":"Do you dare come out and meet us on the battlefield?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Belike your lordship takes us then for fools,","To try if that our own be ours or no."],"id":460,"m":"Your lordship must take us for fools, to risk finding out whether what's ours is ours or not."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["I speak not to that railing Hecate,","But unto thee, Alencon, and the rest;","Will ye, like soldiers, come and fight it out?"],"id":461,"m":"I'm not talking to that ranting witch, but to you, Alencon, and the rest. Will you come out and fight it out like soldiers?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALENCON","lines":["Signior, no."],"id":462,"m":"No, signor."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Signior, hang! base muleters of France!","Like peasant foot-boys do they keep the walls,","And dare not take up arms like gentlemen."],"id":463,"m":"Signor, go hang! Low French mule-drivers! They keep behind the walls like peasant servants and don't dare take up arms like gentlemen."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Away, captains! let 's get us from the walls;","For Talbot means no goodness by his looks.","God be wi' you, my lord! we came but to tell you","That we are here."],"id":464,"m":"Away, captains! Let's get off the walls, for Talbot's looks mean us no good. God be with you, my lord! We only came to tell you we are here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt from the walls.","id":465},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["And there will we be too, ere it be long,","Or else reproach be Talbot's greatest fame!","Vow, Burgundy, by honor of thy house,","Prick'd on by public wrongs sustain'd in France,","Either to get the town again or die:","And I, as sure as English Henry lives,","And as his father here was conqueror,","As sure as in this late-betrayed town","Great Coeur-de-lion's heart was buried,","So sure I swear to get the town or die."],"id":466,"m":"And we'll be there too before long, or else let disgrace be Talbot's greatest fame! Burgundy, swear by the honour of your family, spurred on by the public wrongs you've suffered in France, either to get the town back or die. And I swear, as surely as English Henry lives, as surely as his father was the conqueror here, and as surely as great Lionheart's heart was buried in this recently betrayed town, that I'll take the town or die."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["My vows are equal partners with thy vows."],"id":467,"m":"My vows are equal partners with yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["But, ere we go, regard this dying prince,","The valiant Duke of Bedford. Come, my lord,","We will bestow you in some better place,","Fitter for sickness and for crazy age."],"id":468,"m":"But before we go, look after this dying prince, the brave Duke of Bedford. Come, my lord, we'll move you to some better place, more suitable for sickness and frail old age."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["Lord Talbot, do not so dishonor me:","Here will I sit before the walls of Rouen,","And will be partner of your weal or woe."],"id":469,"m":"Lord Talbot, don't dishonour me like that. I'll sit here before the walls of Rouen and share your good or bad fortune."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["Courageous Bedford, let us now persuade you."],"id":470,"m":"Brave Bedford, let us persuade you now."},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["Not to be gone from hence; for once I read","That stout Pendragon in his litter sick","Came to the field and vanquished his foes.","Methinks I should revive the soldiers' hearts,","Because I ever found them as myself."],"id":471,"m":"Not to leave here; for I once read that brave Pendragon, sick in his litter, came to the battlefield and defeated his enemies. I think I should put fresh heart into the soldiers, for I always found them like myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Undaunted spirit in a dying breast!","Then be it so: heavens keep old Bedford safe!","And now no more ado, brave Burgundy,","But gather we our forces out of hand","And set upon our boasting enemy."],"id":472,"m":"A fearless spirit in a dying body! Then so be it. Heaven keep old Bedford safe! And now no more fuss, brave Burgundy; let's gather our forces at once and attack our boasting enemy."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Bedford and Attendants.","id":473},{"t":"sd","text":"An alarum: excursions. Enter Sir John Fastolfe and a Captain.","id":474},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["Whither away, Sir John Fastolfe, in such haste?"],"id":475,"m":"Where are you going in such a hurry, Sir John Fastolfe?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FASTOLFE","lines":["Whither away! to save myself by flight:","We are like to have the overthrow again."],"id":476,"m":"Where am I going? To save myself by running away. We're likely to be beaten again."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["What! Will you fly, and leave Lord Talbot?"],"id":477,"m":"What! Will you run away and leave Lord Talbot?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FASTOLFE","lines":["Aye,","All the Talbots in the world, to save my life."],"id":478,"m":"Yes, all the Talbots in the world, to save my life."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":479},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["Cowardly knight! ill fortune follow thee!"],"id":480,"m":"Cowardly knight! May bad luck follow you!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":481},{"t":"sd","text":"Retreat: excursions. La Pucelle, Alencon, and Charles fly.","id":482},{"t":"sp","who":"BEDFORD","lines":["Now, quiet soul, depart when heaven please,","For I have seen our enemies' overthrow.","What is the trust or strength of foolish man?","They that of late were daring with their scoffs","Are glad and fain by flight to save themselves."],"id":483,"m":"Now, peaceful soul, leave when heaven pleases, for I have seen our enemies defeated. What can be trusted in foolish man, or his strength? Those who were so bold with their mocking just now are glad to save themselves by running away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Bedford dies, and is carried in by two in his chair.","id":484},{"t":"sd","text":"An alarum. Re-enter Talbot, Burgundy, and the rest.","id":485},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Lost, and recover'd in a day again!","This is a double honor, Burgundy:","Yet heavens have glory for this victory!"],"id":486,"m":"Lost and won back again in a single day! This is a double honour, Burgundy. Yet the glory for this victory belongs to heaven!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["Warlike and martial Talbot, Burgundy","Enshrines thee in his heart, and there erects","Thy noble deeds as valor's monuments."],"id":487,"m":"Warlike, soldierly Talbot, Burgundy enshrines you in his heart and sets up your noble deeds there as monuments to courage."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Thanks, gentle duke. But where is Pucelle now?","I think her old familiar is asleep:","Now where 's the Bastard's braves, and Charles his gleeks?","What, all amort? Rouen hangs her head for grief","That such a valiant company are fled.","Now will we take some order in the town,","Placing therein some expert officers;","And then depart to Paris to the king,","For there young Henry with his nobles lie."],"id":488,"m":"Thank you, gentle Duke. But where is Pucelle now? I think her old demon is asleep. Now where is the Bastard's swaggering, and Charles's mocking? What, all dead-hearted? Rouen hangs her head in grief that such brave company has run away. Now we'll put things in order in the town, placing some experienced officers in charge, and then go to the King in Paris, for young Henry is there with his nobles."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["What Lord Talbot pleaseth Burgundy."],"id":489,"m":"Whatever pleases Lord Talbot pleases Burgundy."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["But yet, before we go, let 's not forget","The noble Duke of Bedford late deceased,","But see his exequies fulfill'd in Rouen:","A braver soldier never couched lance,","A gentler heart did never sway in court;","But kings and mightiest potentates must die,","For that's the end of human misery."],"id":490,"m":"But before we go, let's not forget the noble Duke of Bedford, who has just died; let's see his funeral carried out in Rouen. No braver soldier ever levelled a lance; no gentler heart ever ruled at court. But kings and the mightiest rulers must die, for that is the end of human misery."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":491},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"III","loc":"The plains near Rouen.","id":492},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Charles, the Bastard of Orleans, Alencon, La Pucelle, and forces.","id":493},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Dismay not, princes, at this accident,","Nor grieve that Rouen is so recovered:","Care is no cure, but rather corrosive,","For things that are not to be remedied.","Let frantic Talbot triumph for a while","And like a peacock sweep along his tail;","We'll pull his plumes and take away his train,","If Dauphin and the rest will be but ruled."],"id":494,"m":"Don't be dismayed, princes, by this setback, or grieve that Rouen has been recaptured. Worry is no cure, but rather makes worse things that can't be put right. Let frantic Talbot celebrate for a while and sweep his tail along like a peacock; we'll pluck his feathers and take away his tail if the Dauphin and the rest will just be guided by me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["We have been guided by thee hitherto,","And of thy cunning had no diffidence:","One sudden foil shall never breed distrust"],"id":495,"m":"We have been guided by you up to now and had no doubts about your skill. One sudden defeat will never make us distrust you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASTARD","lines":["Search out thy wit for secret policies,","And we will make thee famous through the world."],"id":496,"m":"Search your mind for secret plans, and we'll make you famous throughout the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALENCON","lines":["We'll set thy statue in some holy place,","And have thee reverenced like a blessed saint.","Employ thee then, sweet virgin, for our good."],"id":497,"m":"We'll put your statue in some holy place and have you worshipped like a blessed saint. So work, sweet virgin, for our good."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Then thus it must be; this doth Joan devise:","By fair persuasions mix'd with sugar'd words","We will entice the Duke of Burgundy","To leave the Talbot and to follow us."],"id":498,"m":"Then this is how it must be; this is Joan's plan: with fair persuasion mixed with sweet words, we'll tempt the Duke of Burgundy to leave Talbot and follow us."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Aye, marry, sweeting, if we could do that,","France were no place for Henry's warriors;","Nor should that nation boast it so with us,","But be extirped from our provinces."],"id":499,"m":"Yes indeed, sweetheart, if we could do that, France would be no place for Henry's soldiers, and that nation wouldn't lord it over us like this, but be rooted out of our provinces."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALENCON","lines":["For ever should they be expulsed from France,","And not have tide of an earldom here."],"id":500,"m":"They'd be thrown out of France forever and not keep so much as an earldom here."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Your honours shall perceive how I will work","To bring this matter to the wished end."],"id":501,"m":"Your lordships will see how I'll work to bring this to the end we want."},{"t":"sd","text":"Drum sounds afar off.","id":502},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","cont":1,"lines":["Hark! by the sound of drum you may perceive","Their powers are marching unto Paris-ward."],"id":503,"m":"Listen! By the sound of the drum you can tell their forces are marching towards Paris."},{"t":"sd","text":"Here sound an English march. Enter, and pass over at a distance, Talbot and his forces.","id":504},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","cont":1,"lines":["There goes the Talbot, with his colors spread,","And all the troops of English after him."],"id":505,"m":"There goes Talbot, with his flags flying, and all the English troops behind him."},{"t":"sd","text":"French march. Enter the Duke of Burgundy and forces.","id":506},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","cont":1,"lines":["Now in the rearward comes the duke and his:","Fortune in favor makes him lag behind.","Summon a parley; we will talk with him."],"id":507,"m":"Now at the back comes the Duke and his men. Luck is on our side to make him lag behind. Call for a parley; we'll talk with him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Trumpets sound a parley.","id":508},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["A parley with the Duke of Burgundy!"],"id":509,"m":"A parley with the Duke of Burgundy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["Who craves a parley with the Burgundy?"],"id":510,"m":"Who asks for a parley with Burgundy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["The princely Charles of France, thy countryman."],"id":511,"m":"The princely Charles of France, your countryman."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["What say'st thou, Charles? for I am marching","hence."],"id":512,"m":"What do you have to say, Charles? I'm marching away from here."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Speak, Pucelle, and enchant him with thy words."],"id":513,"m":"Speak, Pucelle, and enchant him with your words."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Brave Burgundy, undoubted hope of France!","Stay, let thy humble handmaid speak to thee."],"id":514,"m":"Brave Burgundy, certain hope of France! Wait, let your humble servant speak to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["Speak on; but be not over-tedious."],"id":515,"m":"Speak on, but don't go on too long."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Look on thy country, look on fertile France,","And see the cities and the towns defaced","By wasting ruin of the cruel foe.","As looks the mother on her lowly babe","When death doth close his tender dying eyes,","See, see the pining malady of France;","Behold the wounds, the most unnatural wounds,","Which thou thyself hast given her woful breast.","O, turn thy edged sword another way;","Strike those that hurt, and hurt not those that help.","One drop of blood drawn from thy country's bosom","Should grieve thee more than streams of foreign gore:","Return thee therefore with a flood of tears,","And wash away thy country's stained spots."],"id":516,"m":"Look at your country, look at fertile France, and see the cities and towns spoiled by the destruction of the cruel enemy. As a mother looks at her little baby when death closes its tender dying eyes, see, see the wasting sickness of France. Look at the wounds, the most unnatural wounds, that you yourself have given her sorrowful breast. Oh, turn your sharp sword another way. Strike those who hurt her, and don't hurt those who help. One drop of blood drawn from your country's breast should grieve you more than rivers of foreign blood. So come back with a flood of tears and wash away your country's stains."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["Either she hath bewitch'd me with her words,","Or nature makes me suddenly relent."],"id":517,"m":"Either she has bewitched me with her words or natural feeling suddenly makes me soften."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Besides, all French and France exclaims on thee,","Doubting thy birth and lawful progeny.","Who join'st thou with but with a lordly nation","That will not trust thee but for profit's sake?","When Talbot hath set footing once in France,","And fashion'd thee that instrument of ill,","Who then but English Henry will be lord,","And thou be thrust out like a fugitive?","Call we to mind, and mark but this for proof,","Was not the Duke of Orleans thy foe?","And was he not in England prisoner?","But when they heard he was thine enemy,","They set him free without his ransom paid,","In spite of Burgundy and all his friends.","See, then, thou fight'st against thy countrymen","And join'st with them will be thy slaughtermen.","Come, come, return; return, thou wandering lord;","Charles and the rest will take thee in their arms."],"id":518,"m":"Besides, all the French and France itself cry out against you, doubting your birth and legitimacy. Who are you joining but an arrogant nation that will only trust you for profit? When Talbot has once got a foothold in France and made you his tool for harm, who but English Henry will be lord, and you'll be thrown out like a runaway? Let's remember, and just take this as proof: wasn't the Duke of Orleans your enemy? And wasn't he a prisoner in England? But when they heard he was your enemy, they set him free without his ransom being paid, in spite of Burgundy and all his friends. See, then, you are fighting against your own countrymen and joining those who will be your butchers. Come, come back; come back, you wandering lord. Charles and the rest will take you in their arms."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["I am vanquished; these haughty words of hers","Have batt'red me like roaring cannon-shot,","And made me almost yield upon my knees.","Forgive me, country, and sweet countrymen,","And, lords, accept this hearty kind embrace:","My forces and my power of men are yours:","So, farewell, Talbot; I'll no longer trust thee."],"id":519,"m":"I am beaten. These proud words of hers have battered me like roaring cannon-shot and almost brought me to my knees. Forgive me, my country and sweet countrymen; and, lords, accept this warm embrace. My forces and my troops are yours. So goodbye, Talbot; I'll trust you no longer."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["[Aside] Done like a Frenchman: turn and turn","again!"],"id":520,"m":"(Aside) Just like a Frenchman: changing sides again and again!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Welcome, brave duke; thy friendship makes us","fresh."],"id":521,"m":"Welcome, brave Duke; your friendship refreshes us."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASTARD","lines":["And doth beget new courage in our breasts."],"id":522,"m":"And puts new courage in our hearts."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALENCON","lines":["Pucelle hath bravely play'd her part in this,","And doth deserve a coronet of gold."],"id":523,"m":"Pucelle has played her part bravely in this and deserves a gold coronet."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Now let us on, my lords, and join our powers,","And seek how we may prejudice the foe."],"id":524,"m":"Now let's go on, my lords, join our forces and work out how we can damage the enemy."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":525},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"IV","loc":"Paris. The palace.","id":526},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the King, Gloucester, Bishop of Winchester, York, Suffolk, Somerset, Warwick, Exeter: Vernon, Basset, and others. To them with his soldiers, Talbot.","id":527},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["My gracious Prince, and honourable peers,","Hearing of your arrival in this realm,","I have awhile given truce unto my wars,","To do my duty to my sovereign:","In sign whereof, this arm, that hath reclaim'd","To your obedience fifty fortresses,","Twelve cities and seven walled towns of strength,","Beside five hundred prisoners of esteem,","Lets fall his sword before your highness' feet,","And with submissive loyalty of heart","Ascribes the glory of his conquest got","First to my God and next unto your grace. [Kneels]"],"id":528,"m":"My gracious Prince and honourable lords, hearing you had arrived in this kingdom, I have paused my wars for a while to pay my respects to my King. As a sign of which, this arm, which has won back for you fifty fortresses, twelve cities and seven strong walled towns, besides five hundred important prisoners, lets its sword fall at your Highness's feet and, with humble, loyal heart, gives the glory of its conquests first to my God and next to your Grace. (Kneels)"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Is this the Lord Talbot, uncle Gloucester,","That hath so long been resident in France?"],"id":529,"m":"Is this Lord Talbot, uncle Gloucester, who has lived so long in France?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Yes, if it please your majesty, my liege."],"id":530,"m":"Yes, if it please your Majesty, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Welcome, brave captain and victorious lord!","When I was young, as yet I am not old.","I do remember how my father said","A stouter champion never handled sword.","Long since we were resolved of your truth,","Your faithful service and your toil in war;","Yet never have you tasted our reward,","Or been reguerdon'd with so much as thanks.","Because till now we never saw your face:","Therefore, stand up: and for these good deserts,","We here create you Earl of Shrewsbury;","And in our coronation take your place."],"id":531,"m":"Welcome, brave captain and victorious lord! When I was young (though I'm not old yet), I remember my father saying a braver champion never held a sword. We have long been sure of your loyalty, your faithful service and your hard work in war; yet you have never had a reward from us, or even been repaid with thanks, because until now we never saw your face. So stand up, and for these great services we here make you Earl of Shrewsbury; and take your place at our coronation."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sennet. Flourish. Exeunt all but Vernon and Basset.","id":532},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["Now, sir, to you, that were so hot at sea,","Disgracing of these colors that I wear","In honor of my noble Lord of York:—","Dar'st thou maintain the former words thou spakest?"],"id":533,"m":"Now, sir, as for you, who were so hot-tempered at sea, insulting these colours I wear in honour of my noble Lord of York: do you dare stand by the words you said before?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSET","lines":["Yes, sir; as well as you dare patronage","The envious barking of your saucy tongue","Against my lord the Duke of Somerset."],"id":534,"m":"Yes, sir, just as much as you dare defend the spiteful barking of your insolent tongue against my lord, the Duke of Somerset."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["Sirrah, thy lord I honor as he is."],"id":535,"m":"Fellow, I honour your lord for what he is."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSET","lines":["Why, what is he? as good a man as York."],"id":536,"m":"Why, what is he? As good a man as York."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["Hark ye; not so: in witness, take ye that."],"id":537,"m":"Listen: not so. As proof, take that."},{"t":"sd","text":"Strikes him.","id":538},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSET","lines":["Villain, thou know'st the law of arms is such","That whoso draws a sword, 'tis present death,","Or else this blow should broach thy dearest blood.","But I'll unto his majesty, and crave","I may have liberty to venge this wrong;","When thou shalt see I'll meet thee to thy cost."],"id":539,"m":"Villain, you know the law of arms says that anyone who draws a sword here is put to death at once, or else this blow would let out your heart's blood. But I'll go to his Majesty and ask for permission to avenge this wrong. Then you'll see I'll meet you, and it will cost you."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["Well, miscreant, I'll be there as soon as you;","And, after, meet you sooner than you would."],"id":540,"m":"Well, scoundrel, I'll be there as soon as you, and afterwards meet you sooner than you'd like."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":541},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"Paris. A hall of state.","id":542},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the King, Gloucester, Bishop of Winchester, York, Suffolk, Somerset, Warwick, Talbot, Exeter, the Governor of Paris, and others.","id":543},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Lord bishop, set the crown upon his head."],"id":544,"m":"Lord Bishop, set the crown on his head."},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["God save King Henry, of that name the sixth!"],"id":545,"m":"God save King Henry, the sixth of that name!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Now, Governor of Paris, take your oath,","That you elect no other king but him;","Esteem none friends but such as are his friends,","And none your foes but such as shall pretend","Malicious practices against his state:","This shall ye do, so help you righteous God!"],"id":546,"m":"Now, Governor of Paris, take your oath that you will choose no other king but him, count no one your friend but his friends and no one your enemy but those who plot evil against his rule. You will do this, so help you righteous God!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir John Fastolfe.","id":547},{"t":"sp","who":"FASTOLFE","lines":["My gracious sovereign, as I rode from Calais,","To haste unto your coronation,","A letter was deliver'd to my hands,","Writ to your Grace from the Duke of Burgundy."],"id":548,"m":"My gracious King, as I rode from Calais, hurrying to your coronation, a letter was put into my hands, written to your Grace by the Duke of Burgundy."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Shame to the Duke of Burgundy and thee!","I vow'd, base knight, when I did meet thee next,","To tear the garter from thy craven's leg, [Plucking it off]","Which I have done, because unworthily","Thou wast installed in that high degree.","Pardon me, princely Henry, and the rest:","This dastard, at the battle of Patay,","When but in all I was six thousand strong","And that the French were almost ten to one,","Before we met or that a stroke was given,","Like to a trusty squire did run away:","In which assault we lost twelve hundred men;","Myself and divers gentlemen beside","Were there surprised and taken prisoners.","Then judge, great lords, if I have done amiss;","Or whether that such cowards ought to wear","This ornament of knighthood, yea or no."],"id":549,"m":"Shame on the Duke of Burgundy and on you! I swore, low knight, that when I met you next I'd tear the garter from your coward's leg (tearing it off), and I have done it, because you were unworthily given that high rank. Forgive me, princely Henry, and the rest of you. This coward, at the battle of Patay, when I had only six thousand men and the French outnumbered us almost ten to one, ran away like a \"trusty\" squire before we even met or a blow was struck. In that attack we lost twelve hundred men; I and several other gentlemen were surprised and taken prisoner. So judge, great lords, whether I have done wrong, and whether such cowards should wear this badge of knighthood or not."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["To say the truth, this fact was infamous","And ill beseeming any common man,","Much more a knight, a captain, and a leader."],"id":550,"m":"To tell the truth, this act was shameful and would disgrace any common man, let alone a knight, a captain and a leader."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["When first this order was ordain'd, my lords,","Knights of the garter were of noble birth,","Valiant and virtuous, full of haughty courage,","Such as were grown to credit by the wars;","Not fearing death, nor shrinking for distress,","But always resolute in most extremes.","He then that is not furnish'd in this sort","Doth but usurp the sacred name of knight,","Profaning this most honorable order,","And should, if I were worthy to be judge,","Be quite degraded, like a hedge-born swain","That doth presume to boast of gentle blood."],"id":551,"m":"When this order was first founded, my lords, Knights of the Garter were of noble birth, brave and virtuous, full of high courage, men who had won respect in the wars, not afraid of death or shrinking from hardship, but always determined in the greatest dangers. So anyone who doesn't have these qualities only steals the sacred name of knight, defiling this most honourable order, and should, if I were fit to judge, be completely stripped of rank, like a low-born peasant who dares to boast of noble blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Stain to thy countrymen, thou hear'st thy doom!","Be packing, therefore, thou that wast a knight;","Henceforth we banish thee, on pain of death."],"id":552,"m":"Disgrace to your countrymen, you hear your sentence! Get out, then, you who were a knight. From now on we banish you, on pain of death."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Fastolfe.","id":553},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":1,"lines":["And now, my lord protector, view the letter","Sent from our uncle Duke of Burgundy."],"id":554,"m":"And now, Lord Protector, read the letter sent from our uncle, the Duke of Burgundy."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["What means his grace,","that he hath changed his style?","No more but, plain and bluntly, 'To the King!'","Hath he forgot he is his sovereign?","Or doth this churlish superscription","Pretend some alteration in good will?","What's here? [Reads] 'I have, upon especial cause,","Moved with compassion of my country's wreck,","Together with the pitiful complaints","Of such as your oppression feeds upon,","Forsaken your pernicious faction,","And join'd with Charles, the rightful King of France.'","O monstrous treachery! can this be so,","That in alliance, amity and oaths,","There should be found such false dissembling guile?"],"id":555,"m":"What does his Grace mean by changing the way he addresses me? Nothing more than, plain and blunt, \"To the King!\" Has he forgotten he's his king? Or does this rude address mean some change in his loyalty? What's here? (Reads) \"For special reasons, moved by pity for my country's ruin and by the pitiful complaints of those your oppression preys on, I have abandoned your harmful party and joined Charles, the rightful King of France.\" Oh, monstrous treachery! Can it be that such false, deceitful cunning should be found in alliance, friendship and sworn oaths?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What! doth my uncle Burgundy revolt?"],"id":556,"m":"What! Has my uncle Burgundy rebelled?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["He doth, my lord, and is become your foe."],"id":557,"m":"He has, my lord, and has become your enemy."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Is that the worst this letter doth contain?"],"id":558,"m":"Is that the worst the letter contains?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["It is the worst, and all, my lord, he writes."],"id":559,"m":"That's the worst, and all he writes, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Why, then, Lord Talbot there shall talk with him,","And give him chastisement for this abuse.","How say you, my lord? are you not content?"],"id":560,"m":"Why, then, Lord Talbot here will talk with him and punish him for this wrong. What do you say, my lord? Are you willing?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Content, my liege! yes; but that I am prevented,","I should have begg'd I might have been employ'd."],"id":561,"m":"Willing, my lord! Yes; if you hadn't got in first, I would have begged to be sent."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Then gather strength, and march unto him straight:","Let him perceive how ill we brook his treason.","And what offence it is to flout his friends."],"id":562,"m":"Then gather your forces and march straight at him. Let him see how badly we take his treason and what an offence it is to mock his friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["I go, my lord, in heart desiring still","You may behold confusion of your foes."],"id":563,"m":"I'm going, my lord, with all my heart wishing that you may see your enemies destroyed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":564},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Vernon and Basset.","id":565},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["Grant me the combat, gracious sovereign."],"id":566,"m":"Allow me a duel, gracious King."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSET","lines":["And me, my lord, grant me the combat too."],"id":567,"m":"And me, my lord; allow me the duel too."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["This is my servant: hear him, noble prince."],"id":568,"m":"This is my servant; listen to him, noble Prince."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["And this is mine: sweet Henry, favor him."],"id":569,"m":"And this is mine; sweet Henry, favour him."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Be patient, lords, and give them leave to speak.","Say, gentlemen, what makes you thus exclaim?","And wherefore crave you combat? or with whom?"],"id":570,"m":"Be patient, lords, and let them speak. Tell me, gentlemen, why are you shouting like this? Why do you ask for a duel, and with whom?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["With him, my lord; for he hath done me wrong."],"id":571,"m":"With him, my lord, for he has wronged me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSET","lines":["And I with him; for he hath done me wrong."],"id":572,"m":"And I with him, for he has wronged me."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What is that wrong whereof you both complain?","First let me know, and then I'll answer you."],"id":573,"m":"What is this wrong you both complain of? Let me know first, and then I'll answer you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSET","lines":["Crossing the sea from England into France,","This fellow here, with envious carping tongue,","Upbraided me about the rose I wear;","Saying, the sanguine colour of the leaves","Did represent my master's blushing cheeks,","When stubbornly he did repugn the truth","About a certain question in the law","Argued betwixt the Duke of York and him;","With other vile and ignominious terms:","In confutation of which rude reproach,","And in defence of my lord's worthiness,","I crave the benefit of law of arms."],"id":574,"m":"Crossing the sea from England to France, this fellow here, with his spiteful, sneering tongue, taunted me about the rose I wear, saying the blood-red colour of its petals stood for my master's blushing cheeks when he stubbornly denied the truth in a certain legal question argued between him and the Duke of York; and other vile and shameful things. To answer that rude insult and defend my lord's honour, I ask for my right under the law of arms."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["And that is my petition, noble lord:","For though he seem with forged quaint conceit","To set a gloss upon his bold intent,","Yet know, my lord, I was provoked by him;","And he first took exceptions at this badge,","Pronouncing that the paleness of this flower","Bewray'd the faintness of my master's heart."],"id":575,"m":"And that is my request, noble lord. For though he tries to put a fine gloss on his bold intentions with clever made-up stories, know, my lord, that he provoked me. He first found fault with this badge, saying the paleness of this flower revealed the cowardice of my master's heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Will not this malice, Somerset, be left?"],"id":576,"m":"Somerset, won't you drop this spite?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Your private grudge, my Lord of York, will out,","Though ne'er so cunningly you smother it."],"id":577,"m":"Your private grudge will come out, my Lord of York, however cleverly you hide it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Good Lord, what madness rules in brainsick men,","When for so slight and frivolous a cause","Such factious emulations shall arise!","Good cousins both, of York and Somerset,","Quiet yourselves, I pray, and be at peace."],"id":578,"m":"Good Lord, what madness rules in sick-brained men, when such party rivalries spring up from so small and silly a cause! Good kinsmen both, York and Somerset, calm down, please, and be at peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Let this dissension first be tried by fight,","And then your highness shall command a peace."],"id":579,"m":"Let this quarrel be settled by a fight first, and then your Highness can order peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["The quarrel toucheth none but us alone;","Betwixt ourselves let us decide it then."],"id":580,"m":"The quarrel concerns no one but us; so let's decide it between ourselves."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["There is my pledge; accept it, Somerset."],"id":581,"m":"There's my glove as a challenge; take it, Somerset."},{"t":"sp","who":"VERNON","lines":["Nay, let it rest where it began at first."],"id":582,"m":"No, let it stay where it first began."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASSET","lines":["Confirm it so, mine honorable lord."],"id":583,"m":"Agree to that, my honourable lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Confirm it so! Confounded be your strife!","And perish ye, with your audacious prate!","Presumptuous vassals, are you not ashamed","With this immodest clamorous outrage","To trouble and disturb the king and us?","And you, my lords, methinks you do not well","To bear with their perverse objections;","Much less to take occasion from their mouths","To raise a mutiny betwixt yourselves:","Let me persuade you take a better course."],"id":584,"m":"Agree to that! Damn your quarrel! And may you perish with your bold chatter! Arrogant servants, aren't you ashamed to trouble and disturb the King and us with this rude, noisy outrage? And you, my lords, I don't think you do well to put up with their wrong-headed complaints, much less to take the chance from their mouths to start a mutiny among yourselves. Let me persuade you to take a better course."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["It grieves his highness: good my lords, be friends."],"id":585,"m":"It upsets his Highness. My good lords, be friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Come hither, you that would be combatants:","Henceforth I charge you, as you love our favor,","Quite to forget this quarrel and the cause.","And you, my lords, remember where we are:","In France, amongst a fickle wavering nation;","If they perceive dissension in our looks","And that within ourselves we disagree,","How will their grudging stomachs be provoked","To willful disobedience, and rebel!","Beside, what infamy will there arise","When foreign princes shall be certified","That for a toy, a thing of no regard,","King Henry's peers and chief nobility","Destroy'd themselves and lost the realm of France","O, think upon the conquest of my father,","My tender years; and let us not forgo","That for a trifle that was bought with blood!","Let me be umpire in this doubtful strife.","I see no reason, if I wear this rose,"],"id":586,"m":"Come here, you who want to fight. From now on, as you value our favour, I order you to forget this quarrel and its cause completely. And you, my lords, remember where we are: in France, among a fickle, unreliable nation. If they see disagreement in our faces and that we're quarrelling among ourselves, how their resentful hearts will be stirred to wilful disobedience and rebellion! Besides, what disgrace will follow when foreign princes hear that King Henry's lords and chief nobles destroyed themselves and lost the kingdom of France over a trifle, a thing of no importance! Oh, think of my father's conquests and my young age, and let's not throw away for a trifle what was bought with blood! Let me be the judge in this uncertain quarrel. I see no reason, if I wear this rose (putting on a red rose),"},{"t":"sd","text":"Putting on a red rose.","id":587},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":1,"lines":["That any one should therefore be suspicious","I more incline to Somerset than York:","Both are my kinsmen, and I love them both:","As well they may upbraid me with my crown,","Because, forsooth, the king of Scots is crown'd.","But your discretions better can persuade","Than I am able to instruct or teach;","And, therefore, as we hither came in peace,","So let us still continue peace and love.","Cousin of York, we institute your grace","To be our Regent in these parts of France:","And, good my Lord of Somerset, unite","Your troops of horsemen with his bands of foot;","And, like true subjects, sons of your progenitors,","Go cheerfully together and digest","Your angry choler on your enemies.","Ourself, my lord protector and the rest","After some respite will return to Calais;","From thence to England; where I hope ere long","To be presented, by your victories,","With Charles, Alencon, and that traitorous rout."],"id":588,"m":"why anyone should suspect that I lean more towards Somerset than York. Both are my relatives, and I love them both. They might just as well blame me for my crown because, of course, the King of Scots is crowned too. But your own good sense can persuade you better than I can instruct or teach; so, as we came here in peace, let's keep on in peace and love. Cousin of York, we appoint your Grace our Regent in these parts of France. And, my good Lord of Somerset, join your cavalry with his foot soldiers, and like true subjects and sons of your ancestors, go cheerfully together and spend your angry temper on your enemies. I, the Lord Protector and the rest will return to Calais after a short rest, and from there to England, where I hope before long that your victories will present me with Charles, Alencon and that traitorous mob."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Exeunt all but York, Warwick, Exeter and Vernon.","id":589},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["My Lord of York, I promise you, the king","Prettily, methought, did play the orator."],"id":590,"m":"My Lord of York, I promise you, I thought the King played the speaker rather nicely."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["And so he did; but yet I like it not,","In that he wears the badge of Somerset."],"id":591,"m":"So he did; but I don't like the fact that he wears Somerset's badge."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Tush, that was but his fancy, blame him not;","I dare presume, sweet prince, he thought no harm."],"id":592,"m":"Pooh, that was just a whim; don't blame him. I dare say, sweet Prince, he meant no harm."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["An if I wist he did,—but let it rest;","Other affairs must now be managed."],"id":593,"m":"If I knew he did— but let it go. Other business must be dealt with now."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Exeter.","id":594},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Well didst thou, Richard, to suppress thy voice;","For, had the passions of thy heart burst out,","I fear we should have seen decipher'd there","More rancorous spite, more furious raging broils,","Than yet can be imagined or supposed.","But howsoe'er, no simple man that sees","This jarring discord of nobility,","This shouldering of each other in the court,","This factious bandying of their favorites,","But that it doth presage some ill event.","Tis much when scepters are in children's hands;","But more when envy breeds unkind division;","There comes the ruin, there begins confusion."],"id":595,"m":"You did well, Richard, to keep quiet; for if the feelings of your heart had burst out, I fear we would have seen revealed there more bitter spite and more furious, raging quarrels than can yet be imagined. But in any case, no simple man who sees this clashing discord among the nobles, this shoving at each other in court, this factional fighting among their followers, could fail to see that it foretells some evil outcome. It's bad enough when a child holds the sceptre, but worse when envy breeds unnatural division. That's where ruin comes; that's where chaos begins."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":596},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"Before Bordeaux.","id":597},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Talbot, with trump and drum.","id":598},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Go to the gates of Bordeaux, trumpeter:","Summon their general unto the wall."],"id":599,"m":"Go to the gates of Bordeaux, trumpeter, and call their general to the wall."},{"t":"sd","text":"Trumpet sounds. Enter General and others, aloft.","id":600},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","cont":1,"lines":["English John Talbot, Captains, calls you forth,","Servant in arms to Harry King of England;","And thus he would: Open your city-gates,","Be humble to us; call my sovereign yours,","And do him homage as obedient subjects;","And I'll withdraw me and my bloody power:","But, if you frown upon this proffer'd peace,","You tempt the fury of my three attendants,","Lean famine, quartering steel, and climbing fire;","Who in a moment even with the earth","Shall lay your stately and air-braving towers,","If you forsake the offer of their love."],"id":601,"m":"Captains, English John Talbot calls you out, a soldier in the service of Harry, King of England, and this is what he wants: open your city gates, submit to us, call my king your king and pay him homage as obedient subjects, and I'll withdraw myself and my bloody army. But if you frown on this offer of peace, you provoke the fury of my three servants, thin famine, cutting steel and climbing fire, who will in a moment level your grand, sky-defying towers with the earth if you reject their offer of love."},{"t":"sp","who":"GENERAL","lines":["Thou ominous and fearful owl of death,","Our nation's terror and their bloody scourge!","The period of thy tyranny approacheth.","On us thou canst not enter but by death;","For, I protest, we are well fortified","And strong enough to issue out and fight:","If thou retire, the Dauphin, well appointed,","Stands with the snares of war to tangle thee:","On either hand thee there are squadrons pitch'd","To wall thee from the liberty of flight;","And no way canst thou turn thee for redress,","But death doth front thee with apparent spoil,","And pale destruction meets thee in the face.","Ten thousand French have ta'en the sacrament","To rive their dangerous artillery","Upon no Christian soul but English Talbot.","Lo, there thou stand'st, a breathing valiant man,","Of an invincible unconquer'd spirit!","This is the latest glory of thy praise","That I, thy enemy, due thee withal;","For ere the glass, that now begins to run,","Finish the process of his sandy hour,","These eyes, that see thee now well colored,","Shall see thee wither'd, bloody, pale, and dead."],"id":602,"m":"You ominous, fearful owl of death, our nation's terror and its bloody scourge! The end of your tyranny is coming. You can't get in to us except through death, for I declare we're well fortified and strong enough to come out and fight. If you retreat, the Dauphin, well equipped, is waiting with the traps of war to catch you. There are troops set on both sides of you to wall you off from escape, and there's no way you can turn for help without death facing you with certain ruin and pale destruction meeting you face to face. Ten thousand French have sworn on the sacrament to fire their deadly cannon at no Christian soul but English Talbot. Look, there you stand, a living brave man of unbeatable, unconquered spirit! This is the last praise I, your enemy, will give you; for before this hourglass, which is just starting to run, finishes its sandy hour, these eyes that see you now healthy will see you withered, bloody, pale and dead."},{"t":"sd","text":"Drum afar off.","id":603},{"t":"sp","who":"GENERAL","cont":1,"lines":["Hark! hark! the Dauphin's drum, a warning bell,","Sings heavy music to thy timorous soul;","And mine shall ring thy dire departure out."],"id":604,"m":"Listen! Listen! The Dauphin's drum, a warning bell, sings sad music to your fearful soul, and mine will ring out your dreadful death."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt General, etc.","id":605},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["He fables not; I hear the enemy:","Out, some light horsemen, and peruse their wings.","O, negligent and heedless discipline!","How are we park'd and bounded in a pale,","A little herd of England's timorous deer,","Mazed with a yelping kennel of French curs!","If we be English deer, be then in blood;","Not rascal-like, to fall down with a pinch,","But rather, moody-mad and desperate stags,","Turn on the bloody hounds with heads of steel","And make the cowards stand aloof at bay:","Sell every man his life as dear as mine,","And they shall find dear deer of us, my friends.","God and Saint George, Talbot and England's right,","Prosper our colors in this dangerous fight!"],"id":606,"m":"He's not making it up; I hear the enemy. Out, some light horsemen, and scout their flanks. Oh, careless, thoughtless discipline! How we are penned and fenced in, a little herd of England's timid deer, bewildered by a yelping pack of French dogs! If we are English deer, then let us be in full vigour, not like weak deer that fall at a nip, but rather like angry, desperate stags; turn on the bloody hounds with heads of steel and make the cowards keep their distance. Let every man sell his life as dearly as I sell mine, and they'll find us dear deer, my friends. God and Saint George, Talbot and England's right, bless our flags in this dangerous fight!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":607},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"Plains in Gascony.","id":608},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger that meets York. Enter York with trumpet and many soldiers.","id":609},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Are not the speedy scouts return'd again,","That dogg'd the mighty army of the Dauphin?"],"id":610,"m":"Haven't the fast scouts come back yet, who were following the Dauphin's mighty army?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["They are return'd, my lord, and give it out","That he is march'd to Bordeaux with his power,","To fight with Talbot: as he march'd along,","By your espials were discovered","Two mightier troops than that the Dauphin led,","Which join'd with him and made their march for","Bordeaux."],"id":611,"m":"They have come back, my lord, and report that he has marched to Bordeaux with his army to fight Talbot. As he marched along, your spies discovered two larger forces than the one the Dauphin led, which joined up with him and set out for Bordeaux."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["A plague upon that villain Somerset,","That thus delays my promised supply","Of horsemen, that were levied for this siege!","Renowned Talbot doth expect my aid,","And I am lowted by a traitor villain,","And cannot help the noble chevalier:","God comfort him in this necessity!","If he miscarry, farewell wars in France."],"id":612,"m":"A plague on that villain Somerset, who is holding back the cavalry I was promised, raised for this siege! Famous Talbot is expecting my help, and I'm made a fool of by a treacherous villain and can't help the noble knight. God comfort him in this need! If he fails, goodbye to the wars in France."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir William Lucy.","id":613},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCY","lines":["Thou princely leader of our English strength,","Never so needful on the earth of France,","Spur to the rescue of the noble Talbot,","Who now is girdled with a waist of iron,","And hemm'd about with grim destruction.","To Bordeaux, warlike Duke! to Bordeaux, York!","Else, farewell, Talbot, France, and England's honor."],"id":614,"m":"Princely leader of our English forces, never so needed on French soil, ride hard to the rescue of noble Talbot, who is now surrounded by a belt of iron and hemmed in by grim destruction. To Bordeaux, warlike Duke! To Bordeaux, York! Or else goodbye to Talbot, France and England's honour."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["O God, that Somerset, who in proud heart","Doth stop my cornets, were in Talbot's place!","So should we save a valiant gentleman","By forfeiting a traitor and a coward.","Mad ire and wrathful fury makes me weep,","That thus we die, while remiss traitors sleep."],"id":615,"m":"Oh God, if only Somerset, who in his pride holds back my cavalry, were in Talbot's place! Then we would save a brave gentleman by losing a traitor and a coward. Mad anger and furious rage make me weep that we die like this while lazy traitors sleep."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCY","lines":["O, send some succor to the distress'd lord!"],"id":616,"m":"Oh, send some help to the lord in danger!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["He dies; we lose; I break my warlike word;","We mourn, France smiles; we lose, they daily get;","All 'long of this vile traitor Somerset."],"id":617,"m":"He dies; we lose; I break my soldier's word. We mourn, France smiles; we lose, they gain every day; all because of this vile traitor Somerset."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCY","lines":["Then God take mercy on brave Talbot's soul;","And on his son young John, who two hours since","I met in travel toward his warlike father!","This seven years did not Talbot see his son;","And now they meet where both their lives are done."],"id":618,"m":"Then God have mercy on brave Talbot's soul, and on his son young John, whom I met two hours ago travelling towards his warlike father! For seven years Talbot hasn't seen his son, and now they meet where both their lives will end."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Alas, what joy shall noble Talbot have,","To bid his young son welcome to his grave?","Away! vexation almost stops my breath,","That sunder'd friends greet in the hour of death.","Lucy, farewell: no more my fortune can,","But curse the cause I cannot aid the man.","Maine, Blois, Poictiers, and Tours, are won away,","'Long all of Somerset and his delay."],"id":619,"m":"Alas, what joy will noble Talbot have in welcoming his young son to his grave? Away! Anguish almost stops my breath, that separated loved ones meet at the hour of death. Lucy, goodbye. My fortune can do no more than curse the cause that I can't help the man. Maine, Blois, Poitiers and Tours are lost, all because of Somerset and his delay."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit, with his soldiers.","id":620},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCY","lines":["Thus, while the vulture of sedition","Feeds in the bosom of such great commanders,","Sleeping neglection doth betray to loss","The conquest of our scarce cold conqueror,","That ever living man of memory,","Henry the Fifth: whiles they each other cross,","Lives, honors, lands and all hurry to loss."],"id":621,"m":"So while the vulture of rebellion feeds in the hearts of such great commanders, sleepy neglect loses the conquests of our hardly cold conqueror, Henry the Fifth, that man who will live forever in memory. While they block each other, lives, honours, lands and everything rush to ruin."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":622},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"IV","loc":"Other plains in Gascony.","id":623},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Somerset, with his army; a Captain of Talbot's with him.","id":624},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["It is too late; I cannot send them now:","This expedition was by York and Talbot","Too rashly plotted: all our general force","Might with a sally of the very town","Be buckled with: the over-daring Talbot","Hath sullied all his gloss of former honor","By this unheedful, desperate, wild adventure:","York set him on to fight and die in shame,","That, Talbot dead, great York might bear the name."],"id":625,"m":"It's too late; I can't send them now. York and Talbot planned this expedition too rashly. Our whole army could have been taken on by a single sortie from the town. Over-bold Talbot has tarnished all the shine of his former honour by this careless, desperate, wild venture. York sent him to fight and die in shame so that, with Talbot dead, great York could have the fame."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["Here is Sir William Lucy, who with me","Set from our o'er-match'd forces forth for aid."],"id":626,"m":"Here is Sir William Lucy, who set out with me from our outnumbered forces to get help."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir William Lucy.","id":627},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["How now, Sir William! whither were you sent?"],"id":628,"m":"What now, Sir William! Where were you sent from?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCY","lines":["Whither, my lord? from bought and sold Lord Talbot;","Who, ring'd about with bold adversity,","Cries out for noble York and Somerset,","To beat assailing death from his weak legions;","And whiles the honorable captain there","Drops bloody sweat from his war-wearied limbs,","And, in advantage lingering, looks for rescue,","You, his false hopes, the trust of England's honor,","Keep off aloof with worthless emulation.","Let not your private discord keep away","The levied succors that should lend him aid,","While he, renowned noble gentleman,","Yield up his life unto a world of odds.","Orleans the Bastard, Charles, Burgundy,","Alencon, Reignier, compass him about,","And Talbot perisheth by your default."],"id":629,"m":"Where from, my lord? From Lord Talbot, betrayed and sold, who, ringed around with bold danger, calls for noble York and Somerset to beat back attacking death from his weak troops. And while that honourable captain drips bloody sweat from his war-weary limbs, holding out and looking for rescue, you, his false hopes, the trustees of England's honour, keep your distance in worthless rivalry. Don't let your private quarrel keep away the reinforcements raised to help him, while he, a famous, noble gentleman, gives up his life against overwhelming odds. The Bastard of Orleans, Charles, Burgundy, Alencon and Reignier surround him, and Talbot dies through your failure."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["York set him on; York should have sent him aid."],"id":630,"m":"York sent him into it; York should have sent him help."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCY","lines":["And York as fast upon your grace exclaims;","Swearing that you withhold his levied host,","Collected for this expedition."],"id":631,"m":"And York cries out just as hard against your Grace, swearing that you are holding back the forces he raised for this expedition."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["York lies; he might have sent and had the horse:","I owe him little duty, and less love;","And take foul scorn to fawn on him by sending."],"id":632,"m":"York lies. He could have sent for the cavalry and had it. I owe him little duty and less love, and I'd scorn to fawn on him by sending it unasked."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCY","lines":["The fraud of England, not the force of France,","Hath now entrapp'd the noble-minded Talbot:","Never to England shall he bear his life;","But dies, betray'd to fortune by your strife."],"id":633,"m":"English deceit, not French strength, has now trapped noble-minded Talbot. He'll never bring his life back to England, but dies, betrayed to fortune by your quarrel."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["Come, go; I will dispatch the horsemen straight:","Within six hours they will be at his aid."],"id":634,"m":"Come, go; I'll send the cavalry at once. They'll reach him within six hours."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCY","lines":["Too late comes rescue; he is ta'en or slain;","For fly he could not, if he would have fled;","And fly would Talbot never, though he might."],"id":635,"m":"Help comes too late. He's captured or killed; for he couldn't have run if he'd wanted to, and Talbot would never run even if he could."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOMERSET","lines":["If he be dead, brave Talbot, then adieu!"],"id":636,"m":"If he's dead, then farewell, brave Talbot!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCY","lines":["His fame lives in the world, his shame in you."],"id":637,"m":"His fame lives on in the world, his shame in you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":638},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"V","loc":"The English camp near Bordeaux.","id":639},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Talbot and John his son.","id":640},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["O young John Talbot! I did send for thee","To tutor thee in stratagems of war,","That Talbot's name might be in thee revived","When sapless age and weak unable limbs","Should bring thy father to his drooping chair.","But, O malignant and ill-boding stars!","Now thou art come unto a feast of death,","A terrible and unavoided danger:","Therefore, dear boy, mount on my swiftest horse;","And I'll direct thee how thou shalt escape","By sudden flight: come, dally not, be gone."],"id":641,"m":"Oh, young John Talbot! I sent for you to teach you the tactics of war, so that Talbot's name might live again in you when dried-up old age and weak, useless limbs bring your father to his invalid chair. But oh, evil and ill-omened stars! Now you have come to a feast of death, a terrible and unavoidable danger. So, dear boy, get on my fastest horse, and I'll show you how to escape by fleeing at once. Come, don't delay; go."},{"t":"sp","who":"JOHN TALBOT","lines":["Is my name Talbot? and am I your son?","And shall I fly? O, if you love my mother,","Dishonor not her honorable name,","To make a bastard and a slave of me!","The world will say, he is not Talbot's blood,","That basely fled when noble Talbot stood."],"id":642,"m":"Is my name Talbot? And am I your son? And should I run away? Oh, if you love my mother, don't dishonour her good name by making me a bastard and a coward! The world will say he isn't Talbot's flesh and blood who ran away like a coward when noble Talbot stood his ground."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Fly, to revenge my death, if I be slain."],"id":643,"m":"Run, to avenge my death if I am killed."},{"t":"sp","who":"JOHN TALBOT","lines":["He that flies so will ne'er return again."],"id":644,"m":"Whoever runs like that will never come back."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["If we both stay, we both are sure to die."],"id":645,"m":"If we both stay, we're both sure to die."},{"t":"sp","who":"JOHN TALBOT","lines":["Then let me stay; and, father, do you fly;","Your loss is great, so your regard should be;","My worth unknown, no loss is known in me.","Upon my death the French can little boast;","In yours they will, in you all hopes are lost.","Flight cannot stain the honor you have won;","But mine it will, that no exploit have done;","You fled for vantage, every one will swear;","But, if I bow, they'll say it was for fear.","There is no hope that ever I will stay,","If the first hour I shrink and run away.","Here on my knee I beg mortality,","Rather than life preserved with infamy."],"id":646,"m":"Then let me stay, and you run, father. Your loss is great, so you should look after yourself; my worth is unknown, so no loss will be felt in me. The French can't boast much about my death; they will about yours, for all hopes rest in you. Running away can't stain the honour you have won, but it will stain mine, since I've done no great deed. Everyone will swear you ran for good tactical reasons; but if I give way, they'll say it was from fear. There's no hope that I'll ever stand firm if I shrink and run away in my first hour. Here on my knee I beg for death rather than life saved with disgrace."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Shall all thy mother's hopes lie in one tomb?"],"id":647,"m":"Are all your mother's hopes to lie in one tomb?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JOHN TALBOT","lines":["Aye, rather than I'll shame my mother's womb."],"id":648,"m":"Yes, rather than shame my mother's womb."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Upon my blessing, I command thee go."],"id":649,"m":"On my blessing, I order you to go."},{"t":"sp","who":"JOHN TALBOT","lines":["To fight I will, but not to fly the foe."],"id":650,"m":"I'll go to fight, but not to run from the enemy."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Part of thy father may be saved in thee."],"id":651,"m":"Part of your father may be saved in you."},{"t":"sp","who":"JOHN TALBOT","lines":["No part of him but will be shame in me."],"id":652,"m":"No part of him that won't be shame in me."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Thou never hadst renown, nor canst not lose it."],"id":653,"m":"You never had fame, so you can't lose it."},{"t":"sp","who":"JOHN TALBOT","lines":["Yes, your renowned name: shall flight abuse it?"],"id":654,"m":"Yes, your famous name. Shall running away disgrace it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Thy father's charge shall clear thee from that stain."],"id":655,"m":"Your father's order will clear you of that stain."},{"t":"sp","who":"JOHN TALBOT","lines":["You cannot witness for me, being slain.","If death be so apparent, then both fly."],"id":656,"m":"You can't speak up for me once you're dead. If death is so certain, then let's both run."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["And leave my followers here to fight and die;","My age was never tainted with such shame."],"id":657,"m":"And leave my followers here to fight and die? My old age was never stained with such shame."},{"t":"sp","who":"JOHN TALBOT","lines":["And shall my youth be guilty of such blame?","No more can I be sever'd from your side,","Than can yourself yourself in twain divide:","Stay, go, do what you will, the like do I;","For live I will not, if my father die."],"id":658,"m":"And shall my youth be guilty of such blame? I can no more be separated from your side than you can split yourself in two. Stay, go, do what you like, I'll do the same; for I won't live if my father dies."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Then here I take my leave of thee, fair son,","Born to eclipse thy life this afternoon.","Come, side by side together live and die;","And soul with soul from France to heaven fly."],"id":659,"m":"Then here I say goodbye to you, fair son, born to have your life put out this afternoon. Come, side by side let's live and die together, and fly soul with soul from France to heaven."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":660},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"VI","loc":"A field of battle.","id":661},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum: excursions, wherein Talbot's Son is hemmed about, and Talbot rescues him.","id":662},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Saint George and victory; fight, soldiers, fight:","The regent hath with Talbot broke his word,","And left us to the rage of France his sword.","Where is John Talbot? Pause, and take thy breath;","I gave thee life and rescued thee from death."],"id":663,"m":"Saint George and victory! Fight, soldiers, fight! The Regent has broken his word to Talbot and left us to the fury of France's sword. Where is John Talbot? Stop and get your breath. I gave you life, and now I've rescued you from death."},{"t":"sp","who":"JOHN TALBOT","lines":["O, twice my father, twice am I thy son!","The life thou gavest me first was lost and done,","Till with thy warlike sword, despite of fate,","To my determined time thou gavest new date."],"id":664,"m":"Oh, twice my father, twice I am your son! The life you first gave me was lost and finished, until, in spite of fate, your warlike sword gave my appointed time a new date."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["When from the Dauphin's crest thy sword struck fire,","It warm'd thy father's heart with proud desire","Of bold-faced victory. Then leaden age,","Quicken'd with youthful spleen and warlike rage,","Beat down Alencon, Orleans, Burgundy,","And from the pride of Gallia rescued thee.","The ireful bastard Orleans, that drew blood","From thee, my boy, and had the maidenhood","Of thy first fight, I soon encountered,","And interchanging blows I quickly shed","Some of his bastard blood; and in disgrace","Bespoke him thus; 'Contaminated base","And misbegotten blood I spill of thine,","Mean and right poor, for that pure blood of mine,","Which thou didst force from Talbot, my brave boy:'","Here, purposing the Bastard to destroy,","Came in strong rescue. Speak, thy father's care,","Art thou not weary, John? how dost thou fare?","Wilt thou yet leave the battle, boy, and fly,","Now thou art seal'd the son of chivalry?","Fly, to revenge my death when I am dead:","The help of one stands me in little stead.","O, too much folly is it, well I wot,","To hazard all our lives in one small boat!","If I to-day die not with Frenchmen's rage,","To-morrow I shall die with mickle age:","By me they nothing gain an if I stay;","'Tis but the short'ning of my life one day:","In thee thy mother dies, our household's name,","My death's revenge, thy youth, and England's fame:","All these and more we hazard by thy stay;","All these are saved if thou wilt fly away."],"id":665,"m":"When your sword struck sparks from the Dauphin's helmet, it warmed your father's heart with proud desire for bold victory. Then my heavy old age, quickened with young fire and warlike rage, beat down Alencon, Orleans and Burgundy and rescued you from the pride of France. The angry Bastard of Orleans, who drew blood from you, my boy, and had the first taste of your first fight, I soon met, and exchanging blows I quickly spilled some of his bastard blood, and insulted him like this: \"I spill your tainted, low, illegitimate blood, mean and worthless, in return for my pure blood, which you forced from Talbot, my brave boy.\" Here, as I meant to kill the Bastard, strong help came to rescue him. Speak, you who are your father's worry: aren't you tired, John? How are you? Will you leave the battle now, boy, and run, now that you're confirmed as the son of knighthood? Run, to avenge my death when I am dead. The help of one man is little use to me. Oh, I know well it's too foolish to risk all our lives in one small boat! If I don't die today from French rage, I'll die tomorrow from great age. They gain nothing if I stay; it only shortens my life by a day. In you die your mother, our family's name, the revenge for my death, your youth and England's fame. We risk all these and more if you stay; all these are saved if you'll run away."},{"t":"sp","who":"JOHN TALBOT","lines":["The sword of Orleans hath not made me smart;","These words of yours draw life-blood from my heart:","On that advantage, bought with such a shame,","To save a paltry life and slay bright fame,","Before young Talbot from old Talbot fly,","The coward horse that bears me fall and die!","And like me to the peasant boys of France,","To be shame's scorn and subject of mischance!","Surely, by all the glory you have won,","An if I fly, I am not Talbot's son;","Then talk no more of flight, it is no boot;","If son to Talbot, die at Talbot's foot."],"id":666,"m":"The sword of Orleans didn't make me suffer; these words of yours draw blood from my heart. Rather than young Talbot run from old Talbot for that advantage, bought with such shame, to save a worthless life and kill bright fame, may the cowardly horse that carries me fall and die! And may I be compared to the peasant boys of France, mocked by shame and a target for bad luck! Surely, by all the glory you have won, if I run, I'm not Talbot's son. So say no more about running; it's no use. If I'm Talbot's son, I'll die at Talbot's feet."},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Then follow thou thy desperate sire of Crete,","Thou Icarus; thy life to me is sweet:","If thou wilt fight, fight by thy father's side;","And, commendable proved, let 's die in pride."],"id":667,"m":"Then follow your desperate father of Crete, you Icarus. Your life is sweet to me. If you will fight, fight by your father's side, and having proved our worth, let's die in pride."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":668},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"VII","loc":"Another part of the field.","id":669},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum: excursions. Enter old Talbot led by a Servant.","id":670},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Where is my other life? mine own is gone;","O, where's young Talbot? where is valiant John?","Triumphant death, smear'd with captivity,","Young Talbot's valor makes me smile at thee:","When he perceived me shrink and on my knee,","His bloody sword he brandish'd over me,","And, like a hungry lion, did commence","Rough deeds of rage and stern impatience;","But when my angry guardant stood alone,","Tendering my ruin and assail'd of none,","Dizzy-ey'd fury and great rage of heart","Suddenly made him from my side to start","Into the clustering battle of the French;","And in that sea of blood my boy did drench","His over-mounting spirit, and there died,","My Icarus, my blossom, in his pride."],"id":671,"m":"Where is my other life? My own is gone. Oh, where's young Talbot? Where is brave John? Triumphant Death, stained with my captivity, young Talbot's courage makes me smile at you. When he saw me sink to my knee, he waved his bloody sword over me and, like a hungry lion, began rough deeds of rage and fierce impatience. But when my angry guardian stood alone, watching over my fall and attacked by no one, dizzy fury and great rage of heart suddenly made him leap from my side into the crowded ranks of the French. And in that sea of blood my boy drowned his soaring spirit, and there died, my Icarus, my blossom, in his pride."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["O my dear lord, lo where your son is borne!"],"id":672,"m":"Oh, my dear lord, look, here your son is carried!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter soldiers, with the body of young Talbot.","id":673},{"t":"sp","who":"TALBOT","lines":["Thou antic Death, which laugh'st us here to scorn,","Anon, from thy insulting tyranny,","Coupled in bonds of perpetuity,","Two Talbots, winged through the lither sky,","In thy despite shall 'scape mortality.","O thou, whose wounds become hard-favor'd death,","Speak to thy father ere thou yield thy breath!","Brave death by speaking, whether he will or no;","Imagine him a Frenchman and thy foe.","Poor boy! he smiles, methinks, as who should say,","Had death been French, then death had died to-day.","Come, come and lay him in his father's arms:","My spirit can no longer bear these harms.","Soldiers, adieu! I have what I would have,","Now my old arms are young John Talbot's grave."],"id":674,"m":"You grinning clown Death, who laughs at us here in scorn, soon, coupled in everlasting bonds, two Talbots, flying through the yielding sky, will escape from your insulting tyranny and from death, in spite of you. Oh you, whose wounds make ugly death look handsome, speak to your father before you breathe your last! Defy death by speaking, whether he likes it or not; imagine he's a Frenchman and your enemy. Poor boy! I think he smiles, as if to say that if death had been French, death would have died today. Come, come and lay him in his father's arms. My spirit can't bear these hurts any longer. Soldiers, goodbye! I have what I wanted: now my old arms are young John Talbot's grave."},{"t":"sd","text":"Dies.","id":675},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Charles, Alencon, Burgundy, Bastard, La Pucelle, and forces.","id":676},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Had York and Somerset brought rescue in,","We should have found a bloody day of this."],"id":677,"m":"If York and Somerset had brought reinforcements, this would have been a bloody day for us."},{"t":"sp","who":"BASTARD","lines":["How the young whelp of Talbot's, raging-wood,","Did flesh his puny sword in Frenchmen's blood!"],"id":678,"m":"How Talbot's young cub, raging mad, gave his little sword its first taste of French blood!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Once I encounter'd him, and thus I said:","'Thou maiden youth, be vanquish'd by a maid.'","But, with a proud majestical high scorn,","He answer'd thus: 'Young Talbot was not born","To be the pillage of a giglot wench:'","So, rushing in the bowels of the French,","He left me proudly, as unworthy fight."],"id":679,"m":"Once I met him, and I said: \"You virgin youth, be beaten by a virgin.\" But with proud, majestic, lofty scorn he answered: \"Young Talbot wasn't born to be the spoil of a loose wench.\" So, rushing into the thick of the French, he proudly left me, as not worth fighting."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["Doubtless he would have made a noble knight:","See, where he lies inhearsed in the arms","Of the most bloody nurser of his harms!"],"id":680,"m":"No doubt he would have made a noble knight. Look how he lies coffined in the arms of the man who bloodily bred his ruin!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BASTARD","lines":["Hew them to pieces, hack their bones asunder,","Whose life was England's glory, Gallia's wonder."],"id":681,"m":"Hack them to pieces, chop their bones apart, whose lives were England's glory and France's wonder."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["O, no, forbear! for that which we have fled","During the life, let us not wrong it dead."],"id":682,"m":"Oh no, stop! What we ran from while they lived, let's not wrong now they're dead."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Sir William Lucy, attended; Herald of the French preceding.","id":683},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCY","lines":["Herald, conduct me to the Dauphin's tent,","To know who hath obtain'd the glory of the day."],"id":684,"m":"Herald, take me to the Dauphin's tent to find out who has won the glory of the day."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["On what submissive message art thou sent?"],"id":685,"m":"What message of surrender have you been sent with?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCY","lines":["Submission, Dauphin! 'tis a mere French word;","We English warriors wot not what it means.","I come to know what prisoners thou hast ta'en,","And to survey the bodies of the dead."],"id":686,"m":"Surrender, Dauphin! That's purely a French word; we English soldiers don't know what it means. I've come to find out what prisoners you have taken and to look at the bodies of the dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["For prisoners ask'st thou? hell our prison is.","But tell me whom thou seek'st."],"id":687,"m":"You're asking about prisoners? Hell is our prison. But tell me whom you're looking for."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCY","lines":["But where's the great Alcides of the field,","Valiant Lord Talbot, Earl of Shrewsbury,","Created for his rare success in arms,","Great Earl of Washford, Waterford, and Valence;","Lord Talbot of Goodrig and Urchinfield,","Lord Strange of Blackmere, Lord Verdun of Alton,","Lord Cromwell of Wingfield, Lord Furnival of Sheffield,","The thrice-victorious Lord of Falconbridge;","Knight of the noble order of Saint George,","Worthy Saint Michael, and the Golden Fleece;","Great marshal to Henry the Sixth","Of all his wars within the realm of France?"],"id":688,"m":"Where's the great Hercules of the battlefield, brave Lord Talbot, Earl of Shrewsbury, made great Earl of Wexford, Waterford and Valence for his outstanding success in war; Lord Talbot of Goodrich and Urchinfield, Lord Strange of Blackmere, Lord Verdun of Alton, Lord Cromwell of Wingfield, Lord Furnival of Sheffield, the three-times victorious Lord of Falconbridge; Knight of the noble Order of Saint George, worthy Saint Michael and the Golden Fleece; great Marshal to Henry the Sixth in all his wars in France?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Here's a silly stately style indeed!","The Turk, that two and fifty kingdoms hath,","Writes not so tedious a style as this.","Him that thou magnifiest with all these titles","Stinking and fly-blown lies here at our feet."],"id":689,"m":"That's a ridiculously grand list of titles! The Sultan of Turkey, who has fifty-two kingdoms, doesn't write such a long-winded list. The man you glorify with all these titles lies here at our feet, stinking and covered in flies."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCY","lines":["Is Talbot slain, the Frenchman's only scourge,","Your kingdom's terror and black Nemesis?","O, were mine eye-balls into bullets turn'd,","That I in rage might shoot them at your faces!","O, that I could but can these dead to life!","It were enough to fright the realm of France:","Were but his picture left amongst you here,","It would amaze the proudest of you all.","Give me their bodies, that I may bear them hence","And give them burial as beseems their worth."],"id":690,"m":"Is Talbot killed, the only scourge of the French, your kingdom's terror and dark goddess of vengeance? Oh, if only my eyeballs were turned into bullets so that I could shoot them in rage at your faces! Oh, if only I could call these dead back to life! It would be enough to frighten the whole of France. If only his portrait were left here among you, it would terrify the proudest of you. Give me their bodies, so I can carry them away and bury them as their worth deserves."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["I think this upstart is old Talbot's ghost,","He speaks with such a proud commanding spirit,","For God's sake, let him have 'em; to keep them here,","They would but stink, and putrify the air."],"id":691,"m":"I think this upstart is old Talbot's ghost, he speaks with such a proud, commanding spirit. For God's sake, let him have them; if we keep them here, they'd only stink and rot the air."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Go, take their bodies hence."],"id":692,"m":"Go, take their bodies away."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCY","lines":["I'll bear them hence; but from their ashes shall be","rear'd","A phoenix that shall make all France afeard."],"id":693,"m":"I'll carry them away; but from their ashes a phoenix will rise that will make all France afraid."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["So we be rid of them, do with 'em what thou wilt.","And now to Paris, in this conquering vein:","All will be ours, now bloody Talbot's slain."],"id":694,"m":"As long as we're rid of them, do what you like with them. And now to Paris, while we're on a winning streak. Everything will be ours now bloody Talbot is dead."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":695},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"London. The palace.","id":696},{"t":"sd","text":"Sennet. Enter King, Gloucester, and Exeter.","id":697},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Have you perused the letters from the pope,","The emperor, and the Earl of Armagnac?"],"id":698,"m":"Have you read the letters from the Pope, the Emperor and the Earl of Armagnac?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["I have, my lord: and their intent is this:","They humbly sue unto your excellence","To have a godly peace concluded of","Between the realms of England and of France."],"id":699,"m":"I have, my lord, and this is what they want: they humbly ask your Excellency to agree a holy peace between the kingdoms of England and France."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["How doth your grace affect their motion?"],"id":700,"m":"What does your Grace think of their proposal?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Well, my good lord; and as the only means","To stop effusion of our Christian blood","And stablish quietness on every side."],"id":701,"m":"I like it, my good lord, as the only way to stop the spilling of Christian blood and bring peace on every side."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Aye, marry, uncle; for I always thought","It was both impious and unnatural","That such immanity and bloody strife","Should reign among professors of one faith."],"id":702,"m":"Yes indeed, uncle; for I always thought it was both wicked and unnatural that such savagery and bloody fighting should rule among people of the same faith."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Beside, my lord, the sooner to effect","And surer bind this knot of amity,","The Earl of Armagnac, near knit to Charles,","A man of great authority in France,","Proffers his only daughter to your grace","In marriage, with a large and sumptuous dowry."],"id":703,"m":"Besides, my lord, to bring about this bond of friendship faster and tie it more securely, the Earl of Armagnac, closely related to Charles and a man of great power in France, offers your Grace his only daughter in marriage, with a large and rich dowry."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Marriage, uncle! alas, my years are young!","And fitter is my study and my books","Than wanton dalliance with a paramour.","Yet call the ambassadors; and, as you please,","So let them have their answers every one:","I shall be well content with any choice","Tends to God's glory and my country's weal."],"id":704,"m":"Marriage, uncle! Alas, I'm young! My studies and my books suit me better than playful flirting with a sweetheart. Still, call the ambassadors and give them each whatever answer you think best. I'll be happy with any choice that serves God's glory and my country's good."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Winchester in Cardinal's habit, a Legate and two Ambassadors.","id":705},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["What! is my Lord of Winchester install'd","And call'd unto a cardinal's degree?","Then I perceive that will be verified","Henry the Fifth did sometime prophesy,","'If once he come to be a cardinal,","He'll make his cap co-equal with the crown.'"],"id":706,"m":"What! Has my Lord of Winchester been installed and promoted to cardinal? Then I see what Henry the Fifth once prophesied will come true: \"If he ever becomes a cardinal, he'll make his hat equal to the crown.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["My lords ambassadors, your several suits","Have been consider'd and debated on.","Your purpose is both good and reasonable;","And therefore are we certainly resolved","To draw conditions of a friendly peace;","Which by my Lord of Winchester we mean","Shall be transported presently to France."],"id":707,"m":"My lords ambassadors, your various requests have been considered and debated. Your aim is both good and reasonable, so we have firmly decided to draw up terms for a friendly peace, which we intend my Lord of Winchester to take to France at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["And for the proffer of my lord your master,","I have inform'd his highness so at large,","As liking of the lady's virtuous gifts,","Her beauty and the value of her dower,","He doth intend she shall be England's Queen."],"id":708,"m":"And as for your master's offer, I have told his Highness about it in full, and liking the lady's virtues, her beauty and the value of her dowry, he intends her to be England's Queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["In argument and proof of which contract,","Bear her this jewel, pledge of my affection.","And so, my lord protector, see them guarded","And safely brought to Dover; where inshipp'd,","Commit them to the fortune of the sea."],"id":709,"m":"As a sign and proof of this agreement, take her this jewel, a pledge of my love. And so, Lord Protector, see them escorted and brought safely to Dover, and there, once they've boarded, trust them to the fortune of the sea."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Winchester and Legate.","id":710},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["Stay my lord legate: you shall first receive","The sum of money which I promised","Should be deliver'd to his holiness","For clothing me in these grave ornaments."],"id":711,"m":"Wait, my Lord Legate. First you'll receive the sum of money I promised to deliver to his Holiness for dressing me in these solemn robes."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEGATE","lines":["I will attend upon your lordship's leisure."],"id":712,"m":"I'll wait until your lordship is ready."},{"t":"sp","who":"WINCHESTER","lines":["[Aside] Now Winchester will not submit, I trow,","Or be inferior to the proudest peer.","Humphrey of Gloucester, thou shalt well perceive","That neither in birth or for authority,","The bishop will be overborne by thee:","I'll either make thee stoop and bend thy knee,","Or sack this country with a mutiny."],"id":713,"m":"(Aside) Now I'm sure Winchester won't submit to or be lower than the proudest lord. Humphrey of Gloucester, you'll see clearly that the bishop won't be overpowered by you, either in birth or in authority. I'll either make you bow and bend your knee or wreck this country with a rebellion."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":714},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"France. Plains in Anjou.","id":715},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Charles, Burgundy, Alencon, Bastard, Reignier, La Pucelle, and forces.","id":716},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["These news, my lords, may cheer our drooping spirits:","'Tis said the stout Parisians do revolt","And turn again unto the warlike French."],"id":717,"m":"This news, my lords, may cheer up our drooping spirits: they say the bold Parisians are rebelling and coming back to the warlike French."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALENCON","lines":["Then march to Paris, royal Charles of France,","And keep not back your powers in dalliance."],"id":718,"m":"Then march to Paris, royal Charles of France, and don't hold your forces back in idleness."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Peace be amongst them, if they turn to us;","Else, ruin combat with their palaces!"],"id":719,"m":"Peace be with them, if they come back to us; otherwise, may ruin fight with their palaces!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Scout.","id":720},{"t":"sp","who":"SCOUT","lines":["Success unto our valiant general,","And happiness to his accomplices!"],"id":721,"m":"Success to our brave general, and happiness to his allies!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["What tidings send our scouts? I prithee, speak."],"id":722,"m":"What news do our scouts send? Please speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"SCOUT","lines":["The English army, that divided was","Into two parties, is now conjoin'd in one,","And means to give you battle presently."],"id":723,"m":"The English army, which was divided into two parts, has now joined into one and means to give you battle at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Somewhat too sudden, sirs, the warning is;","But we will presently provide for them."],"id":724,"m":"The warning is rather sudden, sirs, but we'll get ready for them at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"BURGUNDY","lines":["I trust the ghost of Talbot is not there:","Now he is gone, my lord, you need not fear."],"id":725,"m":"I'm sure the ghost of Talbot isn't there. Now he's gone, my lord, you needn't be afraid."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Of all base passions, fear is most accursed.","Command the conquest, Charles, it shall be thine,","Let Henry fret and all the world repine."],"id":726,"m":"Of all low feelings, fear is the most cursed. Order the victory, Charles, and it will be yours. Let Henry fret and the whole world complain."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Then on, my lords; and France be fortunate!"],"id":727,"m":"Then forward, my lords, and good fortune to France!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":728},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"III","loc":"Before Angiers.","id":729},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Excursions. Enter La Pucelle.","id":730},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["The regent conquers, and the Frenchmen fly.","Now help, ye charming spells and periapts;","And ye choice spirits that admonish me,","And give me signs of future accidents. [Thunder]","You speedy helpers, that are substitutes","Under the lordly monarch of the north,","Appear and aid me in this enterprise."],"id":731,"m":"The Regent is winning, and the French are running away. Now help me, you magic spells and charms, and you chosen spirits who advise me and give me signs of future events! (Thunder) You quick helpers who serve under the lordly king of the north, appear and help me in this enterprise."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Fiends.","id":732},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","cont":1,"lines":["This speedy and quick appearance argues proof","Of your accustom'd diligence to me.","Now, ye familiar spirits, that are cull'd","Out of the powerful regions under earth,","Help me this once, that France may get the field."],"id":733,"m":"This quick appearance proves your usual devotion to me. Now, you familiar spirits picked from the powerful regions under the earth, help me this once so that France may win the battle."},{"t":"sd","text":"They walk and speak not.","id":734},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","cont":1,"lines":["O, hold me not with silence over-long!","Where I was wont to feed you with my blood,","I'll lop a member off and give it you","In earnest of a further benefit,","So you do condescend to help me now."],"id":735,"m":"Oh, don't keep me waiting in silence too long! Where I used to feed you with my blood, I'll cut off a limb and give it to you as a down payment on a further reward, if you'll agree to help me now."},{"t":"sd","text":"They hang their heads.","id":736},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","cont":1,"lines":["No hope to have redress? My body shall","Pay recompense, if you will grant my suit."],"id":737,"m":"No hope of help? My body will pay you, if you'll grant my request."},{"t":"sd","text":"They shake their heads.","id":738},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","cont":1,"lines":["Cannot my body nor blood-sacrifice","Entreat you to your wonted furtherance?","Then take my soul, my body, soul and all,","Before that England give the French the foil."],"id":739,"m":"Can't my body or my blood sacrifice persuade you to help me as usual? Then take my soul, my body, soul and all, before England defeats the French."},{"t":"sd","text":"They depart.","id":740},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","cont":1,"lines":["See, they forsake me! Now the time is come","That France must vail her lofty-plumed crest,","And let her head fall into England's lap.","My ancient incantations are too weak,","And hell too strong for me to buckle with:","Now, France, thy glory droopeth to the dust."],"id":741,"m":"Look, they're abandoning me! Now the time has come when France must lower her proud, plumed helmet and let her head fall into England's lap. My old spells are too weak, and hell is too strong for me to wrestle with. Now, France, your glory droops into the dust."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":742},{"t":"sd","text":"Excursions. Re-enter La Pucelle fighting hand to hand with York: La Pucelle is taken. The French fly.","id":743},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Damsel of France, I think I have you fast:","Unchain your spirits now with spelling charms,","And try if they can gain your liberty.","A goodly prize, fit for the devil's grace!","See, how the ugly witch doth bend her brows,","As if with Circe she would change my shape!"],"id":744,"m":"Girl of France, I think I've got you tight. Now unleash your spirits with your spells and see if they can win your freedom. A fine prize, fit for the devil's grace! See how the ugly witch frowns, as if she would change my shape like Circe!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Chang'd to a worser shape thou canst not be."],"id":745,"m":"You can't be changed into a worse shape."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["O, Charles the Dauphin is a proper man;","No shape but his can please your dainty eye."],"id":746,"m":"Oh, Charles the Dauphin is a handsome man; no shape but his can please your fussy eye."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["A plaguing mischief light on Charles and thee!","And may ye both be suddenly surprised","By bloody hands, in sleeping on your beds!"],"id":747,"m":"May a plague of trouble fall on Charles and you! And may you both be surprised by murderers while you sleep in your beds!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Fell banning hag; enchantress, hold thy tongue!"],"id":748,"m":"Cruel cursing hag! Witch, hold your tongue!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["I prithee, give me leave to curse awhile."],"id":749,"m":"Please let me curse a while."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Curse, miscreant, when thou comest to the stake."],"id":750,"m":"Curse, villain, when you come to the stake."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":751},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Enter Suffolk, with Margaret in his hand.","id":752},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Be what thou wilt, thou art my prisoner."],"id":753,"m":"Be who you like, you are my prisoner."},{"t":"sd","text":"Gazes on her.","id":754},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","cont":1,"lines":["O fairest beauty, do not fear nor fly!","For I will touch thee but with reverent hands;","I kiss these fingers for eternal peace,","And lay them gently on thy tender side.","Who art thou? say, that I may honor thee."],"id":755,"m":"Oh, fairest beauty, don't be afraid or run away! I'll only touch you with respectful hands. I kiss these fingers as a sign of everlasting peace and lay them gently at your side. Who are you? Tell me, so I can honour you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Margaret my name, and daughter to a king,","The King of Naples, whosoe'er thou art."],"id":756,"m":"My name is Margaret, and I'm a king's daughter, the King of Naples, whoever you are."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["An earl I am, and Suffolk am I call'd.","Be not offended, nature's miracle,","Thou art allotted to be ta'en by me.","So doth the swan her downy cygnets save,","Keeping them prisoner underneath her wings.","Yet, if this servile usage once offend,","Go and be free again as Suffolk's friend."],"id":757,"m":"I am an earl, and my name is Suffolk. Don't be offended, miracle of nature; it's your lot to be taken by me. That's how the swan protects her fluffy cygnets, keeping them prisoner under her wings. Still, if this treatment as a captive offends you, go and be free again as Suffolk's friend."},{"t":"sd","text":"She is going.","id":758},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","cont":1,"lines":["O, stay! I have no power to let her pass;","My hand would free her, but my heart says no.","As plays the sun upon the glassy streams,","Twinkling another counterfeited beam,","So seems this gorgeous beauty to mine eyes.","Fain would I woo her, yet I dare not speak:","I'll call for pen and ink, and write my mind.","Fie, de la Pole! disable not thyself;","Hast not a tongue? is she not here?","Wilt thou be daunted at a woman's sight?","Aye, beauty's princely majesty is such,","Confounds the tongue and makes the senses rough."],"id":759,"m":"Oh, stay! I don't have the power to let her go. My hand would free her, but my heart says no. As the sun plays on glassy streams, sparkling with a second, reflected beam, so this gorgeous beauty seems to my eyes. I'd like to woo her, but I don't dare speak. I'll call for pen and ink and write my thoughts. Shame, de la Pole! Don't belittle yourself. Haven't you got a tongue? Isn't she here? Will you be scared by the sight of a woman? Yes, beauty's princely majesty is such that it ties the tongue and dulls the senses."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Say, Earl of Suffolk,—if thy name be so—","What ransom must I pay before I pass?","For I perceive I am thy prisoner."],"id":760,"m":"Tell me, Earl of Suffolk, if that's your name, what ransom must I pay before I can go? For I see I am your prisoner."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["How canst thou tell she will deny thy suit,","Before thou make a trial of her love?"],"id":761,"m":"(Aside) How can you tell she'll reject your suit before you've tested her love?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Why speak'st thou not? what ransom must I pay?"],"id":762,"m":"Why don't you speak? What ransom must I pay?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["She's beautiful and therefore to be woo'd;","She is a woman, therefore to be won."],"id":763,"m":"(Aside) She's beautiful, and so she should be wooed; she's a woman, and so she can be won."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Wilt thou accept of ransom? yea, or no."],"id":764,"m":"Will you accept a ransom? Yes or no?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Fond man, remember that thou hast a wife;","Then how can Margaret be thy paramour?"],"id":765,"m":"(Aside) Foolish man, remember you have a wife. So how can Margaret be your lover?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["I were best leave him, for he will not hear."],"id":766,"m":"I'd better leave him, for he won't listen."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["There all is marr'd; there lies a cooling card."],"id":767,"m":"(Aside) That spoils everything; that's the card that cools my hopes."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["He talks at random; sure, the man is mad."],"id":768,"m":"He's rambling; the man must be mad."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["And yet a dispensation may be had."],"id":769,"m":"(Aside) And yet a special permission from the Church could be had."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["And yet I would that you would answer me."],"id":770,"m":"And yet I wish you'd answer me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["I'll win this Lady Margaret. For whom?","Why, for my king; tush, that 's a wooden thing!"],"id":771,"m":"(Aside) I'll win this Lady Margaret. For whom? Why, for my king. Pooh, that's a wooden idea!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["He talks of wood: it is some carpenter."],"id":772,"m":"He's talking about wood; he must be a carpenter."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Yet so my fancy may be satisfied,","And peace established between these realms.","But there remains a scruple in that too;","For though her father be the King of Naples,","Duke of Anjou and Maine, yet is he poor,","And our nobility will scorn the match."],"id":773,"m":"(Aside) Yet that way my desire may be satisfied and peace made between these kingdoms. But there's a problem with that too: though her father is King of Naples and Duke of Anjou and Maine, he's poor, and our nobles will scorn the match."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Hear ye, captain, are you not at leisure?"],"id":774,"m":"Listen, captain, aren't you free to talk?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["It shall be so, disdain they ne'er so much:","Henry is youthful and will quickly yield.","Madam, I have a secret to reveal."],"id":775,"m":"(Aside) It shall be so, however much they scorn it. Henry is young and will quickly give in. (To her) Madam, I have a secret to tell you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["What though I be enthrall'd? he seems a knight,","And will not any way dishonor me."],"id":776,"m":"(Aside) What if I am a prisoner? He seems to be a knight and won't dishonour me in any way."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Lady, vouchsafe to listen what I say."],"id":777,"m":"Lady, be good enough to listen to what I say."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Perhaps I shall be rescued by the French;","And then I need not crave his courtesy."],"id":778,"m":"(Aside) Perhaps the French will rescue me, and then I won't need to beg for his courtesy."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Sweet madam, give me hearing in a cause—"],"id":779,"m":"Sweet madam, hear me out on a matter—"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Tush! women have been captivate ere now."],"id":780,"m":"(Aside) Pooh! Women have been taken captive before now."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Lady, wherefore talk you so?"],"id":781,"m":"Lady, why do you talk like that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["I cry you mercy, 'tis but Quid for Quo."],"id":782,"m":"I beg your pardon; it's only tit for tat."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Say, gentle princess, would you not suppose","Your bondage happy, to be made a queen?"],"id":783,"m":"Tell me, gentle princess, wouldn't you think your captivity happy if it made you a queen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["To be a queen in bondage is more vile","Than is a slave in base servility;","For princes should be free."],"id":784,"m":"To be a queen in captivity is worse than to be a slave in low servitude, for royalty should be free."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["And so shall you,","If happy England's royal king be free."],"id":785,"m":"And so you shall be, if happy England's royal king is free."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Why, what concerns his freedom unto me?"],"id":786,"m":"Why, what has his freedom to do with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["I'll undertake to make thee Henry's queen,","To put a golden scepter in thy hand","And set a precious crown upon thy head,","If thou wilt condescend to be my—"],"id":787,"m":"I'll undertake to make you Henry's queen, to put a golden sceptre in your hand and set a precious crown on your head, if you'll agree to be my—"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["What?"],"id":788,"m":"What?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["His love."],"id":789,"m":"His love."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["I am unworthy to be Henry's wife."],"id":790,"m":"I'm not worthy to be Henry's wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["No, gentle madam; I unworthy am","To woo so fair a dame to be his wife,","And have no portion in the choice myself.","How say you, madam, are ye so content?"],"id":791,"m":"No, gentle madam; I'm unworthy to woo so fair a lady to be his wife and have no share in the choice myself. What do you say, madam? Do you agree?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["An if my father please, I am content."],"id":792,"m":"If my father agrees, I agree."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Then call our captain and our colors forth.","And, madam, at your father's castle walls","We'll crave a parley, to confer with him."],"id":793,"m":"Then call out our captains and our flags. And, madam, we'll ask for a parley at your father's castle walls to talk with him."},{"t":"sd","text":"A parley sounded. Enter Reignier on the walls.","id":794},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","cont":1,"lines":["See, Reignier, see, thy daughter prisoner!"],"id":795,"m":"Look, Reignier, look, your daughter is a prisoner!"},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["To whom?"],"id":796,"m":"Whose?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["To me."],"id":797,"m":"Mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["Suffolk, what remedy?","I am a soldier, and unapt to weep,","Or to exclaim on fortune's fickleness."],"id":798,"m":"Suffolk, what can be done? I'm a soldier and not inclined to weep or rail against fortune's fickleness."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Yes, there is remedy enough, my lord:","Consent, and for thy honor give consent,","Thy daughter shall be wedded to my king;","Whom I with pain have woo'd and won thereto;","And this her easy-held imprisonment","Hath gain'd thy daughter princely liberty."],"id":799,"m":"Yes, there's plenty to be done, my lord. Agree, and for your honour agree, that your daughter will marry my king, whom I've wooed and won to it with some effort; and this easy imprisonment of hers has won your daughter royal freedom."},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["Speaks Suffolk as he thinks?"],"id":800,"m":"Does Suffolk mean what he says?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Fair Margaret knows","That Suffolk doth not flatter, face, or feign."],"id":801,"m":"Fair Margaret knows that Suffolk doesn't flatter, pretend or lie."},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["Upon thy princely warrant, I descend","To give thee answer of thy just demand."],"id":802,"m":"On your princely word, I'll come down to give you an answer to your fair request."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit from the walls.","id":803},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["And here I will expect thy coming."],"id":804,"m":"And I'll wait here for you to come."},{"t":"sd","text":"Trumpets sound. Enter Reignier, below.","id":805},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["Welcome, brave earl, into our territories:","Command in Anjou what your honor pleases."],"id":806,"m":"Welcome, brave Earl, to our lands. Command whatever your lordship pleases in Anjou."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Thanks, Reignier, happy for so sweet a child,","Fit to be made companion with a king:","What answer makes your grace unto my suit?"],"id":807,"m":"Thank you, Reignier, lucky to have so sweet a child, fit to be a king's partner. What answer does your Grace give to my proposal?"},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["Since thou dost deign to woo her little worth","To be the princely bride of such a lord;","Upon condition I may quietly","Enjoy mine own, the country Maine and Anjou,","Free from oppression or the stroke of war,","My daughter shall be Henry's, if he please."],"id":808,"m":"Since you are good enough to woo her, of little worth as she is, to be the royal bride of such a lord: on condition that I may peacefully enjoy my own lands, the counties of Maine and Anjou, free from oppression and the blows of war, my daughter shall be Henry's, if he wishes."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["That is her ransom; I deliver her;","And those two counties I will undertake","Your Grace shall well and quietly enjoy."],"id":809,"m":"That's her ransom. I release her, and I promise your Grace that you'll enjoy those two counties fully and in peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["And I again, in Henry's royal name,","As deputy unto that gracious king,","Give thee her hand, for sign of plighted faith."],"id":810,"m":"And I in return, in Henry's royal name, as deputy for that gracious king, give you her hand as a sign of her promise."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Reignier of France, I give thee kingly thanks,","Because this is in traffic of a king.","[Aside] And yet, methinks, I could be well content","To be mine own attorney in this case.","I'll over then to England with this news,","And make this marriage to be solemnized.","So, farewell, Reignier; set this diamond safe","In golden palaces, as it becomes."],"id":811,"m":"Reignier of France, I give you royal thanks, since this is a king's business. (Aside) And yet I think I'd be very happy to act on my own behalf in this case. (Aloud) I'll go over to England with this news and arrange for this marriage to be celebrated. So goodbye, Reignier; keep this diamond safe in golden palaces, as it deserves."},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["I do embrace thee as I would embrace","The Christian prince, King Henry, were he here."],"id":812,"m":"I embrace you as I would embrace the Christian prince, King Henry, if he were here."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Farewell, my lord: good wishes, praise and prayers.","Shall Suffolk ever have of Margaret. [Going]"],"id":813,"m":"Goodbye, my lord. Suffolk will always have Margaret's good wishes, praise and prayers. (Going)"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Farewell, sweet madam: but hark you, Margaret;","No princely commendations to my king?"],"id":814,"m":"Goodbye, sweet madam. But listen, Margaret: no royal greetings for my king?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Such commendations as becomes a maid,","A virgin and his servant, say to him."],"id":815,"m":"Tell him such greetings as suit a girl, a virgin and his servant."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Words sweetly placed and modestly directed.","But, madam, I must trouble you again;","No loving token to his majesty?"],"id":816,"m":"Words sweetly put and modestly aimed. But, madam, I must trouble you again: no love token for his Majesty?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["Yes, my good lord, a pure unspotted heart,","Never yet taint with love, I send the king."],"id":817,"m":"Yes, my good lord: I send the King a pure, unspotted heart, never yet touched by love."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["And this withal. [Kisses her]"],"id":818,"m":"And this as well. (Kisses her)"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARET","lines":["That for thyself: I will not so presume","To send such peevish tokens to a king."],"id":819,"m":"That's for you. I won't be so bold as to send such silly tokens to a king."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Reignier and Margaret.","id":820},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["O, wert thou for myself! But, Suffolk, stay;","Thou mayst not wander in that labyrinth;","There Minotaurs and ugly treasons lurk.","Solicit Henry with her wondrous praise:","Bethink thee on her virtues that surmount,","And natural graces that extinguish art;","Repeat their semblance often on the seas,","That, when thou comest to kneel at Henry's feet,","Thou mayst bereave him of his wits with wonder."],"id":821,"m":"Oh, if only you were for me! But, Suffolk, stop: you mustn't wander into that maze. Monsters and ugly treasons lurk there. Woo Henry with wonderful praise of her. Think about her outstanding virtues and natural graces that put art to shame. Go over their image often on the voyage, so that when you come to kneel at Henry's feet, you can rob him of his wits with wonder."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":822},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"IV","loc":"Camp of the Duke of York in Anjou.","id":823},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter York, Warwick, and others.","id":824},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Bring forth that sorceress condemn'd to burn."],"id":825,"m":"Bring out that witch who is condemned to burn."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter La Pucelle, guarded, and a Shepherd.","id":826},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Ah, Joan, this kills thy father's heart outright!","Have I sought every country far and near,","And now it is my chance to find thee out,","Must I behold thy timeless cruel death?","Ah, Joan, sweet daughter Joan, I'll die with thee!"],"id":827,"m":"Ah, Joan, this breaks your father's heart completely! Have I searched every land far and near, and now that I happen to find you, must I see your early, cruel death? Ah, Joan, sweet daughter Joan, I'll die with you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Decrepit miser! base ignoble wretch!","I am descended of a gentler blood:","Thou art no father nor no friend of mine."],"id":828,"m":"Broken-down old wretch! Low, common creature! I'm descended from nobler blood. You are no father or friend of mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Out, out! My lords, as please you, 'tis not so;","I did beget her, all the parish knows.","Her mother liveth yet, can testify","She was the first fruit of my bachelorship."],"id":829,"m":"No, no! My lords, if you please, it isn't so. I fathered her; the whole parish knows it. Her mother is still alive and can testify she was my first child after I married."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Graceless! wilt thou deny thy parentage?"],"id":830,"m":"Shameless girl! Will you deny your parents?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["This argues what her kind of life hath been,","Wicked and vile; and so her death concludes."],"id":831,"m":"This shows what kind of life she's led, wicked and vile; and her death ends it the same way."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["Fie, Joan, that thou wilt be so obstacle!","God knows thou art a collop of my flesh;","And for thy sake have I shed many a tear:","Deny me not, I prithee, gentle Joan."],"id":832,"m":"Shame, Joan, that you are so stubborn! God knows you are a piece of my flesh, and I've shed many tears for you. Please don't deny me, gentle Joan."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Peasant, avaunt! You have suborn'd this man,","Of purpose to obscure my noble birth."],"id":833,"m":"Away, peasant! (To the English) You have bribed this man to hide my noble birth."},{"t":"sp","who":"SHEPHERD","lines":["'Tis true, I gave a noble to the priest","The morn that I was wedded to her mother.","Kneel down and take my blessing, good my girl.","Wilt thou not stoop? Now cursed be the time","Of thy nativity! I would the milk","Thy mother gave thee when thou suck'dst her breast,","Had been a little ratsbane for thy sake!","Or else, when thou didst keep my lambs a-field,","I wish some ravenous wolf had eaten thee!","Dost thou deny thy father, cursed drab?","O, burn her, burn her! hanging is too good."],"id":834,"m":"It's true, I gave the priest a coin the morning I married her mother. Kneel down and take my blessing, my good girl. Won't you bow? Then cursed be the hour you were born! I wish the milk your mother gave you when you sucked her breast had been rat poison, for your sake! Or else, when you looked after my lambs in the field, I wish some hungry wolf had eaten you! Do you deny your father, cursed slut? Oh, burn her, burn her! Hanging's too good for her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":835},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Take her away; for she hath lived too long,","To fill the world with vicious qualities."],"id":836,"m":"Take her away, for she has lived too long, filling the world with wickedness."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["First, let me tell you whom you have condemn'd:","Not me begotten of a shepherd swain,","But issued from the progeny of kings;","Virtuous and holy; chosen from above,","By inspiration of celestial grace,","To work exceeding miracles on earth.","I never had to do with wicked spirits:","But you, that are polluted with your lusts,","Stain'd with the guiltless blood of innocents,","Corrupt and tainted with a thousand vices,","Because you want the grace that others have,","You judge it straight a thing impossible","To compass wonders but by help of devils.","No, misconceived! Joan of Arc hath been","A virgin from her tender infancy,","Chaste and immaculate in very thought;","Whose maiden blood, thus rigorously effused,","Will cry for vengeance at the gates of heaven."],"id":837,"m":"First let me tell you whom you have condemned: not someone fathered by a shepherd peasant, but one descended from kings; virtuous and holy, chosen from above by the inspiration of heavenly grace to work great miracles on earth. I never had anything to do with evil spirits. But you, who are filthy with your lusts, stained with the blameless blood of innocent people, corrupt and infected with a thousand vices, because you lack the grace others have, immediately judge it impossible to perform wonders except with the help of devils. No, you are mistaken! Joan of Arc has been a virgin from her earliest childhood, chaste and pure even in thought, and her virgin blood, cruelly spilled like this, will cry out for vengeance at the gates of heaven."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Aye, aye: away with her to execution!"],"id":838,"m":"Yes, yes. Away with her to execution!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["And hark ye, sirs; because she is a maid,","Spare for no faggots, let there be enow:","Place barrels of pitch upon the fatal stake,","That so her torture may be shortened."],"id":839,"m":"And listen, men: since she's a virgin, don't hold back on the firewood; let there be plenty. Put barrels of tar on the fatal stake so that her suffering may be shortened."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Will nothing turn your unrelenting hearts?","Then, Joan, discover thine infirmity,","That warranteth by law to be thy privilege.","I am with child, ye bloody homicides:","Murder not then the fruit within my womb,","Although ye hale me to a violent death."],"id":840,"m":"Will nothing soften your merciless hearts? Then, Joan, reveal your condition, which by law gives you protection. I am pregnant, you bloody murderers. So don't murder the fruit in my womb, even if you drag me to a violent death."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Now heaven forfend! the holy maid with child!"],"id":841,"m":"Heaven forbid! The holy virgin pregnant!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["The greatest miracle that e'er ye wrought:","Is all your strict preciseness come to this?"],"id":842,"m":"The greatest miracle you ever performed! Has all your strict purity come to this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["She and the Dauphin have been juggling:","I did imagine what would be her refuge."],"id":843,"m":"She and the Dauphin have been up to tricks. I suspected what her last resort would be."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Well, go to; we'll have no bastards live;","Especially since Charles must father it."],"id":844,"m":"Well, come on; we won't let any bastards live, especially since Charles must be the father."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["You are deceived; my child is none of his:","It was Alencon that enjoy'd my love."],"id":845,"m":"You're wrong; my child isn't his. It was Alencon who enjoyed my love."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Alencon! that notorious Machiavel!","It dies, an if it had a thousand lives."],"id":846,"m":"Alencon! That notorious schemer! It dies, if it had a thousand lives."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["O, give me leave, I have deluded you:","'Twas neither Charles nor yet the duke I named,","But Reignier, king of Naples, that prevail'd."],"id":847,"m":"Oh, let me correct myself; I misled you. It was neither Charles nor the Duke I named, but Reignier, King of Naples, who won me."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["A married man! that's most intolerable."],"id":848,"m":"A married man! That's completely intolerable."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Why, here's a girl! I think she knows not well","There were so many, whom she may accuse."],"id":849,"m":"Why, what a girl! I don't think she knows for sure, there were so many she could accuse."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["It's sign she hath been liberal and free."],"id":850,"m":"It's a sign she's been generous and free with herself."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["And yet, forsooth, she is a virgin pure.","Strumpet, thy words condemn thy brat and thee:","Use no entreaty, for it is in vain."],"id":851,"m":"And yet, if you please, she's a pure virgin. Whore, your own words condemn your brat and you. Don't plead; it's useless."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUCELLE","lines":["Then lead me hence; with whom I leave my curse:","May never glorious sun reflex his beams","Upon the country where you make abode:","But darkness and the gloomy shade of death","Environ you, till mischief and despair","Drive you to break your necks or hang yourselves!"],"id":852,"m":"Then lead me away; I leave you my curse. May the glorious sun never shine its rays on the country where you live, but may darkness and the gloomy shadow of death surround you, until misfortune and despair drive you to break your necks or hang yourselves!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit, guarded.","id":853},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Break thou in pieces and consume to ashes,","Thou foul accursed minister of hell!"],"id":854,"m":"Break into pieces and burn to ashes, you foul, cursed servant of hell!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Cardinal Beaufort, Bishop of Winchester, attended.","id":855},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["Lord regent, I do greet your excellence","With letters of commission from the king.","For know, my lords, the states of Christendom,","Moved with remorse of these outrageous broils,","Have earnestly implored a general peace","Betwixt our nation and the aspiring French;","And here at hand the Dauphin and his train","Approacheth, to confer about some matter."],"id":856,"m":"Lord Regent, I greet your Excellency with a commission from the King. For you should know, my lords, that the states of Christendom, moved by pity for these outrageous wars, have earnestly begged for a general peace between our nation and the ambitious French; and the Dauphin and his followers are approaching close by to discuss terms."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Is all our travail turn'd to this effect?","After the slaughter of so many peers,","So many captains, gentlemen and soldiers,","That in this quarrel have been overthrown,","And sold their bodies for their country's benefit,","Shall we at last conclude effeminate peace?","Have we not lost most part of all the towns,","By treason, falsehood, and by treachery,","Our great progenitors had conquered?","O, Warwick, Warwick! I foresee with grief","The utter loss of all the realm of France."],"id":857,"m":"Has all our labour come to this? After the slaughter of so many lords, captains, gentlemen and soldiers, who have fallen in this quarrel and given their bodies for their country's good, are we going to agree to a weak, womanish peace at last? Haven't we lost most of the towns our great ancestors conquered, through treason, lies and treachery? Oh, Warwick, Warwick! I foresee with grief the complete loss of the whole kingdom of France."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["Be patient, York: if we conclude","a peace,","It shall be with such strict and severe covenants","As little shall the Frenchmen gain thereby."],"id":858,"m":"Be patient, York. If we make peace, it will be with such strict and harsh terms that the French will gain little from it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Charles, Alencon, Bastard, Reignier, and others.","id":859},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["Since, lords of England, it is thus agreed","That peaceful truce shall be proclaim'd in France,","We come to be informed by yourselves","What the conditions of that league must be."],"id":860,"m":"Lords of England, since it is agreed that a peaceful truce will be proclaimed in France, we have come to hear from you what the terms of that agreement must be."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Speak, Winchester; for boiling choler chokes","The hollow passage of my poison'd voice,","By sight of these our baleful enemies."],"id":861,"m":"Speak, Winchester; for boiling anger at the sight of these hateful enemies of ours chokes the hollow passage of my poisoned voice."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARDINAL","lines":["Charles, and the rest, it is enacted thus:","That, in regard King Henry gives consent,","Of mere compassion and of lenity,","To ease your country of distressful war,","And suffer you to breathe in fruitful peace,","You shall become true liegemen to his crown:","And, Charles, upon condition thou wilt swear","To pay him tribute and submit thyself,","Thou shalt be placed as viceroy under him,","And still enjoy the regal dignity."],"id":862,"m":"Charles, and the rest of you, these are the terms: since King Henry agrees, out of pure pity and mercy, to free your country from painful war and let you breathe in fruitful peace, you will become loyal subjects of his crown. And, Charles, on condition that you swear to pay him tribute and submit to him, you'll be made viceroy under him and still enjoy royal rank."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALENCON","lines":["Must he be then as shadow of himself?","Adorn his temples with a coronet,","And yet, in substance and authority,","Retain but privilege of a private man?","This proffer is absurd and reasonless."],"id":863,"m":"Must he be a shadow of himself, then? Decorate his head with a crown, and yet, in reality and power, keep only the rights of a private man? This offer is absurd and unreasonable."},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["'Tis known already that I am possess'd","With more than half the Gallian territories,","And therein reverenced for their lawful king:","Shall I, for lucre of the rest unvanquish'd,","Detract so much from that prerogative,","As to be call'd but viceroy of the whole?","No, lord ambassador, I'll rather keep","That which I have than, coveting for more,","Be cast from possibility of all."],"id":864,"m":"It's well known that I already hold more than half the lands of France and am honoured there as their lawful king. Shall I, for the sake of gaining the rest that's still unconquered, give up so much of that royal right as to be called only viceroy of the whole? No, Lord Ambassador; I'd rather keep what I have than, by grasping for more, lose the chance of everything."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Insulting Charles! hast thou by secret means","Used intercession to obtain a league,","And, now the matter grows to compromise,","Stand'st thou aloof upon comparison?","Either accept the title thou usurp'st,","Of benefit proceeding from our king","And not of any challenge of desert,","Or we will plague thee with incessant wars."],"id":865,"m":"Insolent Charles! Have you secretly used go-betweens to get a treaty, and now that it comes to an agreement, do you hold back and quibble over terms? Either accept the title you have stolen as a favour from our king, and not as something you have earned, or we'll torment you with endless wars."},{"t":"sp","who":"REIGNIER","lines":["My lord, you do not well in obstinacy","To cavil in the course of this contract:","If once it be neglected, ten to one","We shall not find like opportunity."],"id":866,"m":"My lord, you're wrong to be stubborn and pick holes in this agreement. If we once let it slip, it's ten to one we won't get another chance like it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALENCON","lines":["To say the truth, it is your policy","To save your subjects from such massacre","And ruthless slaughters as are daily seen,","By our proceeding in hostility;","And therefore take this compact of a truce,","Although you break it when your pleasure serves."],"id":867,"m":"To tell the truth, it's good policy to save your subjects from the massacres and merciless slaughter we see every day as we keep on fighting. So take this treaty of truce, even if you break it when it suits you."},{"t":"sp","who":"WARWICK","lines":["How say'st thou, Charles? shall our condition stand?"],"id":868,"m":"What do you say, Charles? Shall our terms stand?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CHARLES","lines":["It shall;","Only reserv'd, you claim no interest","In any of our towns of garrison."],"id":869,"m":"They shall, on the one condition that you claim no rights in any of our garrison towns."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Then swear allegiance to his majesty,","As thou art knight, never to disobey","Nor be rebellious to the crown of England","Thou, nor thy nobles, to the crown of England.","So, now dismiss your army when ye please;","Hang up your ensigns, let your drums be still,","For here we entertain a solemn peace."],"id":870,"m":"Then swear allegiance to his Majesty: as you are a knight, never to disobey or rebel against the crown of England, neither you nor your nobles. So, now disband your army when you like; hang up your flags and silence your drums, for here we make a solemn peace."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":871},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"V","loc":"London. The royal palace.","id":872},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Suffolk in conference with the King, Gloucester and Exeter.","id":873},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Your wondrous rare description, noble earl,","Of beauteous Margaret hath astonish'd me.","Her virtues graced with external gifts","Do breed love's settled passions in my heart:","And like as rigor of tempestuous gusts","Provokes the mightiest hulk against the tide,","So am I driven by breath of her renown,","Either to suffer shipwreck or arrive","Where I may have fruition of her love."],"id":874,"m":"Your wonderful description of beautiful Margaret, noble Earl, has amazed me. Her virtues, graced with outward beauty, plant love's firm passion in my heart. And just as the force of stormy winds drives the mightiest ship against the tide, so I am driven by the breath of her fame either to suffer shipwreck or to arrive where I can enjoy her love."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Tush, my good lord, this superficial tale","Is but a preface of her worthy praise;","The chief perfections of that lovely dame,","Had I sufficient skill to utter them,","Would make a volume of enticing lines,","Able to ravish any dull conceit:","And, which is more, she is not so divine,","So full-replete with choice of all delights,","But with as humble lowliness of mind","She is content to be at your command;","Command, I mean, of virtuous intents,","To love and honor Henry as her lord."],"id":875,"m":"Pooh, my good lord, this surface account is only an introduction to the praise she deserves. The chief perfections of that lovely lady, if I had the skill to describe them, would fill a book of enchanting lines able to delight the dullest mind. And what's more, she isn't so divine, so full of every choice delight, that she isn't content, with humble modesty, to be at your command: command, I mean, in virtuous matters, to love and honour Henry as her lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["And otherwise will Henry ne'er presume.","Therefore, my lord protector, give consent","That Margaret may be England's royal queen."],"id":876,"m":"And Henry will never presume to ask otherwise. So, Lord Protector, give your consent for Margaret to be England's royal queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["So should I give consent to flatter sin.","You know, my lord, your highness is betroth'd","Unto another lady of esteem:","How shall we then dispense with that contract,","And not deface your honor with reproach?"],"id":877,"m":"That would be agreeing to flatter sin. You know, my lord, your Highness is engaged to another lady of high rank. How can we set aside that contract without staining your honour with disgrace?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["As doth a ruler with unlawful oaths;","Or one that, at a triumph having vow'd","To try his strength, forsaketh yet the lists","By reason of his adversary's odds:","A poor earl's daughter is unequal odds,","And therefore may be broke without offense."],"id":878,"m":"The way a ruler sets aside unlawful oaths; or like someone who has vowed to test his strength at a tournament but then leaves the lists because his opponent is so outclassed. A poor earl's daughter is an unequal match, and so it can be broken off without offence."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Why, what, I pray, is Margaret more than that?","Her father is no better than an earl,","Although in glorious titles he excel."],"id":879,"m":"Why, what is Margaret more than that, I'd like to know? Her father is no better than an earl, though he has grand titles."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Yes, my lord, her father is a king,","The King of Naples and Jerusalem;","And of such great authority in France,","As his alliance will confirm our peace,","And keep the Frenchmen in allegiance."],"id":880,"m":"Yes, my lord, her father is a king, the King of Naples and Jerusalem, and of such great power in France that an alliance with him will confirm our peace and keep the French loyal."},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["And so the Earl of Armagnac may do,","Because he is near kinsman unto Charles."],"id":881,"m":"And so might the Earl of Armagnac, since he's a close relative of Charles."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXETER","lines":["Beside, his wealth doth warrant a liberal dower,","Where Reignier sooner will receive than give."],"id":882,"m":"Besides, his wealth guarantees a generous dowry, whereas Reignier is more likely to take than give."},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["A dower, my lords! disgrace not so your king,","That he should be so abject, base and poor,","To choose for wealth and not for perfect love.","Henry is able to enrich his queen,","And not to seek a queen to make him rich:","So worthless peasants bargain for their wives,","As market-men for oxen, sheep, or horse.","Marriage is a matter of more worth","Than to be dealt in by attorneyship;","Not whom we will; but whom his grace affects,","Must be companion of his nuptial bed:","And therefore, lords, since he affects her most,","It most of all these reasons bindeth us,","In our opinions she should be preferr'd.","For what is wedlock forced but a hell,","An age of discord and continual strife?","Whereas the contrary bringeth bliss,","And is a pattern of celestial peace.","Whom should we match with Henry, being a king,","But Margaret, that is daughter to a king?","Her peerless feature, joined with her birth,","Approves her fit for none but for a king;","Her valiant courage and undaunted spirit,","More than in women commonly is seen,","Will answer our hope in issue of a king;","For Henry, son unto a conqueror,","Is likely to beget more conquerors,","If with a lady of so high resolve","As is fair Margaret he be link'd in love.","Then yield, my lords; and here conclude with me","That Margaret shall be queen, and none but she."],"id":883,"m":"A dowry, my lords! Don't disgrace your king by making him so low, mean and poor that he chooses for money and not for true love. Henry can make his queen rich; he doesn't need to look for a queen to make him rich. That's how worthless peasants bargain for their wives, like traders for oxen, sheep or horses. Marriage is too important to be arranged by agents. Not whom we want, but whom his Grace loves, must share his marriage bed. And so, lords, since he loves her most, that reason binds us above all others to think she should be preferred. For what is forced marriage but a hell, a lifetime of quarrelling and constant strife? Whereas the opposite brings happiness and is a model of heavenly peace. Whom should we match with Henry, a king, but Margaret, the daughter of a king? Her matchless looks, together with her birth, prove her fit for no one but a king. Her brave courage and fearless spirit, greater than is commonly seen in women, will fulfil our hope for a king's heir; for Henry, son of a conqueror, is likely to father more conquerors if he is joined in love with a lady as determined as fair Margaret. So give way, my lords, and agree with me here that Margaret shall be queen, and no one but her."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Whether it be through force of your report,","My noble Lord of Suffolk, or for that","My tender youth was never yet attaint","With any passion of inflaming love,","I cannot tell; but this I am assured,","I feel such sharp dissension in my breast,","Such fierce alarums both of hope and fear,","As I am sick with working of my thoughts.","Take, therefore, shipping; post, my lord, to France;","Agree to any covenants, and procure","That Lady Margaret do vouchsafe to come","To cross the seas to England, and be crown'd","King Henry's faithful and anointed queen:","For your expenses and sufficient charge,","Among the people gather up a tenth.","Be gone, I say; for till you do return,","I rest perplexed with a thousand cares.","And you, good uncle, banish all offense:","If you do censure me by what you were,","Not what you are, I know it will excuse","This sudden execution of my will.","And so, conduct me where, from company,","I may revolve and ruminate my grief."],"id":884,"m":"Whether it's the power of your report, my noble Lord of Suffolk, or because my young heart has never yet been touched by any burning love, I can't tell; but I am sure of this: I feel such sharp conflict in my heart, such fierce alarms of hope and fear, that I'm sick with the turmoil of my thoughts. So take ship; hurry to France, my lord. Agree to any terms, and make sure Lady Margaret agrees to cross the sea to England and be crowned King Henry's faithful and anointed queen. For your expenses and costs, collect a tax of a tenth from the people. Go, I say; for until you return I'll be troubled by a thousand worries. And you, good uncle, put away all offence. If you judge me by what you were when young, not by what you are now, I know it will excuse this sudden carrying out of my wishes. And so take me somewhere away from company where I can turn over and brood on my grief."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":885},{"t":"sp","who":"GLOUCESTER","lines":["Aye, grief, I fear me, both at first and last."],"id":886,"m":"Yes, grief, I'm afraid, both at the beginning and at the end."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Gloucester and Exeter.","id":887},{"t":"sp","who":"SUFFOLK","lines":["Thus Suffolk hath prevail'd; and thus he goes,","As did the youthful Paris once to Greece,","With hope to find the like event in love,","But prosper better than the Troyan did.","Margaret shall now be queen, and rule the king;","But I will rule both her, the king and realm."],"id":888,"m":"So Suffolk has won, and so he goes, as young Paris once went to Greece, hoping for a similar result in love, but to do better than the Trojan did. Margaret will now be queen and rule the King; but I will rule her, the King and the kingdom."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":889}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Lord Talbot","group":"The English in France","desc":"The English champion in France, a name that French mothers use to frighten their children. Captured after Fastolfe's flight, he is exchanged, retakes Orleans by a night attack, outwits the Countess of Auvergne, wins back Rouen and is made Earl of Shrewsbury. He tears the Garter from Fastolfe's leg as a disgrace to knighthood. Trapped before Bordeaux when York and Somerset each refuse to send help, he begs his son to flee, fights beside him, and dies holding his body: 'my old arms are young John Talbot's grave.'"},{"name":"Joan la Pucelle (Joan of Arc)","group":"The French","desc":"A shepherd's daughter who says the Virgin Mary appeared to her and told her to free France. She sees through Charles's disguise, beats him in single combat, and raises the siege of Orleans. She takes Rouen by a trick, then talks Burgundy back to the French side. The English call her witch and strumpet, and in 5.3 she does call up fiends, who refuse her. Captured by York, she disowns her shepherd father, claims royal birth and virginity, then pregnancy by three different men, and is led off to be burned, cursing England."},{"name":"King Henry VI","group":"The English court","desc":"The son of Henry the Fifth, a child when the play opens and seen only from Act 3. He is pious and gentle, weeps at his uncles' quarrels, restores Richard Plantagenet as Duke of York, is crowned in Paris, and casually puts on a red rose to show it means nothing. Persuaded by Suffolk's praise, he breaks his engagement to Armagnac's daughter to marry Margaret, a choice his uncle Gloucester warns against."},{"name":"Duke of Gloucester (Humphrey)","group":"The English court","desc":"The King's uncle and Lord Protector, quick-tempered and proud of his office. He fights Winchester's men at the Tower gates and in the streets of London, accuses him in Parliament, and makes peace only for the King's sake. He arranges the Armagnac match and opposes the marriage to Margaret: 'grief, I fear me, both at first and last.'"},{"name":"Bishop of Winchester (Henry Beaufort, later Cardinal)","group":"The English court","desc":"The King's great-uncle, an ambitious churchman who plots to control the young King. He shuts Gloucester out of the Tower, swears to have his head, and gives him a hollow hand of peace. In Act 5 he has bought a cardinal's hat and vows that Gloucester will stoop to him, or he will 'sack this country with a mutiny'."},{"name":"Richard Plantagenet, Duke of York","group":"The English court","desc":"Son of the executed Earl of Cambridge, and so stripped of his rank. In the Temple Garden he plucks a white rose against Somerset's red. His dying uncle Mortimer tells him he is the rightful heir to the crown but must wait. The King restores him as Duke of York and makes him Regent in France. He blames Somerset for Talbot's death, captures Joan, condemns her, and scorns the peace that ends the play."},{"name":"Duke of Somerset (John Beaufort)","group":"The English court","desc":"Leader of the red rose. He taunts Plantagenet with his father's treason in the Temple Garden and keeps the quarrel alive in France. Ordered to join his cavalry to York's infantry, he refuses to send the horse to Bordeaux, blaming York for a rash plan, and sends them too late to save Talbot."},{"name":"Earl of Suffolk (William de la Pole)","group":"The English court","desc":"He plucks a red rose with Somerset. In Act 5 he captures Margaret, falls for her himself, and, being married, woos her for the King instead. He wins Henry with extravagant praise, agrees to give Anjou and Maine to her father, and ends the play announcing that Margaret will rule the King, and he will rule her, the King and the realm."},{"name":"Earl of Warwick","group":"The English court","desc":"He takes the white rose with Plantagenet and prophesies that the Temple Garden quarrel will send 'a thousand souls to death'. He backs Plantagenet's restoration in Parliament and helps York condemn Joan."},{"name":"Duke of Bedford","group":"The English in France","desc":"Regent of France and the King's uncle. He leaves Henry the Fifth's funeral to fight. Old and dying at Rouen, he refuses to be moved and sits in his chair before the walls until he has seen the French flee, then dies content."},{"name":"Duke of Exeter","group":"The English court","desc":"The King's great-uncle and guardian. He speaks alone at the end of key scenes, warning that the nobles' quarrels are fire under 'feigned ashes', and recalling the prophecy that Henry born at Windsor would lose all."},{"name":"Charles the Dauphin","group":"The French","desc":"Heir to the French crown, crowned at Rheims, but treated by the play as a lover and boaster more than a leader. He is beaten in combat by Joan, falls for her, and promises to make her France's saint. He blames her when Talbot surprises Orleans and accepts a truce in Act 5 while planning to break it."},{"name":"Reignier, Duke of Anjou","group":"The French","desc":"Called King of Naples. He tests Joan by standing in for the Dauphin and fights with Charles. He gives his daughter Margaret to Suffolk for King Henry in return for keeping Anjou and Maine in peace."},{"name":"Duke of Burgundy","group":"The English in France","desc":"A French duke allied to England. He helps Talbot scale the walls of Orleans and retake Rouen, then is talked back to France by Joan's speech about his wounded country, a turn she mocks as 'Done like a Frenchman'."},{"name":"Margaret of Anjou","group":"The French","desc":"Reignier's daughter, captured by Suffolk near Angiers. She is witty and quick, answers his mutterings with puns, and agrees to marry Henry if her father consents. She becomes Henry's queen in the Henry VI plays that follow."},{"name":"John Talbot","group":"The English in France","desc":"Talbot's young son, who arrives at Bordeaux after seven years apart from his father. He refuses again and again to flee, wins his first fight against the Bastard of Orleans, and dies in the French ranks."},{"name":"Sir William Lucy","group":"The English in France","desc":"The messenger who begs York and then Somerset to rescue Talbot, and who tells each that the other's quarrel is killing him. After the battle he recites Talbot's titles to the French and claims the bodies, promising that a phoenix will rise from their ashes."},{"name":"Edmund Mortimer","group":"The English court","desc":"Plantagenet's uncle, imprisoned in the Tower since Henry the Fifth's reign because he has a better claim to the crown than the house of Lancaster. Dying, he explains the family claim to Plantagenet and advises silence."},{"name":"Countess of Auvergne","group":"The French","desc":"A French lady who invites Talbot to her castle to trap him. She mocks him as a 'weak and writhled shrimp', but Talbot's horn brings his soldiers, proving his 'substance' is his army. She apologises and gives them dinner."},{"name":"Earl of Salisbury","group":"The English in France","desc":"A veteran English commander at Orleans, killed by the Master Gunner's cannon while looking through the tower grate. Talbot swears revenge on his body."},{"name":"Sir John Fastolfe","group":"The English in France","desc":"A cowardly English knight who flees at Patay, causing Talbot's capture, and again at Rouen. Talbot strips him of the Garter and the King banishes him."},{"name":"Vernon and Basset","group":"The English court","desc":"Vernon wears the white rose for York; Basset wears the red for Somerset. Their quarrel at sea and in Paris shows the nobles' feud spreading to their followers."},{"name":"The Bastard of Orleans and Alencon","group":"The French","desc":"French lords who fight beside Charles. The Bastard brings Joan to court and wounds young John Talbot; Alencon mocks the English and the truce terms."},{"name":"The Shepherd","group":"The French","desc":"Joan's father, who finds her as she is condemned. When she disowns him he curses her and says hanging is too good for her."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"At Henry the Fifth's funeral in Westminster Abbey, Bedford, Gloucester and Exeter mourn the dead King, while Gloucester and the Bishop of Winchester quarrel over the church's part in his death. Three messengers bring disasters from France: town after town lost through want of men, money and unity; Charles crowned King at Rheims; and Talbot captured after Fastolfe ran away. Bedford leaves for France, Gloucester goes to proclaim young Henry king, and Exeter goes to guard him. Alone, Winchester plans to steal the King and control the state.","key":["The play begins with a funeral: the great king is dead and his conquests start to fall away.","The messenger blames English factions for the losses, linking the war abroad to quarrels at home.","Winchester's closing speech shows a churchman hungry for power over a child king."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whose funeral opens the play?","o":["Richard II's","Henry the Fifth's","Salisbury's","Bedford's"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"view"},{"q":"According to the first messenger, why were the French towns lost?","o":["Treachery by the Duke of Burgundy, who has gone over to the French","A shortage of men and money, and English factions","Joan's witchcraft","Plague in the army"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How was Talbot captured?","o":["He was betrayed by Burgundy's men at the gates of Paris at night","Fastolfe fled and a Walloon speared him in the back","He surrendered to Joan","He was ambushed in Orleans"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the quarrel at the funeral suggest about England?","o":["Its lords are united in grief for their dead King","Its leaders are divided as soon as Henry V dies","Its people are ready to make peace with France","Its Church is the only power left in the land"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"question"},{"q":"According to the messengers, why are towns in France being lost?","o":["The French have a far larger and richer army","The English soldiers have all gone home to farm","Want of men, money and unity among the lords","Bad weather has ruined every English campaign"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"Before Orleans, Charles, Alencon and Reignier mock the starving English and attack them, but are beaten back by Salisbury's men. The Bastard of Orleans brings a holy maid who claims divine visions. Reignier pretends to be the Dauphin, but Joan sees through it at once. She tells Charles the Virgin Mary appeared to her, beats him in single combat, and refuses his love until France is free. She promises to raise the siege that night, and Charles vows to trust her.","key":["Joan's power is shown at once: she sees through a trick and wins a duel.","Her claim of a vision from 'God's mother' is the play's first big question: saint or witch?","Charles's quick infatuation and the lords' smutty jokes undercut her holiness."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who pretends to be the Dauphin to test Joan?","o":["Alencon","The Bastard","Reignier","Burgundy"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Joan say happened while she tended her lambs?","o":["An angel came down from heaven and gave her a sword for the Dauphin","The Virgin Mary appeared and told her to free France","She heard the voice of Saint Denis calling her to Orleans","She dreamed of Talbot dead on the field of battle"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Charles test Joan's claims?","o":["By a riddle","By single combat","By asking her to heal a soldier","By sending her alone to Orleans"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Joan prove her powers to Charles?","o":["She heals a wounded soldier and makes it rain over the town of Orleans","She brings a letter from the Pope proving her visions are real","She tells Charles the date of the English King's death in London","She sees through Reignier's disguise and beats Charles in combat"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Joan refuse Charles's love for now?","o":["France must be freed first","She is promised to a shepherd","She thinks he is a coward","She has sworn never to marry"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"}]},"a1s3":{"sum":"Gloucester comes to inspect the Tower of London, but the warders, on Winchester's orders, refuse to let him in. Winchester arrives with his men in tawny coats, and Gloucester's blue-coated men fight them at the gates. The two lords exchange insults ('peel'd priest', 'Winchester goose') until the Mayor of London has an officer proclaim that everyone must go home and carry no weapons. They part swearing to meet again.","key":["The English leaders brawl in London while their armies lose in France.","The blue and tawny coats are the first faction badges, before the roses.","The Mayor's helplessness shows how the great lords' quarrel endangers ordinary order."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who has ordered that Gloucester be kept out of the Tower?","o":["The King","Bedford","Winchester","The Mayor"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What colours do the two lords' servants wear?","o":["Red for Gloucester and white for Winchester's men","Blue for Gloucester, tawny for Winchester","Black and gold","Green and grey"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How is the brawl stopped?","o":["The King arrives and orders them to stop at once","An officer reads a proclamation for the Mayor","Talbot returns","Bedford orders it"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the brawl at the Tower show?","o":["The Tower is the safest place in London","The Mayor supports Winchester's side","Private feuds now threaten public order","London's citizens want war with France"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"How is the fight at the Tower stopped?","o":["The boy King arrives and orders them to stop at once","Bedford returns from France and arrests both sides","The warders open fire on both of the companies","The Mayor proclaims that all must disarm and go home"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"information"}]},"a1s4":{"sum":"In Orleans the Master Gunner aims a cannon at the tower grate where the English spy on the town and leaves his Boy to watch. On the turret, Salisbury welcomes Talbot back from captivity; Talbot tells how he was paraded in the market place and guarded by marksmen even in bed. As Salisbury surveys the town through the grate, the Boy fires: Salisbury and Gargrave fall. A messenger reports that the Dauphin has joined with Joan la Pucelle to raise the siege, and Talbot swears to avenge Salisbury.","key":["Talbot's account of his captivity shows how the French fear his very name.","A boy's cannon shot kills Salisbury, a veteran of thirteen battles: chivalry is meeting new warfare.","Talbot's 'Pucelle or puzzel' sets up the scornful English view of Joan."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who fires the shot that kills Salisbury?","o":["Joan, from the walls of Orleans","The Master Gunner's Boy","Charles, the Dauphin","The Bastard of Orleans"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How was Talbot treated as a prisoner?","o":["Kindly, as a guest","Mocked in the market place and guarded by marksmen","Kept in a dungeon in Paris","Ransomed at once"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"For whom was Talbot exchanged?","o":["The Duke of Orleans, held in the Tower","Lord Ponton de Santrailles","The Bastard","Alencon"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Talbot's account of his captivity show about the French?","o":["They treated him as an honoured guest","They planned to make him their general","They let him go free without a ransom","They fear him even as a prisoner"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"How is Salisbury killed?","o":["Joan kills him in single combat at the gate","A boy fires a cannon at the tower grate","A French spy poisons his wine at supper","He falls from the walls during an attack"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a1s5":{"sum":"In the battle before Orleans Talbot chases the Dauphin, but Joan drives the English back. Talbot fights her hand to hand and cannot beat her; she leaves him to bring food into Orleans. Talbot calls her a witch and shames his fleeing men, comparing them to sheep running from wolves, but they retreat to their trenches and Joan enters the city.","key":["Talbot cannot defeat Joan: the hero meets his match in a woman.","He explains her power as witchcraft, 'by fear, not force'.","His speech about the lions on England's coat makes the defeat a matter of national shame."],"quiz":[{"q":"What happens when Talbot fights Joan?","o":["He kills her with a single blow of his sword before the gates of Orleans","He cannot overcome her and she leaves to supply Orleans","She surrenders to him in the field and is sent to England","Charles stops the fight"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"implication"},{"q":"What does Talbot compare his fleeing soldiers to?","o":["Puppies and sheep","Rats leaving a ship","Leaves in autumn","Chickens"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"Who enters Orleans at the end of the scene?","o":["Talbot","Bedford","Joan","Burgundy"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Talbot explain his failure against Joan?","o":["He blames his tired horse","He calls her a witch","He says she cheated him","He blames Burgundy's men"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Talbot's sheep-and-wolves image accuse his men of?","o":["Being too fierce and cruel in battle","Running away like frightened animals","Eating too much of the army's food","Following Joan into the city gates"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"concept"}]},"a1s6":{"sum":"On the walls of Orleans Joan announces that the siege is raised. Charles praises her extravagantly, promises to share his crown with her, to build her a monument greater than the pyramids, and to make her the patron saint of France instead of Saint Denis.","key":["The French celebrate too soon: the next scene reverses their victory.","Charles's praise makes Joan into a saint while she is still alive.","The scene is short and boastful, preparing for a fall."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Charles promise Joan?","o":["Marriage, once the English are driven out of France","To share his crown and make her France's saint","A castle in Anjou with lands of her own","Command of the army"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"implication"},{"q":"Which saint does Charles say France will stop praying to?","o":["Saint George","Saint Michael","Saint Denis","Saint Martin"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Reignier want the citizens to do?","o":["Ring bells, light bonfires and feast","Rebuild the walls","Pray in silence","Chase the English"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Charles's praise of Joan suggest?","o":["He is a careful and sensible ruler of France","He secretly fears that Joan is a witch","He is carried away by excess and flattery","He wants to send Joan home to her father"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Whom does Charles promise Joan will replace as patron saint?","o":["Saint Denis","Saint George","Saint Michael","Saint Louis"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"implication"}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"While the French feast, Talbot, Bedford and Burgundy climb the walls of Orleans at night by different routes, crying 'Saint George! A Talbot!' The French leap from the walls half dressed. Charles accuses Joan of tricking them; she blames their careless watch, and each lord insists his own part of the wall was safe. An English soldier collects the clothes they leave behind, using only Talbot's name as a weapon.","key":["The English win by surprise, the same tactic the French called sorcery.","The French lords quarrel and blame each other, mirroring the English nobles.","The soldier's joke shows how Talbot's name alone is a weapon."],"quiz":[{"q":"How do the English retake Orleans?","o":["By starving it out over a long winter siege, until the people gave in","By scaling the walls at night while the French feast","By Burgundy's treachery","By Joan's betrayal"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Joan answer Charles's accusation?","o":["She confesses she was asleep in her bed when the English came","She blames the watch for being careless","She curses Talbot","She leaves France"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the English soldier say is his only weapon?","o":["A dagger taken from a Frenchman","The cry of Talbot's name","A long pike","His fists"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the French quarrel after the attack reveal?","o":["They agree that Joan was right all along","They decide to surrender to Talbot at once","Each blames the others rather than himself","They blame the English for breaking a truce"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"question"},{"q":"What does the soldier's use of Talbot's name show?","o":["Talbot's reputation alone frightens the French","The soldier is secretly Talbot in disguise","The French have never heard of Talbot","Talbot is the only English soldier left"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"inference"}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"In Orleans at dawn Talbot orders Salisbury's body to be set in the market place and vows to build him a tomb there telling of the sack of the town. Burgundy jokes that he saw the Dauphin and his 'trull' running arm in arm. A messenger brings an invitation from the Countess of Auvergne, who wants to see the famous Talbot. Talbot accepts and goes alone, but first whispers instructions to a Captain.","key":["Talbot keeps his vow to Salisbury: five French dead for every drop of his blood.","Burgundy's joke continues the English view of Joan as Charles's whore.","Talbot's whisper sets up the trick of the next scene."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Talbot plan for Salisbury?","o":["To send his body to England","A tomb in Orleans recording the sack of the town","A statue in London","To bury him at sea"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Who invites Talbot to visit?","o":["The Duchess of Burgundy, his ally's wife","The Countess of Auvergne","Joan","The Queen of France"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Talbot do before leaving?","o":["Writes his will and sends it to England","Whispers instructions to a Captain","Takes Bedford with him","Changes into disguise"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Talbot plan a tomb in the market place?","o":["To honour Salisbury and record the sack of Orleans","To mark the place where he was held prisoner","To bury the French soldiers killed in the night","To show the French King where he will be buried"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Talbot's whisper to the Captain prepare?","o":["A trap for any trap the Countess sets","A secret attack on the Dauphin's camp","A message to the King in London","An escape from Orleans that night"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"implication"}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"The Countess of Auvergne plans to win fame by capturing Talbot. When he arrives she scorns his small size, calling him a child and a 'writhled shrimp', and then declares him her prisoner. Talbot laughs: she has only his shadow. He winds his horn and soldiers burst in; they are his substance, sinews, arms and strength. The Countess apologises, and Talbot asks only for food and wine for his soldiers.","key":["The Countess compares herself to Tomyris, another woman trying to defeat a great warrior.","Talbot's riddle of shadow and substance says a leader's real self is his army.","The scene is the play's lightest, a comic duel won by courtesy."],"quiz":[{"q":"How does the Countess describe Talbot when she sees him?","o":["A mighty giant, as tall as the castle tower","A child and a weak, wrinkled shrimp","A devil sent from hell to plague France","A noble knight in shining armour"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"view"},{"q":"What does Talbot say she has captured?","o":["His heart","Only his shadow","His sword","His son"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Talbot ask for in the end?","o":["Her castle and all her lands in Auvergne","Ransom","Wine and food for his soldiers","Her hand"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Talbot mean by his 'shadow' and 'substance'?","o":["He is nothing without his soldiers","His real body is in another town","His ghost will haunt the Countess","He is taller than she believes"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"concept"},{"q":"How does Talbot treat the Countess after her plot fails?","o":["Harshly, sending her to London in chains","Generously, asking only for food and wine","Coldly, burning her castle to the ground","Cruelly, mocking her in front of his men"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"view"}]},"a2s4":{"sum":"In the Temple Garden in London, Richard Plantagenet and Somerset argue about a point of law. Unable to get a verdict, Plantagenet plucks a white rose and invites those who agree with him to do the same; Somerset plucks a red. Warwick, Vernon and a Lawyer choose white; Suffolk chooses red. Somerset taunts Plantagenet that his father was executed for treason, making him a mere yeoman. Plantagenet swears to wear the white rose until he dies or rises, and Warwick prophesies that this brawl will send a thousand souls to death between the red rose and the white.","key":["The roses become the badges of York and Lancaster: the Wars of the Roses begin as a garden quarrel.","Somerset's insult about Cambridge's treason makes the dispute personal and dynastic.","Warwick's prophecy reaches forward to the civil wars of the later Henry VI plays."],"quiz":[{"q":"Which rose does Plantagenet pluck?","o":["Red","White","Yellow","Pink"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"How does Somerset insult Plantagenet?","o":["He calls him a coward who would run from any fight in France or England","He says his father was executed for treason, making him a yeoman","He mocks his poverty and his lack of lands and servants","He calls him French"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Who sides with Somerset?","o":["Warwick","Vernon","Suffolk","The Lawyer"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why is the Temple Garden scene important to the history plays?","o":["It shows the start of the Wars of the Roses","It shows how the war with France was won","It shows Henry V's funeral and the crowning","It shows Joan's capture and her trial"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Somerset use against Plantagenet?","o":["His father's execution for treason","His poor knowledge of the law","His friendship with the French","His low birth as a yeoman's son"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"purpose"}]},"a2s5":{"sum":"In the Tower, the dying Edmund Mortimer waits for his nephew. Plantagenet arrives and asks why his father, the Earl of Cambridge, was beheaded. Mortimer explains that Henry IV deposed Richard II, and that the Percies and later Cambridge tried to put Mortimer on the throne because through Lionel, Duke of Clarence, he had the better claim. He names Plantagenet his heir, warns him to be careful because Lancaster is firmly rooted, and dies. Plantagenet resolves to go to Parliament to win back his rights.","key":["Mortimer gives the genealogy behind the Wars of the Roses: Clarence's line against Lancaster's.","His advice of 'silence' and policy shapes York's patient ambition.","Plantagenet hides his thoughts: 'what I do imagine, let that rest.'"],"quiz":[{"q":"Why has Mortimer been imprisoned?","o":["For the murder of a lord at court in his youth","Because he had a claim to the crown","For debt","For witchcraft"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"From which son of Edward III does Mortimer descend?","o":["John of Gaunt","Lionel, Duke of Clarence","Edmund Langley","The Black Prince"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Mortimer advise Plantagenet?","o":["To rebel at once and gather an army in the north to take the crown","To be politic and silent, for Lancaster is strong","To flee to France","To marry Margaret"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Why does Mortimer warn Plantagenet to be careful?","o":["His claim to the throne is very weak","The house of Lancaster is firmly rooted","The King has ordered his arrest","He has no friends left in the court"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"implication"},{"q":"What does Mortimer's story give Plantagenet?","o":["A fortune in gold","A claim to the throne","A place in France","A pardon for his crimes"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"purpose"}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"In Parliament Gloucester tries to present a list of accusations; Winchester tears it up and they trade charges. Their servants, banned from carrying weapons, fight with stones in the streets and burst in with bloody heads. The boy King pleads with tears for peace, and Gloucester and Winchester shake hands, though both privately mean nothing by it. Warwick presents Plantagenet's petition and the King restores him as Duke of York; Somerset curses him aside. Gloucester advises the King to be crowned in Paris. Exeter, alone, fears that the quarrels will burst into flame and recalls the prophecy that Henry born at Windsor will lose all.","key":["The quarrel of Gloucester and Winchester spreads from the lords to their servants and into the streets.","The King's goodness makes peace, but only on the surface.","Restoring York, Henry rewards the man who will challenge his crown."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Winchester do with Gloucester's written charges?","o":["Reads them aloud","Tears them up","Signs them","Burns them"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What do the servants fight with after weapons are banned?","o":["Clubs","Stones","Swords","Torches"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the King make Richard Plantagenet?","o":["Earl of Shrewsbury","Duke of York","Regent of France","Protector"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Why is the handshake between Gloucester and Winchester hollow?","o":["Both are wounded","Neither means it","The King forbids it","Exeter has bribed them"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Exeter's prophecy add to the scene?","o":["Hope that Henry will win all of France soon","A sense that Henry's reign will end in loss","A plan for the King's coronation in Paris","A warning that Winchester will soon be Pope"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"information"}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"Joan, disguised as a peasant with soldiers carrying sacks of corn, enters Rouen and signals with a torch; the French take the town. Talbot escapes, cursing her. The sick Bedford is carried in his chair before the walls. Joan mocks the English from the walls, asking if they like the taste of her corn, and taunts old Bedford. Talbot and Burgundy vow to retake Rouen or die. Bedford refuses to be moved. In the battle Fastolfe runs away again, but the English win; Bedford dies having seen the French flee, and Talbot plans his funeral.","key":["Joan wins with a trick, Talbot wins back with courage: the scene is a microcosm of the war.","Bedford's death in his chair is a dignified end for the old generation.","Fastolfe's second flight sets up his disgrace."],"quiz":[{"q":"How does Joan get into Rouen?","o":["Over the walls by night with ladders","Disguised as a peasant selling corn","Through a tunnel","By bribing the governor"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Bedford insist on?","o":["Returning to England at once for his health","Staying in his chair before the walls","Fighting Joan himself, though he is old","Making peace with the French"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What happens to Bedford?","o":["He is captured","He dies after seeing the French flee","He is killed by Joan","He recovers"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Joan take Rouen?","o":["By a long siege that starves the town out","By a trick, with soldiers disguised as peasants","By bribing the English captain of the gates","By a sudden storm that drives the English out"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Bedford's choice to stay show?","o":["Fear of being carried away by the French","Anger at Talbot for losing the town","A wish to make peace with Joan at Rouen","Old courage that shames Fastolfe's cowardice"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"inference"}]},"a3s3":{"sum":"On the plains near Rouen Joan tells the French lords not to despair and proposes to win Burgundy away from Talbot. As the English march past towards Paris, she calls a parley with Burgundy and pleads with him to look at the wounds he has given his own country, and warns that the English will throw him out once they win. Burgundy is moved and rejoins the French. Joan mutters, 'Done like a Frenchman: turn and turn again!'","key":["Joan's speech on wounded France is one of the play's most persuasive.","Burgundy's change of side shows how fragile alliances are.","Joan's aside mocks the very persuasion she has just used."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Joan persuade Burgundy to do?","o":["Kill Talbot in his tent while he sleeps tonight","Leave the English and join the French","Retire to his estates","Spy on York"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What example does Joan use to show English bad faith?","o":["They freed the Duke of Orleans, Burgundy's enemy, without ransom","They burned Burgundy's castle and all of his towns in Flanders last summer","They imprisoned his son in the Tower of London without trial","They taxed Burgundy"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does Joan say aside after Burgundy turns?","o":["God be thanked, for France is saved by this","Done like a Frenchman: turn and turn again","Now Talbot dies, alone and without a friend","This will not last a week"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Joan win Burgundy over?","o":["She offers him the crown of France as a gift","She threatens to burn his lands in Burgundy","She proves the English have betrayed Talbot","She shows him the wounds of his own country"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Joan's aside 'turn and turn again' imply?","o":["Burgundy is a loyal friend of France","Burgundy is fickle and easily turned","The French will win every battle now","Joan plans to betray the Dauphin"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"inference"}]},"a3s4":{"sum":"In Paris Talbot lays his sword at the King's feet and lists his conquests: fifty fortresses, twelve cities, seven walled towns and five hundred prisoners. Henry, who remembers his father's praise of Talbot, makes him Earl of Shrewsbury. When the court leaves, Vernon of the white rose and Basset of the red quarrel over insults at sea; Vernon strikes Basset, who will ask the King for leave to fight.","key":["Talbot's loyalty and the King's gratitude show the bond that should hold the realm together.","At once the rose quarrel reappears among followers.","Striking a blow at court is forbidden, so Basset turns to the law of arms."],"quiz":[{"q":"What title does the King give Talbot?","o":["Duke of Bedford","Earl of Shrewsbury","Regent of France","Earl of Warwick"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Which rose does Vernon wear?","o":["Red, for Somerset","White, for York","None","Both"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why doesn't Basset strike back at once?","o":["He is afraid of Vernon and of the Duke of York's men in the hall","Drawing a sword at court is punishable by death","The King is watching","Vernon apologises"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"How does Henry reward Talbot?","o":["He gives him Joan as a prisoner","He makes him Regent of France","He makes him Earl of Shrewsbury","He gives him Somerset's lands"],"a":2,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the Vernon and Basset quarrel show?","o":["The French have planted spies at court","The King's soldiers want to go home","The rose feud is spreading to followers","Talbot's men refuse to obey the King"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"question"}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"Henry is crowned King of France in Paris. Fastolfe arrives with a letter from Burgundy, and Talbot tears the Garter from his leg for his cowardice at Patay; the King banishes him. Burgundy's letter announces he has joined Charles, and Talbot is sent to punish him. Vernon and Basset ask for a trial by combat, and York and Somerset begin to quarrel again. Gloucester rebukes them, and the King pleads for unity, reminding them they are in France among enemies. To show it means nothing, he puts on a red rose, then makes York Regent of France and orders Somerset to join his cavalry to York's infantry. York dislikes the rose; Exeter foresees ruin when 'envy breeds unkind division'.","key":["The coronation is undermined at once by cowardice, betrayal and quarrels.","Henry's red rose is innocent in intention and disastrous in effect.","Dividing command between York and Somerset sets up Talbot's death."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Talbot tear from Fastolfe?","o":["His sword-belt","The Garter","His gilt spurs","His badge"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the King do to show the roses mean nothing?","o":["He forbids them both on pain of death","He puts on a red rose","He wears both roses on his cap","He burns them"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"concept"},{"q":"How does the King divide command in France?","o":["Talbot alone","York as Regent, with Somerset's horse joined to York's foot","Somerset as Regent","Burgundy and Talbot"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why is Henry's choice of a red rose a mistake?","o":["It insults the French at his crowning","It is the colour of the French King","It shows him taking sides in the feud","It means he has chosen to marry"],"a":2,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Why does Talbot tear off Fastolfe's Garter?","o":["For stealing from the army","For his cowardice in battle","For insulting the King","For joining the French"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"Before Bordeaux Talbot summons the town to surrender to King Henry, threatening famine, steel and fire. The French General answers that Talbot is trapped: the town is strong, and the Dauphin's army surrounds him. He praises Talbot's spirit and says that before his hourglass runs out Talbot will be dead. Hearing the Dauphin's drum, Talbot compares his men to deer hemmed in by hounds and urges them to sell their lives dearly.","key":["Talbot's three servants, famine, steel and fire, are the old language of conquest.","The General's speech praises his enemy as it predicts his death.","The deer image turns the hunters into the hunted."],"quiz":[{"q":"What 'three attendants' does Talbot threaten Bordeaux with?","o":["Plague, flood and fire","Famine, steel and fire","York, Somerset and Burgundy","Archers, horse and foot"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the General say will happen before his hourglass runs out?","o":["The Dauphin will retreat","Talbot will be dead","Bordeaux will surrender","Burgundy will return"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What animals does Talbot compare his men to?","o":["Lions at bay in the hills of Africa","Deer surrounded by hounds","Wolves","Eagles"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"How does the General's speech change Talbot's position?","o":["It shows the French will soon surrender","It shows Talbot has won the town easily","It shows the Dauphin has gone back to Paris","It shows the hunter is now the hunted"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Talbot urge his trapped men to do?","o":["Sell their lives dearly","Surrender to the French","Run for the river","Wait for the King"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"information"}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"On the plains of Gascony York hears that the Dauphin's forces have joined to attack Talbot. He curses Somerset for holding back the promised cavalry. Sir William Lucy begs York to rescue Talbot, but York says he cannot without the horse. Lucy tells him young John Talbot has just arrived to join his father. York leaves, blaming Somerset for the loss of Maine, Blois, Poitiers and Tours. Lucy says sedition is a vulture feeding on the commanders while Henry the Fifth's conquests are lost.","key":["York's rage is genuine, but he does nothing.","Lucy is the voice of loyalty caught between two proud men.","The news of John Talbot makes the coming disaster personal."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why won't York go to help Talbot?","o":["He is ill with a fever in his tent","Somerset has not sent the cavalry","Talbot insulted him","He is besieging Paris"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Who begs York to rescue Talbot?","o":["Sir William Lucy","Sir Richard Vernon, a white rose man","Warwick","Exeter"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Lucy compare sedition to?","o":["A worm","A vulture","A storm","A fire"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"Why can't York rescue Talbot?","o":["York's men are sick and tired","Somerset holds back the cavalry","Talbot refuses any help from York","The King orders York to stay put"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Lucy's vulture image say about the commanders?","o":["They are hungry and need more food","They are brave and fierce in battle","Their quarrels feed on England's losses","They watch from the hills like birds"],"a":2,"b":2,"p":"concept"}]},"a4s4":{"sum":"Somerset says it is too late to send help: York and Talbot planned the expedition rashly, and York has set Talbot on to die so that he can take the glory. Lucy tells Somerset that Talbot is 'bought and sold', surrounded by all the French lords and dying by their default. Each duke blames the other. Somerset finally agrees to send the horsemen, but Lucy says it is too late: Talbot will be taken or slain.","key":["Somerset mirrors York exactly: each blames the other.","Lucy names the cause: 'the fraud of England, not the force of France'.","The help comes too late, a pattern of the whole play."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Somerset claim about York?","o":["He is a coward who has never fought in France or anywhere else","He set Talbot on to die so he could take the glory","He is secretly French","He has taken bribes"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"According to Lucy, what has trapped Talbot?","o":["The great force of France","The fraud of England","Joan's witchcraft","The weather"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Somerset finally do?","o":["Refuses outright to send a single man to Talbot","Agrees to send the horse, too late","Goes himself to Bordeaux with all his men","Asks the King what he should do"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the blame game between York and Somerset show?","o":["Pride matters more to them than Talbot's life","They both care deeply for Talbot's safety","They agree that Talbot was right to fight","They both blame the King for his weakness"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does Lucy mean by 'bought and sold'?","o":["Talbot has been betrayed by his own side","Talbot has been ransomed for a large sum","Talbot has been sold to the French King","Talbot has bought his way out of danger"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"concept"}]},"a4s5":{"sum":"In his camp near Bordeaux Talbot greets his son John, whom he sent for to learn war, and urges him to flee on the swiftest horse. John refuses: to flee would make him a bastard and a coward and shame his mother. In rhyming couplets they argue, each wanting the other to escape. At last Talbot accepts his son's choice, and they go together to live and die side by side.","key":["The scene is written in rhyming couplets, giving it the formality of a ritual.","Honour and family name matter more to John than life.","The father who sent for his son to learn war now must watch him die in it."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Talbot urge John to do?","o":["Fight by his side","Flee on his swiftest horse","Go to York for help","Surrender to the French"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does John refuse?","o":["He wants a reward and a title from the King","Fleeing would shame his name and his mother","He hates the French and wants to kill them all","He has no horse"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What is unusual about the form of the scene?","o":["It is all in prose, with no verse at all","It is in rhyming couplets","It is sung by a choir offstage","It is in French"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does John refuse to flee?","o":["He does not believe the French are near","He has been ordered by the King to stay","He wants to win his father's earldom","Flight would shame his name and his mother"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What effect do the rhyming couplets have?","o":["They make the debate formal and moving","They make the scene comic and light","They show that the Talbots are French","They show that both men are lying"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"implication"}]},"a4s6":{"sum":"In the battle Talbot rescues his son, who has been surrounded. John has fought the Bastard of Orleans and drawn blood. Talbot again begs him to flee now that he has proved himself; John again refuses, saying flight would make him unworthy to be Talbot's son. Talbot calls him Icarus and they go to fight together.","key":["Talbot gives his son life a second time, then accepts his death.","John's first fight, against the Bastard, is a test of blood and legitimacy.","The Icarus image prepares for the son's fall."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whom does John Talbot wound in his first fight?","o":["Charles","The Bastard of Orleans","Alencon","Burgundy"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Talbot call his son?","o":["Hercules","Icarus","Hector","Achilles"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"What does John say rather than flee?","o":["He will die at Talbot's foot","He will go home to England and his mother","He will find York and bring help","He will surrender"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Talbot call his son Icarus?","o":["He fears the boy will fly too high and fall","He thinks the boy will fly away from danger","He wants his son to learn how to build wings","He thinks the boy is too clever for the army"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"concept"},{"q":"What has John proved before Talbot rescues him?","o":["His courage, by wounding the Bastard","His cowardice, by running from the field","His skill, by killing the Dauphin","His loyalty, by saving Somerset"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a4s7":{"sum":"Talbot, wounded and led by a servant, describes how John protected him, then rushed into the French ranks and died like Icarus. Soldiers bring in the boy's body; Talbot speaks to it, takes it in his arms and dies. The French lords arrive; Joan says young Talbot scorned to fight a 'giglot wench'. Lucy comes to ask for the prisoners and lists Talbot's many titles, which Joan mocks. Lucy claims the bodies and promises that from their ashes a phoenix will rise; Charles lets him take them and heads for Paris.","key":["Talbot's death, holding his son, is the emotional climax of the play.","Lucy's long list of titles against Joan's 'stinking and fly-blown' sets honour against mockery.","Charles's restraint ('let us not wrong it dead') shows the French respect Talbot."],"quiz":[{"q":"How does Talbot die?","o":["Killed by Joan in single combat on the field","Holding the body of his son","Hanged by the French in the market place","In York's camp, of his wounds"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Joan respond to Lucy's list of titles?","o":["She weeps","She mocks it as tedious and says Talbot lies stinking at her feet","She salutes him","She asks for ransom"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"view"},{"q":"What does Lucy say will rise from the Talbots' ashes?","o":["A lion","A phoenix","A white rose","A ghost"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Joan's mockery of Talbot's titles affect the scene?","o":["It shows that she secretly admires Talbot","It proves that Lucy was lying about Talbot","It shows the French are kind to their foes","It contrasts French scorn with English grief"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Lucy's phoenix promise?","o":["Revenge will rise from the Talbots' ashes","Talbot will be raised from the dead","A new King will be crowned in Paris","A great bird will fly over England"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"implication"}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"In London the King hears that the Pope, the Emperor and the Earl of Armagnac urge peace with France. Gloucester supports it, and adds that Armagnac offers his only daughter in marriage with a large dowry. Henry, who prefers his books, agrees. Exeter notices that Winchester has become a cardinal and recalls Henry the Fifth's warning. Alone with the Papal Legate, Winchester promises the money he paid for his hat and vows to make Gloucester stoop or wreck the country.","key":["The King agrees to marry for peace, a contract he will soon break.","Winchester has bought his cardinal's hat.","Peace with France comes just as the factions at home grow stronger."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whose daughter is Henry first engaged to?","o":["Reignier's daughter, Margaret","The Earl of Armagnac's","Burgundy's daughter","The French King's sister"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"view"},{"q":"How has Winchester become a cardinal?","o":["By election","By paying the Pope","By the King's gift","By a miracle"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Henry say he prefers to marriage?","o":["Hunting","His study and books","War","Music"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Winchester's cardinal's hat reveal?","o":["He is a humble man of God","He has bought power with money","He has given up politics","He has made peace with Gloucester"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does Henry's response to the marriage offer show?","o":["He is a passionate king who wants a French wife","He is a warlike king who distrusts all of France","He is a greedy king who wants a large dowry","He is a bookish king who goes along with advice"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"inference"}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"In Anjou the French hear that Paris has revolted and turned to them. A scout reports that the English armies have united to fight. Burgundy says they need not fear now that Talbot is dead, and Joan calls fear the most cursed of passions.","key":["With Talbot gone, the French grow confident.","The English unite only now, too late.","Joan's last confident words before her spirits fail her."],"quiz":[{"q":"What news cheers the French?","o":["Talbot is alive and has been taken prisoner by the Bastard","The Parisians have revolted to their side","York is dead in the field","Burgundy has returned"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the scout report?","o":["The English have fled","The two English armies have joined","Joan is captured","Peace is signed"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Joan call the most cursed passion?","o":["Love","Fear","Anger","Pride"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"What does the news from Paris suggest?","o":["The English have taken all of France","The French King has fled to Italy","English power in France is crumbling","The Pope has excommunicated Joan"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What changes for the French now that Talbot is dead?","o":["They no longer fear the English","They want to make peace at once","They decide to invade England","They mourn him as a hero"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a5s3":{"sum":"Before Angiers, the French losing, Joan calls on her familiar spirits. Fiends appear but will not speak. She offers her blood, a limb, her body and finally her soul, but they hang their heads and leave. York captures her and mocks her. Suffolk captures Margaret, Reignier's daughter, and is struck by her beauty. Married already, he decides to win her for the King, and through comic asides and cross-talk persuades her to accept if her father agrees. Reignier comes down from the walls and consents on condition he keeps Anjou and Maine. Suffolk kisses Margaret and leaves, planning to dazzle Henry with her praise.","key":["The fiends' scene answers the play's question about Joan in the English way: she is a witch.","As one dangerous French woman is captured, another is brought into England's heart.","Suffolk's courtship is comic, but its price, Anjou and Maine, is serious."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Joan offer the fiends last of all?","o":["Her sword","Her soul","Charles","Her father"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Who captures Margaret?","o":["York","Suffolk","Warwick","Somerset"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Reignier ask in return for his daughter?","o":["A great dowry for his daughter, paid in English gold","To keep Anjou and Maine in peace","Joan's freedom from the English","The crown of France"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the fiends' silence mean for Joan?","o":["Her spirits are too weak to talk","She has won the battle anyway","She can still escape the English","Her power has abandoned her"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"concept"},{"q":"What does Suffolk's plan for Margaret suggest about his aims?","o":["He wants to make peace for its own sake","He wants power through the King's wife","He wants to marry Margaret himself","He wants to send her back to her father"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"inference"}]},"a5s4":{"sum":"In York's camp Joan is brought out condemned. Her old shepherd father finds her, but she denies him and claims noble blood; he curses her and leaves. She declares herself a holy virgin whose blood will cry for vengeance, then, when that fails, says she is pregnant, naming Charles, then Alencon, then Reignier. York and Warwick mock her; Warwick orders plenty of firewood and pitch so that her suffering will be short, and she is led away cursing England. Winchester, now a cardinal, brings news of a peace treaty. York calls it effeminate. Charles at first refuses to be a mere viceroy, but Reignier and Alencon urge him to accept, Alencon adding that he can break the truce when it suits him. Charles agrees, provided the English claim none of his garrison towns, and swears allegiance to Henry.","key":["Joan's denial of her father and her shifting claims destroy her credibility.","Her curse looks forward to the darkness of the next plays.","The peace is a humiliation to York and a hollow promise from Charles."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who does Joan deny?","o":["Charles","Her shepherd father","York","God"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Joan try to escape execution?","o":["By flight from her guards at night","By claiming to be pregnant","By a large bribe to York","By converting to the English side"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What title does Charles accept in the peace?","o":["King of France","Viceroy under Henry","Duke of Anjou","Regent"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Joan's denial of her father affect our view of her?","o":["It shows pride and makes her seem false","It proves that she truly is of noble birth","It shows she wants to protect the old man","It makes the English take pity on her"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"view"},{"q":"What does Alencon's advice show about the peace?","o":["It will last for a hundred years","It is a true and lasting friendship","It will be broken when it suits France","It will make France part of England"],"a":2,"b":4,"p":"inference"}]},"a5s5":{"sum":"In London Suffolk's praise of Margaret has won the King's heart. Gloucester objects that Henry is engaged to Armagnac's daughter, and Exeter notes Reignier is poor. Suffolk argues that marriage should be for love, not money, and that Margaret's courage will give the King conquering sons. Henry sends Suffolk to fetch her, raising a tax to pay for it. Gloucester foresees grief. Alone, Suffolk compares himself to Paris going to Greece and boasts that Margaret will rule the King, and he will rule her, the King and the realm.","key":["Henry breaks his word for love, a mistake his father would not have made.","Suffolk's comparison to Paris recalls the war Helen caused.","The ending sets up the troubles of 2 Henry VI."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Gloucester object to the marriage?","o":["Margaret is French","Henry is already engaged to Armagnac's daughter","Margaret is too old","Suffolk is lying"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"How will Suffolk's expenses be paid?","o":["From the King's own treasury, as is usual","By a tax of a tenth on the people","By Reignier","By Winchester"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"Who does Suffolk compare himself to?","o":["Paris going to Greece","Caesar crossing the Rubicon","Aeneas","Hector"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"Why does Gloucester object to the marriage?","o":["Margaret is too old for the King","Henry is already engaged elsewhere","Margaret is the daughter of a duke","Henry wants to marry a Scottish princess"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Suffolk's Paris comparison foreshadow?","o":["A happy and long royal marriage","A peace treaty that will last","A great victory over the French","Disaster, as in the Trojan War"],"a":3,"b":4,"p":"implication"}]}},"notes":{"2":"Henry the Fifth died in 1422, aged 35, leaving a son under a year old. Comets were believed to foretell the death of princes and changes of state.","3":"Gloucester is Humphrey, Henry the Fifth's youngest brother, appointed Protector of England during the new King's childhood.","6":"Gloucester hints that Winchester's churchmen prayed for Henry's death so that they could control a weak, 'effeminate' successor.","7":"Gloucester's wife Eleanor is proud and ambitious; her plotting becomes important in 2 Henry VI.","9":"Bedford breaks off mid-sentence ('bright—') when the messenger enters: perhaps 'bright Cassiopeia'. Julius Caesar's soul was said to have become a comet.","11":"Shakespeare compresses history: these towns were lost over some twenty years after Henry's death, not at his funeral.","15":"'Flower-de-luces' are the fleurs-de-lis of France, quartered on the English royal arms since Edward III claimed the French crown.","26":"The battle described is Patay (1429). The real Sir John Fastolf was blamed for retreating; Shakespeare spells him 'Fastolfe'. A Walloon is a soldier from the southern Netherlands.","29":"Saint George's feast is 23 April. Bedford's bonfires will be French towns burning.","38":"'Jack out of office' is a proverbial phrase for a man who has lost his post. Eltham Palace in Kent was a royal residence.","42":"Mars, god of war, was known for the irregular movement of his planet; Charles means no one can predict the fortunes of war.","50":"Froissart was a fourteenth-century chronicler. Oliver and Roland (Rowland) were Charlemagne's greatest knights; Samson and Goliath were giants of strength.","57":"The sibyls were prophetesses of the ancient world; the Romans kept their books of prophecy.","63":"Joan of Arc (about 1412 to 1431) said she heard the voices of saints. Shakespeare's Joan says the Virgin Mary appeared to her and changed her from 'black and swart' to beautiful.","65":"The story of Joan's sword, found at the church of Saint Catherine at Fierbois in Touraine, comes from the chronicles.","69":"Deborah was an Old Testament prophetess and judge who led Israel to victory (Judges 4 to 5).","75":"'Shrives this woman to her smock' is a bawdy joke: he is hearing her confession down to her underwear.","81":"'Saint Martin's summer' is a spell of fine weather around Saint Martin's Day (11 November); 'halcyon days' are calm days in winter. Caesar told a frightened boatman that he carried Caesar and his fortune.","82":"Mahomet was said to have trained a dove to eat from his ear, pretending it was the Holy Spirit. Helen, mother of Constantine, was said to have found the true Cross; Saint Philip's four daughters prophesied (Acts 21).","89":"'Conveyance' means stealing or underhand dealing. Gloucester suspects Winchester of taking arms and treasure from the Tower.","104":"'Peel'd priest' mocks the shaven crown (tonsure) of a cleric.","107":"Damascus was traditionally where Cain killed Abel. Winchester and Gloucester were in fact half-uncle and nephew, not brothers.","112":"A 'Winchester goose' was slang for venereal disease or a prostitute: the Bishop of Winchester licensed the brothels in Southwark.","139":"Ponton de Santrailles (Xaintrailles) was a French captain. Prisoner exchanges between nobles of equal rank were common.","150":"Talbot's 'Plantagenet' here refers to Salisbury's royal kinship. The Nero image, playing as Rome burned, shows Talbot's revenge will be ruthless.","156":"'Puzzel' means a slut or drab, a pun on 'Pucelle' (maid). 'Dolphin' is the old English spelling and sound of 'Dauphin'.","171":"Hannibal is said to have escaped from the Romans by tying burning sticks to the horns of oxen, terrifying the enemy: victory by fear, not force.","180":"Astraea was the goddess of justice who left the earth at the end of the Golden Age. The gardens of Adonis bloomed and faded in a few days.","183":"Rhodope was a Greek courtesan said to have built a pyramid in Egypt. Darius's jewelled coffer was taken by Alexander the Great, who kept Homer's poems in it. Saint Denis is the patron saint of France.","195":"Burgundy's joke means: let her not be made pregnant by a man while she follows the French army.","247":"Tomyris, queen of the Scythians, killed the Persian king Cyrus and put his head in a bag of blood.","252":"Hercules and Hector were the greatest heroes of legend, both huge and strong. The real Talbot was in his seventies by the time of his death.","278":"The Temple was one of the Inns of Court in London, where lawyers trained and lived. The garden scene is Shakespeare's invention; there is no record that the rose badges began this way.","283":"Warwick claims to judge hawks, dogs, swords, horses and girls, but not the 'nice sharp quillets', the fine points, of law.","304":"A canker is a grub or worm that eats rosebuds from within.","313":"Lionel Duke of Clarence was the third son of Edward III; John of Gaunt, ancestor of the Lancastrians, was the fourth. York claims through Lionel's line on his mother's side.","316":"'Attainted' means convicted of treason, which cancelled a family's titles and rights of inheritance. Plantagenet claims his father was 'attached' (arrested) but never formally attainted.","324":"Warwick's prophecy anticipates the Wars of the Roses (1455 to 1487), the subject of 2 and 3 Henry VI and Richard III.","332":"Shakespeare confuses two Edmund Mortimers. The Earl of March did not die in the Tower; he served Henry V and died in Ireland in 1425. Nestor was the old counsellor of the Greeks at Troy.","343":"Mortimer's genealogy: Edward III's sons included Edward the Black Prince (father of Richard II), Lionel of Clarence, John of Gaunt (father of Henry IV) and Edmund Langley, Duke of York. Plantagenet descends from Clarence through his mother and from Langley through his father.","353":"'Restored to my blood' means having the attainder removed, so that he can inherit his father's titles and lands.","360":"Winchester was the son of John of Gaunt by his mistress Katherine Swynford, born before they married, though later legitimised.","368":"Warwick puns on Rome and 'roam'; in Shakespeare's time the words sounded alike.","381":"The Mayor reports the servants have obeyed the letter of the weapons ban by fighting with stones.","388":"An 'inkhorn mate' is a scholar or clerk who carries a pen and inkpot: a sneer at the Bishop's learning.","423":"Henry V was born at Monmouth in Wales, Henry VI at Windsor. The prophecy that one would win all and the other lose all is recorded by Holinshed.","429":"The French means 'Who goes there?' Joan answers 'Peasants, poor people of France.'","450":"Darnel is a weed found in corn fields, thought to cause dizziness. Joan mocks the English for losing Rouen to sacks of corn.","454":"To 'break a lance' and 'run a tilt' are terms from jousting.","458":"Charles's whispering lords are mocked as a parliament; the Speaker presides over the Commons.","466":"Richard I, 'Coeur-de-lion' (Lionheart), had his heart buried at Rouen.","471":"Uther Pendragon, King Arthur's father, was carried in a litter to a battle and won.","518":"Charles, Duke of Orleans, was captured at Agincourt in 1415 and held in England for twenty-five years; his release in 1440 was against Burgundy's wishes. Shakespeare moves it earlier.","520":"Joan mocks the fickleness of the French, the very quality she has just exploited.","531":"The real Talbot was made Earl of Shrewsbury in 1442, long after Henry VI's coronation in Paris in 1431.","549":"The Order of the Garter, founded by Edward III, is England's highest order of knighthood; its badge is a garter worn below the left knee.","555":"Burgundy addresses the letter plainly 'To the King', not 'to my sovereign': a sign he no longer owes allegiance.","586":"'France, amongst a fickle wavering nation' is a common English prejudice in the play.","588":"Henry argues that a red rose means no more than a Scottish crown: both are just emblems.","595":"Exeter's 'Tis much when sceptres are in children's hands' echoes Ecclesiastes 10:16, 'Woe to thee, O land, when thy king is a child.'","602":"The owl was a bird of ill omen. The General's hourglass marks the hour left of Talbot's life.","606":"'Rascal' deer were lean, inferior deer; 'in blood' means in good condition, ready to fight. 'Dear deer' is a pun.","612":"'Lowted' means made a fool of, treated like a lout.","621":"Lucy calls Henry the Fifth 'our scarce cold conqueror': the losses come within years of his death.","625":"In history Talbot was killed at Castillon in 1453, more than twenty years after Joan's death: Shakespeare brings their stories together.","641":"The father-and-son scenes of Act 4 are written mostly in rhyming couplets, giving them a ceremonial, almost ballad-like quality.","665":"The Bastard 'had the maidenhood of thy first fight': he drew first blood from John in his first battle.","667":"Daedalus of Crete made wings for himself and his son Icarus. Icarus flew too near the sun, the wax melted and he fell into the sea.","688":"Lucy's list of titles comes from the epitaph on Talbot's tomb at Rouen, as recorded in the chronicles. Alcides is Hercules.","689":"'The Turk' is the Ottoman Sultan, whose official titles were famously long.","699":"The Pope, the Holy Roman Emperor and the Earl of Armagnac all pressed for peace; the Congress of Arras met in 1435.","706":"Winchester (Henry Beaufort) became a cardinal in 1426.","713":"Winchester's 'sack this country with a mutiny' prepares for his plotting against Gloucester in 2 Henry VI.","731":"The 'monarch of the north' is the devil: the north was traditionally associated with Lucifer. Joan's 'periapts' are charms worn as amulets.","744":"Circe was the enchantress in Homer's Odyssey who turned Odysseus's men into pigs.","756":"Margaret's father Reignier (René of Anjou) held the titles King of Naples, Sicily and Jerusalem but had little real power.","765":"Suffolk was in fact married. He is reminding himself he cannot have Margaret.","769":"A dispensation was a special licence from the Pope to set aside a church law, here perhaps to end a marriage.","782":"'Quid for quo' (quid pro quo) means tit for tat.","808":"Giving up Anjou and Maine became a scandal in England and is attacked at the start of 2 Henry VI.","828":"Joan's denial of her father contradicts her own words in 1.2, 'I am by birth a shepherd's daughter.'","834":"A 'noble' was a gold coin worth a third of a pound, here the fee to the priest at his wedding.","840":"Pregnant women could not be executed until after the birth.","852":"Joan's curse looks forward to the civil war of the later Henry VI plays.","862":"The historical Treaty of Tours (1444) arranged a truce and Henry's marriage to Margaret; it did not make Charles a viceroy.","878":"A 'triumph' here is a tournament; the 'lists' are the enclosure where knights fought.","884":"'A tenth' was a tax of a tenth part of goods. In 2 Henry VI it is remembered as one of Suffolk's crimes.","888":"Paris, prince of Troy, went to Greece and carried off Helen, causing the Trojan War. Suffolk's hope to 'prosper better' is ironic: he is killed in 2 Henry VI."},"gloss":{"bruited":"reported, made famous","fabulous":"invented, untrue","riddling merchant":"fellow who talks in riddles","for the nonce":"for the occasion","contrarieties":"contradictions","sinews":"muscles, strength","abuse":"insult, offence","nice":"precise, overly fine","significants":"signs","insinuating":"ingratiating, creeping","apparell'd":"dressed","crestless":"without a coat of arms, low-born","exempt":"excluded, cut off","studious":"eager, careful","politic":"shrewd, cautious","extemporal":"unprepared, improvised","lewd":"wicked, vile","usurer":"moneylender charging interest","lascivious":"lustful","pebble stones":"pebbles","pate":"head","pledge":"token of challenge (a glove)","embrace":"hug; accept","fond":"foolish","stomachs":"tempers, pride","unready":"undressed","platforms":"plans, schemes","servitors":"servants, soldiers","Pucelle":"maid, virgin (French)","dam":"mother (of an animal)","unavoided":"unavoidable","doom":"sentence, judgement","packing":"leaving at once","'long":"because","apparent":"obvious, certain","girdled":"surrounded","Bewray'd":"revealed","condescend":"agree","surmount":"excel","plighted":"pledged","gloss":"shine; false surface","wonted":"usual","cull'd":"picked out","prevail":"succeed","progeny":"descendants, lineage","co-equal":"equal","laments":"mourning","censure":"judge","importing":"foretelling, signifying","crystal tresses":"shining tails (of comets)","consented":"agreed together, conspired","replete":"filled","effeminate":"weak, soft, unmanly","jars":"quarrels","marish":"marsh","invocate":"call upon","broils":"quarrels, civil wars","corse":"corpse","discomfiture":"defeat","flower-de-luces":"fleurs-de-lis, the French royal lilies","steeled coat":"armour","intermissive":"coming at intervals, recurring","bedew":"wet with tears","at large":"in full","enrank":"draw up in ranks","agaz'd":"staring in amazement","amain":"loudly, with full force","vaward":"vanguard, front line","dastard":"cowardly; coward","hale":"drag","weal":"welfare, state","provender":"fodder","gimmors":"gimmals, mechanical joints or gears","cheer":"face, expression","unfallible":"infallible, certain","swart":"swarthy, dark-skinned","Amazon":"warrior woman of legend","thrall":"slave, captive","recreants":"cowards, traitors","halcyon":"calm, peaceful","conveyance":"theft, underhand dealing","warrantize":"guarantee, security","proditor":"traitor","bearing-cloth":"christening robe","contumeliously":"insolently","distrain'd":"seized","espials":"spies","contumelious":"insulting","grisly":"grim, terrible","adamant":"hardest of materials","puzzel":"slut, drab","dolphin":"Dauphin (with a pun on the sea creature)","victual":"supply with food","lists":"pleases","pyramis":"pyramid","court of guard":"guardroom","quittance":"repay","baleful":"deadly, evil","trull":"whore","Hecate":"goddess of witchcraft","writhled":"wrinkled","captivate":"captured","cates":"delicacies","purblind":"half-blind","quillets":"fine distinctions, quibbles","daw":"jackdaw, a foolish bird","canker":"grub that eats blossoms","yeoman":"commoner, landowner below a gentleman","attainted":"condemned for treason, with titles forfeited","attached":"arrested","cognizance":"badge","pursuivants":"heralds, messengers","exigent":"end, extremity","pithless":"strengthless","sequestration":"imprisonment, separation","enlargement":"release","obloquy":"abuse, insult","weening":"intending, hoping","diadem":"crown","cloy'd":"sated, wearied","pernicious":"harmful","froward":"perverse, stubborn","pestiferous":"poisonous, harmful","inkhorn":"pedantic, bookish","reguerdon":"reward","disanimates":"discourages","practisants":"fellow plotters","darnel":"a weed in corn","paramours":"lovers","muleters":"mule drivers","couched":"levelled (a lance) for attack","exequies":"funeral rites","familiar":"attendant spirit","all amort":"dejected, lifeless","gleeks":"jibes","corrosive":"something that makes things worse","extirped":"rooted out","parley":"talks with an enemy under truce","coronet":"small crown worn by nobles","reguerdon'd":"rewarded","patronage":"defend, uphold","miscreant":"villain","craven":"coward","hedge-born":"low-born","swain":"peasant","superscription":"address on a letter","repugn":"resist, oppose","emulations":"rivalries","objections":"accusations","quartering":"cutting into pieces","rive":"fire, burst","due":"endue, give","peruse":"inspect","pale":"fenced enclosure","rascal":"lean, inferior deer","cornets":"companies of cavalry","lowted":"made a fool of","remiss":"negligent","sedition":"rebellion","sally":"sudden attack from a besieged town","emulation":"rivalry, envy","mickle":"great","boot":"use, advantage","lither":"yielding, soft","antic":"grotesque jester","hard-favor'd":"ugly","giglot":"wanton, loose","inhearsed":"laid in a coffin","Alcides":"Hercules","fly-blown":"covered with flies' eggs","immanity":"savagery","dalliance":"flirtation; idling","inshipp'd":"embarked","periapts":"charms, amulets","vail":"lower","spelling":"spell-casting","banning":"cursing","counterfeited":"reflected, copied","dispensation":"special licence from the Church","peevish":"silly, foolish","collop":"slice of meat; piece of one's own flesh","obstacle":"obstinate, stubborn","suborn'd":"bribed to give false evidence","drab":"slut","ratsbane":"rat poison","faggots":"bundles of firewood","forfend":"forbid","Machiavel":"cunning schemer","reflex":"reflect, shine","remorse":"pity","effeminate peace":"weak, unmanly peace","liegemen":"loyal subjects","cavil":"quibble","fruition":"enjoyment","attorneyship":"acting through agents","affects":"loves","attaint":"touched, infected","revolve":"think over","ruminate":"brood on"}};
const PARTS={"4":[[0,5,"We mourn in black; why don't we mourn in blood? Henry is dead and will never come back to life. We walk beside a wooden coffin and, with our grand presence, glorify death's shameful victory, like prisoners tied to a conqueror's chariot."],[6,10,"What! Shall we curse the unlucky planets that plotted the end of our glory? Or shall we think the cunning French are conjurers and sorcerers who, afraid of him, killed him with magic spells?"]],"9":[[0,7,"Stop, stop these quarrels and calm yourselves. Let's go to the altar. Heralds, attend us. Instead of gold, we'll offer up our weapons, since weapons are no use now Henry is dead. Future ages, expect miserable years, when babies will suck at their mothers' tear-wet eyes, our island will become a marsh of salt tears, and only women will be left to mourn the dead."],[8,12,"Henry the Fifth, I call on your spirit: make this kingdom prosper, keep it from civil wars, fight the hostile planets in the heavens! Your soul will make a far more glorious star than Julius Caesar or bright—"]],"15":[[0,8,"No treachery, but a shortage of men and money. The soldiers are muttering that here at home you keep up rival factions, and while a battle should be fought and won, you are arguing about your generals. One wants a long war that costs little; another wants to move fast but has no means to; a third thinks peace can be won by clever, smooth words, without any spending at all."],[9,12,"Wake up, wake up, English nobles! Don't let idleness tarnish your newly won honours. The French lilies have been cut from your coat of arms; half of England's shield has been hacked away."]],"26":[[0,11,"Oh no; Lord Talbot was defeated. I'll tell you the details. On the tenth of August last, as this fearsome lord was withdrawing from the siege of Orleans with barely six thousand men, he was surrounded and attacked by twenty-three thousand French. He had no time to draw up his men in ranks. He had no pikes to set in front of his archers, so instead they hammered sharp stakes pulled from hedges into the ground in a rough line to keep the cavalry from breaking in."],[12,21,"The fight went on for more than three hours, and brave Talbot performed superhuman wonders with sword and lance. He sent hundreds to hell, and nobody dared stand against him. He killed in a rage here, there and everywhere. The French cried that the devil himself was fighting; the whole army stood staring at him. His soldiers, seeing his fearless spirit, shouted \"A Talbot! A Talbot!\" and charged into the heart of the battle."],[22,32,"The victory would have been certain if Sir John Fastolfe had not acted the coward. He was in the vanguard, placed behind them to support and follow, but he ran away like a coward without striking a single blow. That caused the general disaster and slaughter: they were surrounded by enemies. A low-born Walloon, to win the Dauphin's favour, stabbed Talbot in the back with a spear, a man whom the whole of France with all its forces never dared look in the face."]],"57":[[0,6,"You look sad, and your faces are pale with fear. Has the recent defeat caused this? Don't lose heart, for help is near. I have brought a holy maid with me who, through a vision sent to her from heaven, has been chosen to break this long siege and drive the English out of France."],[7,11,"She has the gift of deep prophecy, greater than the nine sibyls of ancient Rome. She can see what has happened and what is to come. Tell me, shall I call her in? Believe me, my words are true and certain."]],"63":[[0,11,"Dauphin, I was born a shepherd's daughter, my mind untrained in any skill. Heaven and our gracious Lady have chosen to shine on my lowly state. While I was tending my young lambs, my cheeks exposed to the scorching sun, the mother of God appeared to me in a majestic vision and told me to leave my humble work and free my country from disaster. She promised her help and guaranteed success. She revealed herself in full glory,"],[12,20,"and whereas before I was dark and sunburnt, the bright rays she poured on me gave me the beauty you can see. Ask me any question you like and I'll answer without preparing. Test my courage in combat if you dare, and you'll find I go beyond what is expected of my sex. Be sure of this: you'll be lucky if you accept me as your partner in war."]],"81":[[0,3,"I am appointed to be the scourge of the English. Tonight I will certainly break the siege. Expect a late summer of calm, peaceful days now that I have joined these wars."],[4,10,"Glory is like a ripple in water, which keeps spreading wider until it spreads so far it vanishes. With Henry's death the English ripple ends; the glories it held are scattered. Now I am like that proud, triumphant ship that carried Caesar and his good fortune together."]],"133":[[0,8,"But now you won't miss. Do as I say. I'm the chief gunner of this town; I must do something to win favour. The Prince's spies have told me that the English, dug in close in the suburbs, are in the habit of looking over the city through a secret iron grating in that tower, to work out how best to attack us with gunfire or assault."],[9,15,"To stop this nuisance, I've aimed a cannon at it, and for three days now I've watched to see if I could spot them. Now you keep watch, for I can't stay any longer. If you see anyone, run and tell me; you'll find me at the governor's."]],"139":[[0,7,"The Duke of Bedford had a prisoner called the brave Lord Ponton de Santrailles; I was exchanged and ransomed for him. Once, to insult me, they wanted to swap me for a far lower-ranking soldier, which I scornfully refused, asking for death rather than be valued so cheaply. In the end I was released as I wanted."],[8,10,"But oh, treacherous Fastolfe wounds my heart! I would kill him with my bare hands if I had him in my power now."]],"141":[[0,9,"With mockery, scorn and insulting taunts. They put me on show in the open market-place as a public spectacle for everyone. \"Here,\" they said, \"is the terror of the French, the bogeyman who frightens our children.\" Then I broke away from the officers who led me and dug stones out of the ground with my nails to throw at the people watching my shame. My grim face made others run; nobody dared come near for fear of instant death."],[10,17,"They didn't think I was safe even behind iron walls; the fear of my name spread so far among them that they believed I could break steel bars and kick unbreakable posts to pieces. So I had a guard of picked marksmen who walked around me every minute, and if I so much as got out of bed they were ready to shoot me through the heart."]],"150":[[0,9,"What disaster is this that has suddenly struck us? Speak, Salisbury, if you can speak at all. How are you, model of all soldiers? One of your eyes and the side of your face blown off! Cursed tower! Cursed deadly hand that caused this sad tragedy! Salisbury won thirteen battles; he trained Henry the Fifth in war. While any trumpet sounded or drum beat, his sword never stopped striking on the battlefield."],[10,17,"Are you still alive, Salisbury? Though your voice fails, you have one eye left to look to heaven for mercy. The sun sees the whole world with one eye. Heaven, show mercy to no one alive if you deny it to Salisbury! Carry his body away; I'll help bury it. Sir Thomas Gargrave, are you alive? Speak to Talbot; no, look up at him."],[18,25,"Salisbury, take comfort in this: you won't die while— He beckons and smiles at me, as if to say, \"When I am dead and gone, remember to take revenge on the French for me.\" Plantagenet, I will; and like Nero I'll play the lute while watching the towns burn. Your name alone will bring misery to France."]],"183":[[0,10,"It's Joan, not us, who won the day, and for that I'll share my crown with her, and all the priests and friars in my kingdom will sing her praises forever in processions. I'll build her a grander pyramid than Rhodope's in Memphis. In memory of her, when she is dead, her ashes will be carried at great festivals before the kings and queens of France in an urn more precious than the jewelled chest of Darius."],[11,14,"We'll no longer pray to Saint Denis; Joan la Pucelle will be the saint of France. Come in, and let's feast like kings after this golden day of victory."]],"229":[[0,5,"Bring out the body of old Salisbury and set it up here in the market-place, the very centre of this cursed town. Now I have kept my vow to his soul: for every drop of blood drawn from him, at least five Frenchmen have died tonight."],[6,13,"And so that future ages may see what destruction happened to avenge him, I'll build a tomb in their main church where his body will be buried, and on it, for everyone to read, will be carved the sack of Orleans, the treacherous way he died and what a terror he had been to France."],[14,17,"But, lords, in all our bloody slaughter I'm surprised we didn't meet his Grace the Dauphin, his new champion, the virtuous Joan of Arc, or any of his treacherous allies."]],"252":[[0,3,"Is this the scourge of France? Is this the Talbot so feared abroad that mothers quiet their babies with his name? I see the reports are made up and false."],[4,9,"I thought I would see some Hercules, a second Hector, with a grim face and big, powerful limbs. But this is a child, a feeble dwarf! This weak, wrinkled shrimp can't possibly strike such terror into his enemies."]],"324":[[0,4,"This stain they accuse your family of will be wiped out at the next parliament, called to make a truce between Winchester and Gloucester; and if you are not made Duke of York then, I won't live to be called Warwick."],[5,11,"Meanwhile, as a sign of my love for you against proud Somerset and William Pole, I'll wear this rose on your side. And here I make a prophecy: this quarrel today, grown into this faction in the Temple garden, will send a thousand souls to death and endless night between the red rose and the white."]],"332":[[0,6,"Kind keepers of my weak, failing old age, let dying Mortimer rest here. My limbs feel like a man's just pulled off the rack, after my long imprisonment; and these grey hairs, the messengers of death, aged like Nestor's in an age of trouble, show that Edmund Mortimer's end is near."],[7,16,"These eyes, like lamps whose oil is used up, grow dim as they come to their end. My weak shoulders are bowed with heavy grief, and my feeble arms are like a withered vine drooping its dried-up branches to the ground. Yet these feet, numb and too weak to hold up this lump of clay, are swift with desire for a grave, knowing I have no other comfort. But tell me, keeper, will my nephew come?"]],"334":[[0,6,"Enough: then my soul will be satisfied. Poor gentleman! His wrongs equal mine. Ever since Henry of Monmouth began to reign (before his glory I was a great soldier), I have been kept in this hateful confinement, and ever since then Richard has been kept down, robbed of honour and inheritance."],[7,11,"But now Death, who settles all despair, the just and kind judge of men's miseries, releases me from here with sweet freedom. I wish his troubles were over too, so that he could recover what was lost."]],"340":[[0,7,"First, lean your old back against my arm, and like that I'll tell you what is troubling me. Today, while arguing a case, some words arose between Somerset and me, and among them he let his tongue run wild and taunted me with my father's death. That insult silenced me, or I would have paid him back in kind."],[8,11,"So, good uncle, for my father's sake, in honour of a true Plantagenet and for the sake of our family ties, tell me why my father, the Earl of Cambridge, lost his head."]],"343":[[0,8,"I will, if my fading breath allows and death does not come before my story is told. Henry the Fourth, grandfather of this king, deposed his cousin Richard, son of Edward the Black Prince, the eldest son and lawful heir of King Edward, the third of that name. During his reign the Percys of the north, finding his seizure of the crown most unjust, tried to put me on the throne."],[9,20,"What moved these warlike lords to this was that, with young King Richard removed and leaving no children of his own, I was next in line by birth and descent. Through my mother I am descended from Lionel Duke of Clarence, the third son of King Edward the Third, whereas Henry traces his descent from John of Gaunt, only the fourth son of that heroic line. But note this: in that bold, great attempt to put the rightful heir on the throne, I lost my freedom and they lost their lives."],[21,31,"Long after this, when Henry the Fifth reigned after his father Bolingbroke, your father, the Earl of Cambridge, descended from the famous Edmund Langley, Duke of York, who had married my sister, your mother, again took pity on my hard suffering and raised an army, hoping to free me and put me on the throne. But like the rest, that noble earl fell and was beheaded. So the Mortimers, in whom the right to the crown rested, were crushed."]],"358":[[0,8,"Arrogant priest! This place demands my patience, or you'd find out what it means to dishonour me. Don't think that because I set out your vile, outrageous crimes in writing, I invented them or can't repeat word for word what I wrote. No, churchman: your shameless wickedness, your vile, poisonous, quarrelsome tricks are such that even babies chatter about your pride."],[9,15,"You are a most harmful moneylender, stubborn by nature, an enemy of peace, lustful and loose-living more than is fitting for a man of your calling and rank. As for your treachery, what's more obvious than the fact that you set a trap to kill me, both at London Bridge and at the Tower?"],[16,18,"Besides, I fear that if your thoughts were examined closely, the King, your sovereign, would not be entirely safe from the envious hatred of your swollen heart."]],"359":[[0,7,"Gloucester, I defy you. Lords, be good enough to hear my reply. If I were greedy, ambitious or wicked, as he claims, how is it that I'm so poor? And how is it that I don't try to promote or raise myself, but stay in my usual calling? As for quarrelling, who prefers peace more than I do, unless I'm provoked?"],[8,14,"No, my good lords, that isn't what offends him; that isn't what has angered the Duke. It's that nobody should rule but him, nobody but him should be close to the King. That's what breeds thunder in his chest and makes him roar out these accusations. But he'll learn I am as good—"]],"381":[[0,9,"Oh, my good lords, and virtuous Henry, have pity on the city of London, pity us! The bishop's and the Duke of Gloucester's men, recently forbidden to carry any weapons, have filled their pockets with pebbles and, ganging up on opposite sides, pelt each other's heads so hard that many have had their dizzy brains knocked out. Windows are smashed in every street, and we're forced by fear to shut our shops."]],"423":[[0,7,"Yes, we may march in England or in France without seeing what is likely to follow. This recent quarrel between the lords is burning under the false ashes of pretended love and will finally burst into flame. Just as infected limbs rot slowly until bones, flesh and sinews fall away, so this low, envious quarrel will spread."],[8,14,"And now I fear that deadly prophecy which in the time of Henry the Fifth was on the lips of every baby: that Henry born at Monmouth would win everything, and Henry born at Windsor would lose everything. It's so obviously coming true that Exeter wishes his life may end before that unlucky time."]],"466":[[0,4,"And we'll be there too before long, or else let disgrace be Talbot's greatest fame! Burgundy, swear by the honour of your family, spurred on by the public wrongs you've suffered in France, either to get the town back or die."],[5,9,"And I swear, as surely as English Henry lives, as surely as his father was the conqueror here, and as surely as great Lionheart's heart was buried in this recently betrayed town, that I'll take the town or die."]],"516":[[0,7,"Look at your country, look at fertile France, and see the cities and towns spoiled by the destruction of the cruel enemy. As a mother looks at her little baby when death closes its tender dying eyes, see, see the wasting sickness of France. Look at the wounds, the most unnatural wounds, that you yourself have given her sorrowful breast."],[8,13,"Oh, turn your sharp sword another way. Strike those who hurt her, and don't hurt those who help. One drop of blood drawn from your country's breast should grieve you more than rivers of foreign blood. So come back with a flood of tears and wash away your country's stains."]],"518":[[0,7,"Besides, all the French and France itself cry out against you, doubting your birth and legitimacy. Who are you joining but an arrogant nation that will only trust you for profit? When Talbot has once got a foothold in France and made you his tool for harm, who but English Henry will be lord, and you'll be thrown out like a runaway?"],[8,15,"Let's remember, and just take this as proof: wasn't the Duke of Orleans your enemy? And wasn't he a prisoner in England? But when they heard he was your enemy, they set him free without his ransom being paid, in spite of Burgundy and all his friends. See, then, you are fighting against your own countrymen and joining those who will be your butchers."],[16,17,"Come, come back; come back, you wandering lord. Charles and the rest will take you in their arms."]],"528":[[0,3,"My gracious Prince and honourable lords, hearing you had arrived in this kingdom, I have paused my wars for a while to pay my respects to my King."],[4,11,"As a sign of which, this arm, which has won back for you fifty fortresses, twelve cities and seven strong walled towns, besides five hundred important prisoners, lets its sword fall at your Highness's feet and, with humble, loyal heart, gives the glory of its conquests first to my God and next to your Grace. (Kneels)"]],"531":[[0,8,"Welcome, brave captain and victorious lord! When I was young (though I'm not old yet), I remember my father saying a braver champion never held a sword. We have long been sure of your loyalty, your faithful service and your hard work in war; yet you have never had a reward from us, or even been repaid with thanks, because until now we never saw your face."],[9,11,"So stand up, and for these great services we here make you Earl of Shrewsbury; and take your place at our coronation."]],"549":[[0,4,"Shame on the Duke of Burgundy and on you! I swore, low knight, that when I met you next I'd tear the garter from your coward's leg (tearing it off), and I have done it, because you were unworthily given that high rank."],[5,13,"Forgive me, princely Henry, and the rest of you. This coward, at the battle of Patay, when I had only six thousand men and the French outnumbered us almost ten to one, ran away like a \"trusty\" squire before we even met or a blow was struck. In that attack we lost twelve hundred men; I and several other gentlemen were surprised and taken prisoner."],[14,16,"So judge, great lords, whether I have done wrong, and whether such cowards should wear this badge of knighthood or not."]],"551":[[0,5,"When this order was first founded, my lords, Knights of the Garter were of noble birth, brave and virtuous, full of high courage, men who had won respect in the wars, not afraid of death or shrinking from hardship, but always determined in the greatest dangers."],[6,11,"So anyone who doesn't have these qualities only steals the sacred name of knight, defiling this most honourable order, and should, if I were fit to judge, be completely stripped of rank, like a low-born peasant who dares to boast of noble blood."]],"555":[[0,5,"What does his Grace mean by changing the way he addresses me? Nothing more than, plain and blunt, \"To the King!\" Has he forgotten he's his king? Or does this rude address mean some change in his loyalty?"],[6,11,"What's here? (Reads) \"For special reasons, moved by pity for my country's ruin and by the pitiful complaints of those your oppression preys on, I have abandoned your harmful party and joined Charles, the rightful King of France.\""],[12,14,"Oh, monstrous treachery! Can it be that such false, deceitful cunning should be found in alliance, friendship and sworn oaths?"]],"574":[[0,8,"Crossing the sea from England to France, this fellow here, with his spiteful, sneering tongue, taunted me about the rose I wear, saying the blood-red colour of its petals stood for my master's blushing cheeks when he stubbornly denied the truth in a certain legal question argued between him and the Duke of York; and other vile and shameful things."],[9,11,"To answer that rude insult and defend my lord's honour, I ask for my right under the law of arms."]],"584":[[0,4,"Agree to that! Damn your quarrel! And may you perish with your bold chatter! Arrogant servants, aren't you ashamed to trouble and disturb the King and us with this rude, noisy outrage?"],[5,9,"And you, my lords, I don't think you do well to put up with their wrong-headed complaints, much less to take the chance from their mouths to start a mutiny among yourselves. Let me persuade you to take a better course."]],"586":[[0,2,"Come here, you who want to fight. From now on, as you value our favour, I order you to forget this quarrel and its cause completely."],[3,13,"And you, my lords, remember where we are: in France, among a fickle, unreliable nation. If they see disagreement in our faces and that we're quarrelling among ourselves, how their resentful hearts will be stirred to wilful disobedience and rebellion! Besides, what disgrace will follow when foreign princes hear that King Henry's lords and chief nobles destroyed themselves and lost the kingdom of France over a trifle, a thing of no importance!"],[14,18,"Oh, think of my father's conquests and my young age, and let's not throw away for a trifle what was bought with blood! Let me be the judge in this uncertain quarrel. I see no reason, if I wear this rose (putting on a red rose),"]],"588":[[0,8,"why anyone should suspect that I lean more towards Somerset than York. Both are my relatives, and I love them both. They might just as well blame me for my crown because, of course, the King of Scots is crowned too. But your own good sense can persuade you better than I can instruct or teach; so, as we came here in peace, let's keep on in peace and love."],[9,15,"Cousin of York, we appoint your Grace our Regent in these parts of France. And, my good Lord of Somerset, join your cavalry with his foot soldiers, and like true subjects and sons of your ancestors, go cheerfully together and spend your angry temper on your enemies."],[16,20,"I, the Lord Protector and the rest will return to Calais after a short rest, and from there to England, where I hope before long that your victories will present me with Charles, Alencon and that traitorous mob."]],"595":[[0,4,"You did well, Richard, to keep quiet; for if the feelings of your heart had burst out, I fear we would have seen revealed there more bitter spite and more furious, raging quarrels than can yet be imagined."],[5,12,"But in any case, no simple man who sees this clashing discord among the nobles, this shoving at each other in court, this factional fighting among their followers, could fail to see that it foretells some evil outcome. It's bad enough when a child holds the sceptre, but worse when envy breeds unnatural division. That's where ruin comes; that's where chaos begins."]],"601":[[0,5,"Captains, English John Talbot calls you out, a soldier in the service of Harry, King of England, and this is what he wants: open your city gates, submit to us, call my king your king and pay him homage as obedient subjects, and I'll withdraw myself and my bloody army."],[6,11,"But if you frown on this offer of peace, you provoke the fury of my three servants, thin famine, cutting steel and climbing fire, who will in a moment level your grand, sky-defying towers with the earth if you reject their offer of love."]],"602":[[0,5,"You ominous, fearful owl of death, our nation's terror and its bloody scourge! The end of your tyranny is coming. You can't get in to us except through death, for I declare we're well fortified and strong enough to come out and fight."],[6,15,"If you retreat, the Dauphin, well equipped, is waiting with the traps of war to catch you. There are troops set on both sides of you to wall you off from escape, and there's no way you can turn for help without death facing you with certain ruin and pale destruction meeting you face to face. Ten thousand French have sworn on the sacrament to fire their deadly cannon at no Christian soul but English Talbot."],[16,23,"Look, there you stand, a living brave man of unbeatable, unconquered spirit! This is the last praise I, your enemy, will give you; for before this hourglass, which is just starting to run, finishes its sandy hour, these eyes that see you now healthy will see you withered, bloody, pale and dead."]],"606":[[0,5,"He's not making it up; I hear the enemy. Out, some light horsemen, and scout their flanks. Oh, careless, thoughtless discipline! How we are penned and fenced in, a little herd of England's timid deer, bewildered by a yelping pack of French dogs!"],[6,14,"If we are English deer, then let us be in full vigour, not like weak deer that fall at a nip, but rather like angry, desperate stags; turn on the bloody hounds with heads of steel and make the cowards keep their distance. Let every man sell his life as dearly as I sell mine, and they'll find us dear deer, my friends. God and Saint George, Talbot and England's right, bless our flags in this dangerous fight!"]],"629":[[0,8,"Where from, my lord? From Lord Talbot, betrayed and sold, who, ringed around with bold danger, calls for noble York and Somerset to beat back attacking death from his weak troops. And while that honourable captain drips bloody sweat from his war-weary limbs, holding out and looking for rescue, you, his false hopes, the trustees of England's honour, keep your distance in worthless rivalry."],[9,15,"Don't let your private quarrel keep away the reinforcements raised to help him, while he, a famous, noble gentleman, gives up his life against overwhelming odds. The Bastard of Orleans, Charles, Burgundy, Alencon and Reignier surround him, and Talbot dies through your failure."]],"641":[[0,4,"Oh, young John Talbot! I sent for you to teach you the tactics of war, so that Talbot's name might live again in you when dried-up old age and weak, useless limbs bring your father to his invalid chair."],[5,10,"But oh, evil and ill-omened stars! Now you have come to a feast of death, a terrible and unavoidable danger. So, dear boy, get on my fastest horse, and I'll show you how to escape by fleeing at once. Come, don't delay; go."]],"646":[[0,4,"Then let me stay, and you run, father. Your loss is great, so you should look after yourself; my worth is unknown, so no loss will be felt in me. The French can't boast much about my death; they will about yours, for all hopes rest in you."],[5,12,"Running away can't stain the honour you have won, but it will stain mine, since I've done no great deed. Everyone will swear you ran for good tactical reasons; but if I give way, they'll say it was from fear. There's no hope that I'll ever stand firm if I shrink and run away in my first hour. Here on my knee I beg for death rather than life saved with disgrace."]],"665":[[0,5,"When your sword struck sparks from the Dauphin's helmet, it warmed your father's heart with proud desire for bold victory. Then my heavy old age, quickened with young fire and warlike rage, beat down Alencon, Orleans and Burgundy and rescued you from the pride of France."],[6,16,"The angry Bastard of Orleans, who drew blood from you, my boy, and had the first taste of your first fight, I soon met, and exchanging blows I quickly spilled some of his bastard blood, and insulted him like this: \"I spill your tainted, low, illegitimate blood, mean and worthless, in return for my pure blood, which you forced from Talbot, my brave boy.\" Here, as I meant to kill the Bastard, strong help came to rescue him. Speak, you who are your father's worry:"],[17,27,"aren't you tired, John? How are you? Will you leave the battle now, boy, and run, now that you're confirmed as the son of knighthood? Run, to avenge my death when I am dead. The help of one man is little use to me. Oh, I know well it's too foolish to risk all our lives in one small boat! If I don't die today from French rage, I'll die tomorrow from great age. They gain nothing if I stay; it only shortens my life by a day."],[28,31,"In you die your mother, our family's name, the revenge for my death, your youth and England's fame. We risk all these and more if you stay; all these are saved if you'll run away."]],"666":[[0,7,"The sword of Orleans didn't make me suffer; these words of yours draw blood from my heart. Rather than young Talbot run from old Talbot for that advantage, bought with such shame, to save a worthless life and kill bright fame, may the cowardly horse that carries me fall and die! And may I be compared to the peasant boys of France, mocked by shame and a target for bad luck!"],[8,11,"Surely, by all the glory you have won, if I run, I'm not Talbot's son. So say no more about running; it's no use. If I'm Talbot's son, I'll die at Talbot's feet."]],"671":[[0,7,"Where is my other life? My own is gone. Oh, where's young Talbot? Where is brave John? Triumphant Death, stained with my captivity, young Talbot's courage makes me smile at you. When he saw me sink to my knee, he waved his bloody sword over me and, like a hungry lion, began rough deeds of rage and fierce impatience."],[8,15,"But when my angry guardian stood alone, watching over my fall and attacked by no one, dizzy fury and great rage of heart suddenly made him leap from my side into the crowded ranks of the French. And in that sea of blood my boy drowned his soaring spirit, and there died, my Icarus, my blossom, in his pride."]],"674":[[0,4,"You grinning clown Death, who laughs at us here in scorn, soon, coupled in everlasting bonds, two Talbots, flying through the yielding sky, will escape from your insulting tyranny and from death, in spite of you."],[5,10,"Oh you, whose wounds make ugly death look handsome, speak to your father before you breathe your last! Defy death by speaking, whether he likes it or not; imagine he's a Frenchman and your enemy. Poor boy! I think he smiles, as if to say that if death had been French, death would have died today."],[11,14,"Come, come and lay him in his father's arms. My spirit can't bear these hurts any longer. Soldiers, goodbye! I have what I wanted: now my old arms are young John Talbot's grave."]],"688":[[0,11,"Where's the great Hercules of the battlefield, brave Lord Talbot, Earl of Shrewsbury, made great Earl of Wexford, Waterford and Valence for his outstanding success in war; Lord Talbot of Goodrich and Urchinfield, Lord Strange of Blackmere, Lord Verdun of Alton, Lord Cromwell of Wingfield, Lord Furnival of Sheffield, the three-times victorious Lord of Falconbridge; Knight of the noble Order of Saint George, worthy Saint Michael and the Golden Fleece; great Marshal to Henry the Sixth in all his wars in France?"]],"690":[[0,7,"Is Talbot killed, the only scourge of the French, your kingdom's terror and dark goddess of vengeance? Oh, if only my eyeballs were turned into bullets so that I could shoot them in rage at your faces! Oh, if only I could call these dead back to life! It would be enough to frighten the whole of France. If only his portrait were left here among you, it would terrify the proudest of you."],[8,9,"Give me their bodies, so I can carry them away and bury them as their worth deserves."]],"759":[[0,4,"Oh, stay! I don't have the power to let her go. My hand would free her, but my heart says no. As the sun plays on glassy streams, sparkling with a second, reflected beam, so this gorgeous beauty seems to my eyes."],[5,11,"I'd like to woo her, but I don't dare speak. I'll call for pen and ink and write my thoughts. Shame, de la Pole! Don't belittle yourself. Haven't you got a tongue? Isn't she here? Will you be scared by the sight of a woman? Yes, beauty's princely majesty is such that it ties the tongue and dulls the senses."]],"834":[[0,2,"It's true, I gave the priest a coin the morning I married her mother. Kneel down and take my blessing, my good girl."],[3,10,"Won't you bow? Then cursed be the hour you were born! I wish the milk your mother gave you when you sucked her breast had been rat poison, for your sake! Or else, when you looked after my lambs in the field, I wish some hungry wolf had eaten you! Do you deny your father, cursed slut? Oh, burn her, burn her! Hanging's too good for her."]],"837":[[0,5,"First let me tell you whom you have condemned: not someone fathered by a shepherd peasant, but one descended from kings; virtuous and holy, chosen from above by the inspiration of heavenly grace to work great miracles on earth."],[6,12,"I never had anything to do with evil spirits. But you, who are filthy with your lusts, stained with the blameless blood of innocent people, corrupt and infected with a thousand vices, because you lack the grace others have, immediately judge it impossible to perform wonders except with the help of devils."],[13,17,"No, you are mistaken! Joan of Arc has been a virgin from her earliest childhood, chaste and pure even in thought, and her virgin blood, cruelly spilled like this, will cry out for vengeance at the gates of heaven."]],"857":[[0,5,"Has all our labour come to this? After the slaughter of so many lords, captains, gentlemen and soldiers, who have fallen in this quarrel and given their bodies for their country's good, are we going to agree to a weak, womanish peace at last?"],[6,10,"Haven't we lost most of the towns our great ancestors conquered, through treason, lies and treachery? Oh, Warwick, Warwick! I foresee with grief the complete loss of the whole kingdom of France."]],"862":[[0,9,"Charles, and the rest of you, these are the terms: since King Henry agrees, out of pure pity and mercy, to free your country from painful war and let you breathe in fruitful peace, you will become loyal subjects of his crown. And, Charles, on condition that you swear to pay him tribute and submit to him, you'll be made viceroy under him and still enjoy royal rank."]],"875":[[0,5,"Pooh, my good lord, this surface account is only an introduction to the praise she deserves. The chief perfections of that lovely lady, if I had the skill to describe them, would fill a book of enchanting lines able to delight the dullest mind."],[6,11,"And what's more, she isn't so divine, so full of every choice delight, that she isn't content, with humble modesty, to be at your command: command, I mean, in virtuous matters, to love and honour Henry as her lord."]],"883":[[0,8,"A dowry, my lords! Don't disgrace your king by making him so low, mean and poor that he chooses for money and not for true love. Henry can make his queen rich; he doesn't need to look for a queen to make him rich. That's how worthless peasants bargain for their wives, like traders for oxen, sheep or horses. Marriage is too important to be arranged by agents."],[9,17,"Not whom we want, but whom his Grace loves, must share his marriage bed. And so, lords, since he loves her most, that reason binds us above all others to think she should be preferred. For what is forced marriage but a hell, a lifetime of quarrelling and constant strife? Whereas the opposite brings happiness and is a model of heavenly peace."],[18,30,"Whom should we match with Henry, a king, but Margaret, the daughter of a king? Her matchless looks, together with her birth, prove her fit for no one but a king. Her brave courage and fearless spirit, greater than is commonly seen in women, will fulfil our hope for a king's heir; for Henry, son of a conqueror, is likely to father more conquerors if he is joined in love with a lady as determined as fair Margaret. So give way, my lords, and agree with me here that Margaret shall be queen, and no one but her."]],"884":[[0,7,"Whether it's the power of your report, my noble Lord of Suffolk, or because my young heart has never yet been touched by any burning love, I can't tell; but I am sure of this: I feel such sharp conflict in my heart, such fierce alarms of hope and fear, that I'm sick with the turmoil of my thoughts."],[8,16,"So take ship; hurry to France, my lord. Agree to any terms, and make sure Lady Margaret agrees to cross the sea to England and be crowned King Henry's faithful and anointed queen. For your expenses and costs, collect a tax of a tenth from the people. Go, I say; for until you return I'll be troubled by a thousand worries."],[17,22,"And you, good uncle, put away all offence. If you judge me by what you were when young, not by what you are now, I know it will excuse this sudden carrying out of my wishes. And so take me somewhere away from company where I can turn over and brood on my grief."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"faction":["Faction and division","Gloucester against Winchester, York against Somerset, white rose against red: England tears itself apart."],"honour":["Honour, chivalry and fame","Talbot's name, the Garter, John Talbot's choice and the meaning of a knight."],"women":["Women and power","Joan, the Countess of Auvergne and Margaret: women who threaten, trap and charm English men."],"witch":["Saints, witches and the supernatural","Is Joan sent by heaven or by hell? Visions, fiends, prophecy and omens."],"kingship":["Kingship and the child king","A dead hero-king, a gentle boy who cannot rule his nobles, and the men who would rule for him."],"legacy":["Fathers, sons and inheritance","Henry the Fifth's legacy, Talbot and his son, Mortimer's claim passed to York."],"war":["War and the loss of France","Sieges, sudden reversals, betrayals and the slow loss of Henry the Fifth's conquests."],"nation":["England and France","National pride, national insults and what makes an Englishman or a Frenchman."]},"quotes":[{"id":"2","who":"BEDFORD","text":"Hung be the heavens with black, yield day to night!","themes":["kingship","legacy"],"note":"The play's first line turns a funeral into a cosmic catastrophe."},{"id":"2","who":"BEDFORD","text":"King Henry the Fifth, too famous to live long!","themes":["kingship","legacy"],"note":"Fame and early death are linked from the start."},{"id":"3","who":"GLOUCESTER","text":"England ne'er had a king until his time.","themes":["kingship","legacy"],"note":"Henry V is the standard every later ruler fails."},{"id":"3","who":"GLOUCESTER","text":"He ne'er lift up his hand but conquered.","themes":["kingship","war"],"note":"The dead King as pure conquest."},{"id":"4","who":"EXETER","text":"We mourn in black: why mourn we not in blood?","themes":["war","faction"],"note":"Grief turns at once to a call for violence."},{"id":"4","who":"EXETER","text":"Or shall we think the subtle-witted French Conjurers and sorcerers, that afraid of him By magic verses have contriv'd his end?","themes":["witch","nation"],"note":"The English first explain defeat by French witchcraft, before Joan appears."},{"id":"6","who":"GLOUCESTER","text":"None do you like but an effeminate prince, Whom, like a school-boy, you may over-awe.","themes":["kingship","faction"],"note":"Gloucester accuses the Church of wanting a weak king to control."},{"id":"7","who":"WINCHESTER","text":"Thy wife is proud; she holdeth thee in awe,","themes":["women","faction"],"note":"Even at the funeral the quarrel becomes personal and touches on women's power."},{"id":"9","who":"BEDFORD","text":"Our isle be made a marish of salt tears, And none but women left to wail the dead.","themes":["war","legacy"],"note":"Bedford prophesies a ruined England."},{"id":"9","who":"BEDFORD","text":"Prosper this realm, keep it from civil broils,","themes":["faction","kingship"],"note":"The prayer that the play will show unanswered."},{"id":"15","who":"MESSENGER","text":"No treachery; but want of men and money.","themes":["war","faction"],"note":"The messenger refuses the comfort of blaming treachery."},{"id":"15","who":"MESSENGER","text":"That here you maintain several factions,","themes":["faction","war"],"note":"The link between division at home and defeat abroad."},{"id":"15","who":"MESSENGER","text":"Awake, awake, English nobility!","themes":["honour","nation"],"note":"A call to the nobles' honour and duty."},{"id":"15","who":"MESSENGER","text":"Cropp'd are the flower-de-luces in your arms; Of England's coat one half is cut away.","themes":["war","nation"],"note":"Heraldry shows the loss: France is half the royal arms."},{"id":"21","who":"GLOUCESTER","text":"We will not fly, but to our enemies' throats.","themes":["war","honour"],"note":"English defiance in the face of disaster."},{"id":"26","who":"MESSENGER","text":"The French exclaim'd, the devil was in arms;","themes":["witch","honour"],"note":"The French see the devil in Talbot just as the English will in Joan."},{"id":"26","who":"MESSENGER","text":"If Sir John Fastolfe had not play'd the coward.","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"Cowardice, not French strength, causes the first defeat."},{"id":"26","who":"MESSENGER","text":"Thrust Talbot with a spear into the back;","themes":["honour","nation"],"note":"The cowardly blow from behind, an image of betrayal."},{"id":"38","who":"WINCHESTER","text":"But long I will not be Jack out of office:","themes":["faction","kingship"],"note":"Winchester's ambition revealed alone on stage."},{"id":"38","who":"WINCHESTER","text":"The King from Eltham I intend to steal, And sit at chiefest stern of public weal.","themes":["kingship","faction"],"note":"He wants to steer the state through the child King."},{"id":"42","who":"CHARLES","text":"Mars his true moving, even as in the heavens So in the earth, to this day is not known:","themes":["war"],"note":"Fortune in war is unpredictable: the play's pattern of reversals."},{"id":"50","who":"ALENCON","text":"For none but Samsons and Goliases It sendeth forth to skirmish.","themes":["nation","honour"],"note":"Even the French admit English courage."},{"id":"57","who":"BASTARD","text":"A holy maid hither with me I bring,","themes":["witch","women"],"note":"Joan is introduced as holy."},{"id":"61","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Be not amazed, there's nothing hid from me.","themes":["witch","women"],"note":"Joan's first act is to see through a deception."},{"id":"63","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Dauphin, I am by birth a shepherd's daughter,","themes":["women","legacy"],"note":"Her humble birth, which she later denies."},{"id":"63","who":"PUCELLE","text":"God's mother deigned to appear to me,","themes":["witch","women"],"note":"Her claim of a divine vision."},{"id":"63","who":"PUCELLE","text":"And free my country from calamity:","themes":["nation","witch"],"note":"Joan as French patriot."},{"id":"63","who":"PUCELLE","text":"And thou shalt find that I exceed my sex.","themes":["women"],"note":"Joan claims to go beyond what a woman is allowed to be."},{"id":"67","who":"PUCELLE","text":"And while I live, I'll ne'er fly from a man.","themes":["women","honour"],"note":"A double meaning: brave in battle and in love."},{"id":"69","who":"CHARLES","text":"Stay, stay thy hands; thou art an Amazon, And fightest with the sword of Deborah.","themes":["women","witch"],"note":"Charles reaches for classical and biblical warrior women."},{"id":"72","who":"PUCELLE","text":"I must not yield to any rites of love, For my profession's sacred from above:","themes":["women","witch"],"note":"Joan insists on her virginity, the basis of her holiness."},{"id":"75","who":"ALENCON","text":"Doubtless he shrives this woman to her smock;","themes":["women"],"note":"Bawdy mockery undercuts the holy maid."},{"id":"81","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Assign'd am I to be the English scourge.","themes":["witch","nation"],"note":"Joan defines herself against the English."},{"id":"81","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Glory is like a circle in the water, Which never ceaseth to enlarge itself Till by broad spreading it disperse to nought.","themes":["legacy","war"],"note":"A famous image of how glory, like Henry V's, spreads and vanishes."},{"id":"81","who":"PUCELLE","text":"With Henry's death the English circle ends;","themes":["legacy","kingship"],"note":"Joan sees the dead King's legacy dissolving."},{"id":"82","who":"CHARLES","text":"Bright star of Venus, fall'n down on the earth,","themes":["women","witch"],"note":"Charles worships her as goddess as much as saint."},{"id":"89","who":"GLOUCESTER","text":"Since Henry's death, I fear, there is conveyance.","themes":["faction","kingship"],"note":"Suspicion of theft at the heart of the state."},{"id":"95","who":"GLOUCESTER","text":"There's none protector of the realm but I.","themes":["faction","kingship"],"note":"Gloucester insists on his authority."},{"id":"104","who":"GLOUCESTER","text":"Peel'd priest, dost thou command me to be shut out?","themes":["faction"],"note":"Insult to a churchman's tonsure."},{"id":"105","who":"WINCHESTER","text":"I do, thou most usurping proditor, And not protector, of the king or realm.","themes":["faction","kingship"],"note":"Winchester calls the Protector a usurper."},{"id":"107","who":"WINCHESTER","text":"This be Damascus, be thou cursed Cain,","themes":["faction","legacy"],"note":"Civil strife as the first murder, brother against brother."},{"id":"112","who":"GLOUCESTER","text":"Thee I'll chase hence, thou wolf in sheep's array.","themes":["faction"],"note":"The churchman as a hypocrite."},{"id":"116","who":"WINCHESTER","text":"To crown himself king and suppress the prince.","themes":["kingship","faction"],"note":"Each lord accuses the other of plotting against the King."},{"id":"127","who":"MAYOR","text":"Good God, these nobles should such stomachs bear!","themes":["faction"],"note":"An ordinary citizen's view of the lords' pride."},{"id":"150","who":"TALBOT","text":"How farest thou, mirror of all martial men?","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"Salisbury as the model soldier."},{"id":"156","who":"TALBOT","text":"Pucelle or puzzel, dolphin or dogfish,","themes":["women","nation"],"note":"Talbot's wordplay degrades both French leaders."},{"id":"156","who":"TALBOT","text":"Your hearts I'll stamp out with my horse's heels, And make a quagmire of your mingled brains.","themes":["war","nation"],"note":"The brutality of Talbot's vengeance."},{"id":"160","who":"TALBOT","text":"A woman clad in armour chaseth them.","themes":["women","war"],"note":"The shock of a woman leading an army."},{"id":"162","who":"TALBOT","text":"Blood will I draw on thee, thou art a witch,","themes":["witch"],"note":"Drawing a witch's blood was thought to break her power."},{"id":"167","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Talbot, farewell; thy hour is not yet come:","themes":["witch","war"],"note":"Joan seems to know the future, and spares him."},{"id":"171","who":"TALBOT","text":"A witch, by fear, not force, like Hannibal, Drives back our troops and conquers as she lists.","themes":["witch","war"],"note":"Defeat explained as witchcraft."},{"id":"171","who":"TALBOT","text":"They call'd us for our fierceness English dogs; Now, like to whelps, we crying run away.","themes":["nation","honour"],"note":"National pride turned to shame."},{"id":"173","who":"TALBOT","text":"Or tear the lions out of England's coat;","themes":["nation","honour"],"note":"Cowardice betrays the national symbol."},{"id":"175","who":"TALBOT","text":"O, would I were to die with Salisbury! The shame hereof will make me hide my head.","themes":["honour"],"note":"Shame is worse than death to Talbot."},{"id":"179","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Thus Joan la Pucelle hath perform'd her word.","themes":["witch","women"],"note":"Joan's promise kept."},{"id":"180","who":"CHARLES","text":"Thy promises are like Adonis' gardens That one day bloom'd and fruitful were the next.","themes":["witch","war"],"note":"A flattering image that hints at how quickly things fade."},{"id":"183","who":"CHARLES","text":"'Tis Joan, not we, by whom the day is won;","themes":["women","war"],"note":"Charles gives all credit to Joan."},{"id":"183","who":"CHARLES","text":"But Joan la Pucelle shall be France's saint.","themes":["witch","nation"],"note":"Charles canonises her while she lives."},{"id":"191","who":"BEDFORD","text":"To join with witches and the help of hell!","themes":["witch","nation"],"note":"The English see French dependence on Joan as cowardice."},{"id":"195","who":"BURGUNDY","text":"Pray God she prove not masculine ere long,","themes":["women"],"note":"A joke about Joan's virginity."},{"id":"201","who":"TALBOT","text":"And here will Talbot mount, or make his grave.","themes":["honour"],"note":"Talbot's all-or-nothing courage."},{"id":"209","who":"BASTARD","text":"I think this Talbot be a fiend of hell.","themes":["witch","honour"],"note":"The French demonise Talbot as the English demonise Joan."},{"id":"214","who":"CHARLES","text":"Is this thy cunning, thou deceitful dame?","themes":["women","witch"],"note":"Charles turns on Joan at the first defeat."},{"id":"215","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Sleeping or waking must I still prevail,","themes":["women","war"],"note":"Joan resents being expected to work miracles."},{"id":"223","who":"SOLDIER","text":"The cry of Talbot serves me for a sword;","themes":["honour"],"note":"Talbot's name alone wins battles."},{"id":"229","who":"TALBOT","text":"There hath at least five Frenchmen died to-night.","themes":["war","honour"],"note":"Revenge measured in blood."},{"id":"247","who":"COUNTESS","text":"I shall as famous be by this exploit As Scythian Tomyris by Cyrus' death.","themes":["women","honour"],"note":"The Countess wants fame by defeating a great man."},{"id":"252","who":"COUNTESS","text":"Alas, this is a child, a silly dwarf!","themes":["honour"],"note":"The famous hero mocked for his size."},{"id":"252","who":"COUNTESS","text":"It cannot be this weak and writhled shrimp Should strike such terror to his enemies.","themes":["honour","women"],"note":"A comic insult that Talbot will answer."},{"id":"267","who":"TALBOT","text":"No, no, I am but shadow of myself:","themes":["honour"],"note":"Talbot's self is larger than his body."},{"id":"271","who":"TALBOT","text":"These are his substance, sinews, arms and strength,","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"A leader's real self is his army."},{"id":"272","who":"COUNTESS","text":"I find thou art no less than fame hath bruited,","themes":["honour"],"note":"Fame proved true."},{"id":"278","who":"PLANTAGENET","text":"Dare no man answer in a case of truth?","themes":["faction"],"note":"The quarrel opens with a demand for judgement."},{"id":"283","who":"WARWICK","text":"But in these nice sharp quillets of the law, Good faith, I am no wiser than a daw.","themes":["faction"],"note":"The great quarrel starts over a legal point no one can judge."},{"id":"284","who":"PLANTAGENET","text":"The truth appears so naked on my side That any purblind eye may find it out.","themes":["faction"],"note":"Each side is sure of its truth."},{"id":"286","who":"PLANTAGENET","text":"From off this brier pluck a white rose with me.","themes":["faction","legacy"],"note":"The white rose of York is chosen."},{"id":"287","who":"SOMERSET","text":"Pluck a red rose from off this thorn with me.","themes":["faction","legacy"],"note":"The red rose of Lancaster answers it."},{"id":"288","who":"WARWICK","text":"I love no colours, and without all colour Of base insinuating flattery I pluck this white rose with Plantagenet.","themes":["faction"],"note":"Warwick's pun on colour (pretence) as he takes sides."},{"id":"301","who":"SOMERSET","text":"Here in my scabbard, meditating that Shall dye your white rose in a bloody red.","themes":["faction","war"],"note":"Argument becomes a threat of violence."},{"id":"304","who":"PLANTAGENET","text":"Hath not thy rose a canker, Somerset?","themes":["faction"],"note":"Each rose is accused of hidden corruption."},{"id":"305","who":"SOMERSET","text":"Hath not thy rose a thorn, Plantagenet?","themes":["faction"],"note":"Thorns: the danger in York's cause."},{"id":"312","who":"SOMERSET","text":"We grace the yeoman by conversing with him.","themes":["faction","legacy"],"note":"Somerset denies Plantagenet's rank."},{"id":"313","who":"WARWICK","text":"Spring crestless yeomen from so deep a root?","themes":["legacy","faction"],"note":"Warwick defends York's royal descent."},{"id":"315","who":"SOMERSET","text":"His trespass yet lives guilty in thy blood;","themes":["legacy","faction"],"note":"Guilt is inherited in the blood."},{"id":"316","who":"PLANTAGENET","text":"My father was attached, not attainted, Condemn'd to die for treason, but no traitor;","themes":["legacy"],"note":"York's careful defence of his father."},{"id":"316","who":"PLANTAGENET","text":"I'll note you in my book of memory,","themes":["faction"],"note":"York's patient, remembering grudge."},{"id":"318","who":"PLANTAGENET","text":"As cognizance of my blood-drinking hate,","themes":["faction"],"note":"The white rose as a badge of hatred."},{"id":"318","who":"PLANTAGENET","text":"Until it wither with me to my grave, Or flourish to the height of my degree.","themes":["faction","kingship"],"note":"York's ambition hinted: the height of his degree may be the crown."},{"id":"319","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"Go forward, and be chok'd with thy ambition!","themes":["faction"],"note":"Suffolk names York's ambition first."},{"id":"324","who":"WARWICK","text":"Shall send between the red rose and the white A thousand souls to death and deadly night.","themes":["faction","war"],"note":"The prophecy of the Wars of the Roses."},{"id":"328","who":"PLANTAGENET","text":"This quarrel will drink blood another day.","themes":["faction","war"],"note":"The garden scene ends in threat."},{"id":"334","who":"MORTIMER","text":"Just Death, kind umpire of men's miseries,","themes":["legacy"],"note":"Death as release from injustice."},{"id":"343","who":"MORTIMER","text":"Deposed his nephew Richard, Edward's son,","themes":["kingship","legacy"],"note":"The original sin of Lancaster: Richard II's deposition."},{"id":"343","who":"MORTIMER","text":"I was the next by birth and parentage;","themes":["legacy","kingship"],"note":"The Mortimer claim to the crown."},{"id":"343","who":"MORTIMER","text":"I lost my liberty and they their lives.","themes":["legacy","faction"],"note":"The cost of challenging Lancaster."},{"id":"345","who":"MORTIMER","text":"Thou art my heir; the rest I wish thee gather:","themes":["legacy","kingship"],"note":"The claim passes to York, by hint."},{"id":"347","who":"MORTIMER","text":"With silence, nephew, be thou politic: Strong-fixed is the house of Lancaster,","themes":["legacy","kingship"],"note":"Advice of patience that York follows."},{"id":"351","who":"PLANTAGENET","text":"And what I do imagine let that rest.","themes":["legacy","faction"],"note":"York hides his thoughts."},{"id":"353","who":"PLANTAGENET","text":"Here dies the dusky torch of Mortimer, Choked with ambition of the meaner sort:","themes":["legacy","faction"],"note":"Lancaster's ambition called 'meaner'."},{"id":"359","who":"WINCHESTER","text":"It is, because no one should sway but he;","themes":["faction","kingship"],"note":"Winchester's charge against Gloucester."},{"id":"360","who":"GLOUCESTER","text":"Thou bastard of my grandfather!","themes":["faction","legacy"],"note":"Gloucester throws Winchester's birth at him."},{"id":"375","who":"PLANTAGENET","text":"Must your bold verdict enter talk with lords?","themes":["faction","legacy"],"note":"York aware of his lowered status."},{"id":"376","who":"KING","text":"Civil dissension is a viperous worm That gnaws the bowels of the commonwealth.","themes":["faction","kingship"],"note":"The boy King states the play's moral."},{"id":"376","who":"KING","text":"O, what a scandal is it to our crown, That two such noble peers as ye should jar!","themes":["kingship","faction"],"note":"Henry pleads rather than commands."},{"id":"384","who":"FIRST SERVING-MAN","text":"Nay, if we be forbidden stones, we'll fall to it with our teeth.","themes":["faction"],"note":"The quarrel spreads absurdly to the servants."},{"id":"392","who":"KING","text":"O, how this discord doth afflict my soul!","themes":["kingship","faction"],"note":"The King's suffering, not his power, ends the fight."},{"id":"398","who":"KING","text":"I have heard you preach That malice was a great and grievous sin;","themes":["kingship","faction"],"note":"The child shames the bishop."},{"id":"399","who":"WARWICK","text":"What, shall a child instruct you what to do?","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The boy King's moral authority."},{"id":"401","who":"GLOUCESTER","text":"Aye, but, I fear me, with a hollow heart.","themes":["faction"],"note":"The peace is false on both sides."},{"id":"415","who":"KING","text":"Rise, Richard, like a true Plantagenet, And rise created princely Duke of York.","themes":["kingship","legacy"],"note":"Henry raises the man who will claim his crown."},{"id":"418","who":"SOMERSET","text":"Perish, base prince, ignoble Duke of York!","themes":["faction"],"note":"Somerset's private curse."},{"id":"423","who":"EXETER","text":"Burns under feigned ashes of forged love, And will at last break out into a flame;","themes":["faction"],"note":"Exeter sees through the false peace."},{"id":"423","who":"EXETER","text":"That Henry born at Monmouth should win all And Henry born at Windsor lose all:","themes":["kingship","legacy"],"note":"The prophecy that hangs over Henry VI."},{"id":"427","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Through which our policy must make a breach:","themes":["war","women"],"note":"Joan wins by cunning."},{"id":"439","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Behold, this is the happy wedding torch That joineth Rouen unto her countrymen,","themes":["nation","war"],"note":"Joan pictures France as a reunited marriage."},{"id":"446","who":"TALBOT","text":"Pucelle, that witch, that damned sorceress,","themes":["witch","women"],"note":"Talbot's fury at being tricked."},{"id":"450","who":"PUCELLE","text":"'Twas full of darnel: do you like the taste?","themes":["war","women"],"note":"Joan's mockery from the walls."},{"id":"454","who":"PUCELLE","text":"And run a tilt at death within a chair?","themes":["women","honour"],"note":"Joan mocks the dying Bedford."},{"id":"455","who":"TALBOT","text":"Foul fiend of France, and hag of all despite,","themes":["witch","women"],"note":"Talbot's alliterative abuse."},{"id":"461","who":"TALBOT","text":"I speak not to that railing Hecate,","themes":["witch","women"],"note":"Talbot will not speak to a woman."},{"id":"466","who":"TALBOT","text":"So sure I swear to get the town or die.","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"Talbot's oath."},{"id":"469","who":"BEDFORD","text":"Here will I sit before the walls of Rouen, And will be partner of your weal or woe.","themes":["honour","legacy"],"note":"The dying Bedford stays with his men."},{"id":"478","who":"FASTOLFE","text":"All the Talbots in the world, to save my life.","themes":["honour"],"note":"Fastolfe chooses life over honour."},{"id":"483","who":"BEDFORD","text":"Now, quiet soul, depart when heaven please, For I have seen our enemies' overthrow.","themes":["honour","legacy"],"note":"A peaceful death after victory."},{"id":"490","who":"TALBOT","text":"But kings and mightiest potentates must die, For that's the end of human misery.","themes":["legacy","kingship"],"note":"A stoic epitaph for Bedford."},{"id":"494","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Care is no cure, but rather corrosive,","themes":["war","women"],"note":"Joan's practical leadership."},{"id":"516","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Look on thy country, look on fertile France,","themes":["nation","war"],"note":"Joan's patriotic appeal."},{"id":"516","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Behold the wounds, the most unnatural wounds, Which thou thyself hast given her woful breast.","themes":["nation","faction"],"note":"Civil war as a wound to a mother."},{"id":"517","who":"BURGUNDY","text":"Either she hath bewitch'd me with her words, Or nature makes me suddenly relent.","themes":["witch","nation"],"note":"Is Joan's power magic or rhetoric?"},{"id":"520","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Done like a Frenchman: turn and turn again!","themes":["nation"],"note":"Joan mocks French fickleness, perhaps Shakespeare's English joke."},{"id":"528","who":"TALBOT","text":"Ascribes the glory of his conquest got First to my God and next unto your grace.","themes":["honour","kingship"],"note":"Talbot's loyalty to God and King."},{"id":"531","who":"KING","text":"A stouter champion never handled sword.","themes":["honour","legacy"],"note":"Henry V's praise of Talbot passed to his son."},{"id":"549","who":"TALBOT","text":"To tear the garter from thy craven's leg,","themes":["honour"],"note":"Talbot strips the coward of his knighthood."},{"id":"551","who":"TALBOT","text":"Knights of the garter were of noble birth, Valiant and virtuous, full of haughty courage,","themes":["honour"],"note":"Talbot's ideal of knighthood."},{"id":"551","who":"TALBOT","text":"Doth but usurp the sacred name of knight,","themes":["honour"],"note":"Honour must be earned, not worn."},{"id":"586","who":"KING","text":"In France, amongst a fickle wavering nation;","themes":["nation","faction"],"note":"Henry warns that the French will exploit division."},{"id":"586","who":"KING","text":"That for a toy, a thing of no regard,","themes":["faction","kingship"],"note":"Henry thinks the roses trivial."},{"id":"586","who":"KING","text":"I see no reason, if I wear this rose,","themes":["faction","kingship"],"note":"The King's fatal gesture."},{"id":"588","who":"KING","text":"Both are my kinsmen, and I love them both:","themes":["kingship","faction"],"note":"Henry's goodness is his weakness."},{"id":"595","who":"EXETER","text":"Tis much when scepters are in children's hands; But more when envy breeds unkind division;","themes":["kingship","faction"],"note":"Exeter's summary of England's danger."},{"id":"601","who":"TALBOT","text":"Lean famine, quartering steel, and climbing fire;","themes":["war"],"note":"The three servants of conquest."},{"id":"602","who":"GENERAL","text":"Thou ominous and fearful owl of death,","themes":["honour","nation"],"note":"Even his enemy describes Talbot with awe."},{"id":"602","who":"GENERAL","text":"Shall see thee wither'd, bloody, pale, and dead.","themes":["war"],"note":"The hourglass prophecy."},{"id":"606","who":"TALBOT","text":"A little herd of England's timorous deer,","themes":["war","nation"],"note":"Talbot's men trapped like deer."},{"id":"606","who":"TALBOT","text":"And they shall find dear deer of us, my friends.","themes":["honour","war"],"note":"A grim pun: they will sell their lives dearly."},{"id":"615","who":"YORK","text":"That thus we die, while remiss traitors sleep.","themes":["faction","war"],"note":"York's anger at Somerset."},{"id":"619","who":"YORK","text":"That sunder'd friends greet in the hour of death.","themes":["legacy"],"note":"The father and son meet only to die."},{"id":"621","who":"LUCY","text":"Thus, while the vulture of sedition Feeds in the bosom of such great commanders,","themes":["faction","war"],"note":"Faction devours England's leaders."},{"id":"621","who":"LUCY","text":"whiles they each other cross, Lives, honors, lands and all hurry to loss.","themes":["faction","war"],"note":"The cost of the quarrel."},{"id":"625","who":"SOMERSET","text":"York set him on to fight and die in shame,","themes":["faction"],"note":"Somerset's mirror accusation."},{"id":"629","who":"LUCY","text":"Whither, my lord? from bought and sold Lord Talbot;","themes":["honour","faction"],"note":"Talbot betrayed by his own side."},{"id":"629","who":"LUCY","text":"Keep off aloof with worthless emulation.","themes":["faction"],"note":"Rivalry keeps the help away."},{"id":"633","who":"LUCY","text":"The fraud of England, not the force of France, Hath now entrapp'd the noble-minded Talbot:","themes":["faction","nation"],"note":"The play's verdict on Talbot's death."},{"id":"637","who":"LUCY","text":"His fame lives in the world, his shame in you.","themes":["honour","faction"],"note":"Lucy's final rebuke."},{"id":"642","who":"JOHN TALBOT","text":"Is my name Talbot? and am I your son? And shall I fly?","themes":["honour","legacy"],"note":"John defines himself by his name."},{"id":"642","who":"JOHN TALBOT","text":"That basely fled when noble Talbot stood.","themes":["honour","legacy"],"note":"Flight would deny his parentage."},{"id":"646","who":"JOHN TALBOT","text":"Rather than life preserved with infamy.","themes":["honour"],"note":"Honour over life."},{"id":"648","who":"JOHN TALBOT","text":"Aye, rather than I'll shame my mother's womb.","themes":["honour","legacy","women"],"note":"John's honour tied to his mother's."},{"id":"658","who":"JOHN TALBOT","text":"For live I will not, if my father die.","themes":["legacy","honour"],"note":"Loyalty to father beyond life."},{"id":"659","who":"TALBOT","text":"Come, side by side together live and die; And soul with soul from France to heaven fly.","themes":["legacy","honour"],"note":"Father and son choose death together."},{"id":"666","who":"JOHN TALBOT","text":"These words of yours draw life-blood from my heart:","themes":["honour","legacy"],"note":"Being asked to flee hurts more than wounds."},{"id":"666","who":"JOHN TALBOT","text":"If son to Talbot, die at Talbot's foot.","themes":["honour","legacy"],"note":"John's final choice."},{"id":"667","who":"TALBOT","text":"Then follow thou thy desperate sire of Crete, Thou Icarus;","themes":["legacy"],"note":"The myth of the son who falls."},{"id":"671","who":"TALBOT","text":"Where is my other life? mine own is gone;","themes":["legacy"],"note":"Talbot's son as his second self."},{"id":"674","who":"TALBOT","text":"Thou antic Death, which laugh'st us here to scorn,","themes":["legacy"],"note":"Death as a mocking clown."},{"id":"674","who":"TALBOT","text":"Had death been French, then death had died to-day.","themes":["nation","honour"],"note":"Even dead, John defeats the French."},{"id":"674","who":"TALBOT","text":"Now my old arms are young John Talbot's grave.","themes":["legacy","honour"],"note":"Talbot's last line."},{"id":"679","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Young Talbot was not born To be the pillage of a giglot wench:","themes":["women","honour"],"note":"John refuses to fight a woman."},{"id":"682","who":"CHARLES","text":"O, no, forbear! for that which we have fled During the life, let us not wrong it dead.","themes":["honour","nation"],"note":"Charles shows respect for the dead."},{"id":"686","who":"LUCY","text":"Submission, Dauphin! 'tis a mere French word;","themes":["nation","honour"],"note":"English pride, even in defeat."},{"id":"689","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Him that thou magnifiest with all these titles Stinking and fly-blown lies here at our feet.","themes":["honour","women"],"note":"Joan's brutal mockery of fame."},{"id":"693","who":"LUCY","text":"A phoenix that shall make all France afeard.","themes":["legacy","nation"],"note":"Hope of rebirth from Talbot's ashes."},{"id":"694","who":"CHARLES","text":"All will be ours, now bloody Talbot's slain.","themes":["war","nation"],"note":"With Talbot dead, France triumphs."},{"id":"702","who":"KING","text":"It was both impious and unnatural That such immanity and bloody strife Should reign among professors of one faith.","themes":["kingship","war"],"note":"Henry's piety favours peace."},{"id":"704","who":"KING","text":"And fitter is my study and my books Than wanton dalliance with a paramour.","themes":["kingship"],"note":"A scholar, not a warrior king."},{"id":"706","who":"EXETER","text":"He'll make his cap co-equal with the crown.","themes":["kingship","faction"],"note":"The Church's ambition."},{"id":"713","who":"WINCHESTER","text":"I'll either make thee stoop and bend thy knee, Or sack this country with a mutiny.","themes":["faction"],"note":"The cardinal's threat."},{"id":"731","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Now help, ye charming spells and periapts;","themes":["witch"],"note":"Joan turns openly to magic."},{"id":"731","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Under the lordly monarch of the north,","themes":["witch"],"note":"Her spirits serve the devil."},{"id":"739","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Then take my soul, my body, soul and all,","themes":["witch","nation"],"note":"Joan offers her soul for France."},{"id":"741","who":"PUCELLE","text":"And let her head fall into England's lap.","themes":["nation","war"],"note":"France submits."},{"id":"741","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Now, France, thy glory droopeth to the dust.","themes":["nation","war"],"note":"The end of French triumph."},{"id":"749","who":"PUCELLE","text":"I prithee, give me leave to curse awhile.","themes":["witch","women"],"note":"Joan's defiance."},{"id":"757","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"So doth the swan her downy cygnets save, Keeping them prisoner underneath her wings.","themes":["women"],"note":"Suffolk makes capture sound like protection."},{"id":"759","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"My hand would free her, but my heart says no.","themes":["women"],"note":"Suffolk's desire."},{"id":"763","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"She's beautiful and therefore to be woo'd; She is a woman, therefore to be won.","themes":["women"],"note":"A cynical maxim of seduction."},{"id":"771","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"I'll win this Lady Margaret. For whom? Why, for my king;","themes":["women","kingship"],"note":"Suffolk's plan to rule through a queen."},{"id":"784","who":"MARGARET","text":"To be a queen in bondage is more vile Than is a slave in base servility;","themes":["women","kingship"],"note":"Margaret's pride."},{"id":"808","who":"REIGNIER","text":"Enjoy mine own, the country Maine and Anjou,","themes":["war","nation"],"note":"The price of the marriage: lands won by Henry V."},{"id":"821","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"There Minotaurs and ugly treasons lurk.","themes":["women","faction"],"note":"Suffolk sees the danger and goes on."},{"id":"827","who":"SHEPHERD","text":"Ah, Joan, sweet daughter Joan, I'll die with thee!","themes":["legacy","women"],"note":"The father's love."},{"id":"828","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Decrepit miser! base ignoble wretch! I am descended of a gentler blood:","themes":["legacy","women"],"note":"Joan denies her father."},{"id":"834","who":"SHEPHERD","text":"O, burn her, burn her! hanging is too good.","themes":["legacy","witch"],"note":"The father's curse."},{"id":"837","who":"PUCELLE","text":"But issued from the progeny of kings;","themes":["legacy","women"],"note":"Joan's false claim to royal birth."},{"id":"837","who":"PUCELLE","text":"Will cry for vengeance at the gates of heaven.","themes":["witch","women"],"note":"Joan's warning to her judges."},{"id":"840","who":"PUCELLE","text":"I am with child, ye bloody homicides:","themes":["women"],"note":"A desperate plea."},{"id":"841","who":"YORK","text":"Now heaven forfend! the holy maid with child!","themes":["women","witch"],"note":"Mockery of Joan's claimed virginity."},{"id":"852","who":"PUCELLE","text":"May never glorious sun reflex his beams Upon the country where you make abode:","themes":["witch","nation"],"note":"Joan's curse on England."},{"id":"854","who":"YORK","text":"Thou foul accursed minister of hell!","themes":["witch"],"note":"York's verdict on Joan."},{"id":"857","who":"YORK","text":"Shall we at last conclude effeminate peace?","themes":["war","honour"],"note":"York sees peace as weakness."},{"id":"857","who":"YORK","text":"The utter loss of all the realm of France.","themes":["war","legacy"],"note":"York foresees the end."},{"id":"867","who":"ALENCON","text":"Although you break it when your pleasure serves.","themes":["war","nation"],"note":"The truce is made to be broken."},{"id":"883","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"For what is wedlock forced but a hell, An age of discord and continual strife?","themes":["women","kingship"],"note":"An argument for love that serves Suffolk's plot."},{"id":"883","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"For Henry, son unto a conqueror, Is likely to beget more conquerors,","themes":["legacy","kingship"],"note":"Suffolk plays on Henry V's legacy."},{"id":"884","who":"KING","text":"I may revolve and ruminate my grief.","themes":["kingship"],"note":"The King retreats to private grief."},{"id":"886","who":"GLOUCESTER","text":"Aye, grief, I fear me, both at first and last.","themes":["kingship","legacy"],"note":"Gloucester's foreboding."},{"id":"888","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"As did the youthful Paris once to Greece,","themes":["women","war"],"note":"Suffolk as Paris: a love that destroys a kingdom."},{"id":"888","who":"SUFFOLK","text":"Margaret shall now be queen, and rule the king; But I will rule both her, the king and realm.","themes":["kingship","women","faction"],"note":"The play's last lines reveal Suffolk's ambition."}]};
const EX={"intro":["The First Part of King Henry the Sixth begins where Henry V ended: with the death of the great conqueror. At his funeral the news arrives that the French towns he won are falling, the Dauphin has been crowned, and the English champion Lord Talbot has been captured. The English nobles are already quarrelling over who will control the infant King Henry VI.","The play follows two wars. In France, Talbot fights Joan la Pucelle, a shepherd's daughter who says she is sent by the Virgin Mary; Orleans, Rouen and Burgundy's loyalty change hands, until Talbot and his son die near Bordeaux because the Dukes of York and Somerset will not cooperate. In England, the Protector Gloucester feuds with the Bishop of Winchester, and in the Temple Garden Richard Plantagenet and Somerset pluck the white and red roses that will become the badges of the Wars of the Roses.","The play ends with Joan captured and condemned, a shameful truce with France, and the Earl of Suffolk persuading the young King to marry Margaret of Anjou, whom Suffolk means to use to rule both King and kingdom. It is the first of four plays (with 2 and 3 Henry VI and Richard III) that tell how England fell from Henry V's glory into civil war."],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The First Part of Henry the Sixth (Folio title: The first Part of Henry the Sixt)"],["Written","About 1591 to 1592; probably the 'harey the vj' recorded as a new play by Philip Henslowe in March 1592. First printed in the 1623 First Folio"],["Genre","History: one of the Histories in the 1623 First Folio. Chronologically the first play of the first tetralogy (1, 2 and 3 Henry VI, Richard III), though probably written after Parts 2 and 3"],["Setting","England (London, Westminster, the Tower, the Temple Garden) and France (Orleans, Auvergne, Rouen, Paris, Bordeaux, Anjou), from 1422 to the 1440s"],["Protagonist","Lord Talbot, the English champion; the play has no single hero, and England itself is at the centre"],["Antagonists","Joan la Pucelle and the French; at home, the factions of Gloucester and Winchester, York and Somerset"],["Inciting incident","The death of Henry V and the news that the French towns are lost and Talbot captured"],["Turning point","Burgundy's defection (3.3) and the King's red rose (4.1), which leave Talbot exposed"],["Climax","The deaths of Talbot and his son near Bordeaux (4.5 to 4.7), abandoned by York and Somerset"],["Resolution","Joan captured and condemned; a truce with France; Henry agrees to marry Margaret, and Suffolk plans to rule through her"],["Time span","About twenty-three years of history compressed into a few weeks of stage time"],["Major conflicts","England against France; Gloucester against Winchester; York (white rose) against Somerset (red rose); Talbot against Joan"],["Themes","Faction and division, honour and chivalry, women and power, witchcraft and holiness, a child king, fathers and sons, the loss of an inheritance"]],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"Henry the Fifth's funeral; Gloucester and Winchester quarrel","id":6},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Messengers report French towns lost and Talbot captured","id":26},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Joan sees through Reignier and beats Charles in combat","id":61},{"s":"a1s3","t":"Gloucester's and Winchester's men fight at the Tower","id":110},{"s":"a1s4","t":"Salisbury killed by a cannon shot at Orleans","id":150},{"s":"a1s5","t":"Talbot cannot beat Joan; she relieves Orleans","id":171},{"s":"a1s6","t":"Charles promises to make Joan France's saint","id":183},{"s":"a2s1","t":"The English retake Orleans by night","id":201},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Talbot escapes the Countess of Auvergne's trap","id":271},{"s":"a2s4","t":"The white and red roses are plucked in the Temple Garden","id":286},{"s":"a2s4","t":"Warwick prophesies a thousand deaths","id":324},{"s":"a2s5","t":"Mortimer tells Plantagenet of his claim and dies","id":343},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Servants fight with stones; the King makes peace","id":392},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Plantagenet made Duke of York","id":415},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Joan takes Rouen in disguise","id":439},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Talbot retakes Rouen; Bedford dies","id":483},{"s":"a3s3","t":"Joan persuades Burgundy to change sides","id":516},{"s":"a3s4","t":"Talbot made Earl of Shrewsbury","id":531},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Henry crowned in Paris; Fastolfe stripped of the Garter","id":549},{"s":"a4s1","t":"The King puts on a red rose and divides command","id":588},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Talbot trapped before Bordeaux","id":606},{"s":"a4s3","t":"York refuses to help without Somerset's horse","id":615},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Somerset sends help too late","id":633},{"s":"a4s5","t":"John Talbot refuses to flee","id":642},{"s":"a4s7","t":"Talbot dies holding his son's body","id":674},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Henry agrees to marry Armagnac's daughter","id":704},{"s":"a5s3","t":"Joan's fiends forsake her; York captures her","id":741},{"s":"a5s3","t":"Suffolk captures Margaret and woos her for the King","id":787},{"s":"a5s4","t":"Joan denies her father and is condemned to burn","id":852},{"s":"a5s5","t":"Henry sends Suffolk to fetch Margaret","id":888}],"irony":{"2":"The opening prayer for Henry V's spirit to keep England from civil broils is answered by a quarrel within twenty lines.","38":"Winchester complains he is left out just as the others go to serve the King; he alone means to serve himself.","72":"Joan insists on her chastity; at the end she claims to be pregnant by three different men.","183":"Charles plans Joan's monument as a saint; the play ends with her condemned as a witch.","229":"Talbot raises a tomb for Salisbury in Orleans; the town will be French again before long.","252":"The Countess mocks Talbot's shadow, not knowing his substance waits outside.","286":"A private quarrel about law, settled with flowers, starts the Wars of the Roses.","376":"The King calls civil dissension a viperous worm, and in the same scene restores the man who will lead a civil war.","401":"The handshake of peace is sealed with two secret oaths to break it.","415":"Henry gives York his title and lands, strengthening the claim to his own throne.","483":"Bedford dies content, believing he has seen England's enemies overthrown.","520":"Joan mocks Burgundy for changing sides, though she persuaded him to.","531":"Henry rewards Talbot for loyalty in the scene that ends with the factions that will kill him.","588":"Henry puts on a red rose to show it means nothing; it means everything to York.","606":"Talbot's men are deer at bay because their own lords have penned them in.","637":"Somerset says goodbye to brave Talbot as if he were already dead, when his help could still have saved him.","704":"Henry promises to marry Armagnac's daughter; four scenes later he breaks the contract for Margaret.","828":"Joan, who told Charles 'I am by birth a shepherd's daughter', now denies her father.","870":"York demands Charles swear never to rebel; York himself will rebel against Henry.","888":"Suffolk hopes to prosper better than Paris; the next play shows him killed for his part in the marriage."},"devices":{"fire":"Fire, light and stars","beast":"Beasts and hunting","blood":"Blood","magic":"Witchcraft and the devil","flower":"Flowers and growth","herald":"Heraldry, names and titles"},"dev":{"2":[["fire","Comets and stars announce the death of a king.","Comets, importing change of times and states"]],"9":[["fire","Henry's soul imagined as a star brighter than Caesar's.","A far more glorious star thy soul will make"]],"15":[["herald","England's coat of arms has lost its French lilies.","Cropp'd are the flower-de-luces in your arms"]],"81":[["fire","Glory as a circle in water, spreading to nothing.","Glory is like a circle in the water"]],"82":[["fire","Joan as the planet Venus fallen to earth.","Bright star of Venus"]],"112":[["beast","The churchman as a wolf in disguise.","thou wolf in sheep's array"]],"156":[["beast","Pucelle and puzzel, dolphin and dogfish: the French as low creatures.","dolphin or dogfish"]],"162":[["magic","Drawing blood from a witch was thought to break her power.","Blood will I draw on thee, thou art a witch"]],"171":[["beast","Bees driven by smoke, doves by stench: the English as frightened creatures.","So bees with smoke and doves with noisome stench"],["magic","Victory by fear, not force.","A witch, by fear, not force"]],"173":[["herald","The lions on England's coat of arms.","tear the lions out of England's coat"]],"180":[["flower","Joan's promises are quick-flowering gardens.","Adonis' gardens"]],"267":[["herald","Talbot's name and fame are bigger than his body.","I am but shadow of myself"]],"286":[["flower","The white rose as a badge of truth.","pluck a white rose with me"]],"294":[["blood","Blood would turn the white rose red.","paint the white rose red"]],"304":[["flower","The canker that eats a rose from inside.","Hath not thy rose a canker"]],"313":[["flower","York's family as a tree with a deep root.","from so deep a root"]],"318":[["blood","A badge of blood-drinking hate.","blood-drinking hate"]],"324":[["blood","The roses send souls to death.","Shall send between the red rose and the white"]],"332":[["fire","Mortimer's eyes as lamps running out of oil.","like lamps whose wasting oil is spent"]],"353":[["fire","Mortimer as a torch choked out.","Here dies the dusky torch of Mortimer"]],"376":[["beast","Civil strife as a poisonous worm in the body of the state.","Civil dissension is a viperous worm"]],"423":[["fire","The quarrel as fire under false ashes.","Burns under feigned ashes of forged love"]],"439":[["fire","Joan's torch as a wedding torch.","happy wedding torch"]],"494":[["beast","Talbot as a proud peacock to be plucked.","like a peacock sweep along his tail"]],"516":[["blood","France's breast bleeds from Burgundy's sword.","One drop of blood drawn from thy country's bosom"]],"602":[["beast","Talbot as the owl of death.","owl of death"]],"606":[["beast","The English as deer hunted by French curs.","A little herd of England's timorous deer"]],"621":[["beast","Sedition as a vulture.","the vulture of sedition"]],"667":[["fire","Icarus flew too near the sun.","Thou Icarus"]],"693":[["fire","The phoenix rising from ashes.","A phoenix"]],"731":[["magic","Joan calls on spirits of the devil.","the lordly monarch of the north"]],"852":[["fire","Joan curses England with darkness.","May never glorious sun reflex his beams"]]},"stage":{"abbey":"Westminster Abbey, at the funeral of Henry the Fifth","walls":"Before the walls of a French town under siege","battle":"A field of battle","field":"Open plains in France","camp":"An army camp","castle":"The Countess of Auvergne's castle","garden":"The Temple Garden, London","dungeon":"A prison room in the Tower of London","gate":"Before the gates of the Tower of London","hall":"A hall of state","palace":"A royal palace","street":"Inside a captured town"},"stagerules":[["abbey","abbey"],["before the tower","gate"],["tower of london","dungeon"],["temple","garden"],["castle","castle"],["parliament|hall of state","hall"],["within the town","street"],["camp","camp"],["field of battle|part of the field","battle"],["plains","field"],["palace","palace"],["walls|orleans|rouen|bordeaux|angiers","walls"]],"place":{"a1s1":"Westminster Abbey, London","a1s2":"Before Orleans, France","a1s3":"Before the Tower of London","a1s4":"Orleans: the walls and an English turret","a1s5":"Before the walls of Orleans","a1s6":"On the walls of Orleans","a2s1":"Before Orleans, at night","a2s2":"Orleans, inside the town","a2s3":"The Countess's castle, Auvergne","a2s4":"The Temple Garden, London","a2s5":"A room in the Tower of London","a3s1":"Parliament, London","a3s2":"Before Rouen","a3s3":"The plains near Rouen","a3s4":"The palace, Paris","a4s1":"A hall of state, Paris","a4s2":"Before Bordeaux","a4s3":"Plains in Gascony","a4s4":"Other plains in Gascony","a4s5":"The English camp near Bordeaux","a4s6":"A field of battle near Bordeaux","a4s7":"Another part of the field","a5s1":"The palace, London","a5s2":"Plains in Anjou","a5s3":"Before Angiers","a5s4":"York's camp in Anjou","a5s5":"The royal palace, London"},"arc":{"Talbot's power":[1,2,2,6,3,3,7,8,9,9,9,9,9,7,10,9,6,4,3,3,2,0,0,0,0,0,0],"Joan's power":[0,6,6,6,8,9,5,4,4,4,4,4,7,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,9,8,8,2,0,0]},"arcnote":"Talbot begins captured and offstage, then rises as he is freed, swears revenge for Salisbury, retakes Orleans, outwits the Countess and wins back Rouen. He peaks when the King makes him Earl of Shrewsbury in 3.4. From then on he falls: Burgundy has gone over to the French, York and Somerset will not cooperate, and he and his son die at Bordeaux. Joan rises at once, defeating Charles and Talbot and relieving Orleans, drops when the English surprise the town, climbs again with Rouen and Burgundy, and stays high while Talbot dies. Her collapse is sudden: in 5.3 her fiends desert her and York captures her; in 5.4 she is condemned. The two lines never meet at the top together: when one champion rises, the other falls, and both are gone before the play ends with a peace neither would have accepted.","iago":[["a1s1","At the funeral, a messenger warns that English factions are losing France."],["a2s4","Plantagenet and Somerset quarrel over a point of law in the Temple."],["a2s4","Plantagenet plucks a white rose, Somerset a red; Warwick and Vernon take white, Suffolk red."],["a2s4","Somerset taunts Plantagenet with his father's execution for treason."],["a2s4","Plantagenet vows to wear the white rose till he dies or rises; Warwick prophesies a thousand deaths."],["a2s5","Mortimer names Plantagenet heir to the Mortimer claim to the crown."],["a3s1","The King restores Plantagenet as Duke of York; Somerset curses him aside."],["a3s4","Vernon (white) strikes Basset (red) after a quarrel at sea."],["a4s1","Vernon and Basset ask the King for combat; York and Somerset trade threats."],["a4s1","Henry puts on a red rose 'for no reason'; York resents it."],["a4s1","York made Regent of France; Somerset must join his horse to York's foot."],["a4s3","York curses Somerset for holding back the cavalry Talbot needs."],["a4s4","Somerset blames York's rash plan and sends the horse too late."],["a4s7","Talbot and his son die, victims of the quarrel."],["a5s4","York, as Regent, condemns Joan and scorns the peace, his ambition intact."]],"rel":{"1":[["GLOUCESTER","WINCHESTER","hate"],["WINCHESTER","GLOUCESTER","hate"],["CHARLES","PUCELLE","love"],["PUCELLE","CHARLES","trust"],["TALBOT","SALISBURY","love"],["TALBOT","PUCELLE","hate"],["BEDFORD","TALBOT","trust"]],"2":[["PLANTAGENET","SOMERSET","hate"],["WARWICK","PLANTAGENET","trust"],["SUFFOLK","SOMERSET","trust"],["SOMERSET","PLANTAGENET","hate"],["COUNTESS","TALBOT","deceive"],["MORTIMER","PLANTAGENET","love"],["CHARLES","PUCELLE","love"]],"3":[["KING","GLOUCESTER","love"],["GLOUCESTER","WINCHESTER","deceive"],["WINCHESTER","GLOUCESTER","deceive"],["KING","PLANTAGENET","trust"],["PUCELLE","BURGUNDY","deceive"],["BURGUNDY","TALBOT","hate"],["KING","TALBOT","love"],["VERNON","BASSET","hate"]],"4":[["YORK","SOMERSET","hate"],["SOMERSET","YORK","hate"],["KING","YORK","trust"],["TALBOT","FASTOLFE","hate"],["TALBOT","JOHN TALBOT","love"],["JOHN TALBOT","TALBOT","love"],["LUCY","TALBOT","love"]],"5":[["SUFFOLK","MARGARET","love"],["SUFFOLK","KING","deceive"],["KING","MARGARET","love"],["PUCELLE","SHEPHERD","hate"],["YORK","PUCELLE","hate"],["WINCHESTER","GLOUCESTER","hate"],["GLOUCESTER","SUFFOLK","hate"]]},"context":[["Date","Probably written about 1591 to 1592. Philip Henslowe's diary records 'harey the vj' as a new play at the Rose theatre in March 1592, and it drew large audiences. Many scholars think it was written after the plays now called Parts 2 and 3, as a 'prequel' to cash in on their success."],["Authorship","The play is widely thought to be collaborative. Studies of vocabulary and style suggest that Thomas Nashe wrote much of Act 1 and that Shakespeare's hand is clearest in the Temple Garden scene and the Talbot scenes of Act 4. The New Oxford Shakespeare (2016) names Nashe and Christopher Marlowe with Shakespeare. The Folio printed it as Shakespeare's."],["Sources","The main sources are Edward Hall's Union of the Two Noble and Illustre Families of Lancaster and York (1548) and Raphael Holinshed's Chronicles (1587). Hall saw the story as God's punishment of England for the deposition of Richard II, ending only with the Tudors."],["The real history","Shakespeare compresses and rearranges thirty years. Henry V died in 1422; Orleans was relieved in 1429; Joan was burned at Rouen in 1431, the year Henry VI was crowned in Paris; Bedford died in 1435; Burgundy changed sides in 1435; Talbot died at Castillon, near Bordeaux, in 1453, twenty-two years after Joan; Henry married Margaret in 1445. Talbot and Joan never met in single combat."],["Joan of Arc","The real Joan was tried for heresy by an English-controlled church court and burned at Rouen. A later trial in 1456 cleared her name, and she was made a saint in 1920. English chroniclers like Hall called her a witch and a whore, and the play mostly follows them, though it gives her powerful speeches."],["Talbot","John Talbot, first Earl of Shrewsbury, was England's most famous soldier in the later Hundred Years War. In 1592 Thomas Nashe wrote that it would have delighted 'brave Talbot, the terror of the French' to know that he would triumph again on the stage and be wept over by ten thousand spectators: almost certainly a reference to this play."],["The Temple Garden","There is no historical record of the scene of the roses. Shakespeare invented it to give the Wars of the Roses a single starting point. The red and white roses were Lancastrian and Yorkist badges; the Tudors joined them in the Tudor rose."],["The first tetralogy","1, 2 and 3 Henry VI and Richard III tell the story from Henry V's death to Richard III's defeat at Bosworth in 1485 by Henry Tudor, Queen Elizabeth's grandfather. Part 1 sets up the main threads: York's claim, Margaret and Suffolk, Gloucester and Winchester."],["England and France in the 1590s","When the play was first staged, English troops under the Earl of Essex were fighting in Normandy in support of the Protestant Henri IV, including a siege of Rouen in 1591. Talbot's campaigns would have felt topical."],["Women in power","The play's dangerous French women (Joan, the Countess, Margaret) were staged under a female monarch, Elizabeth I, who was also called a virgin warrior. Critics have asked whether Joan is partly a dark mirror of Elizabeth."],["Staging","The play is full of battles, sieges and walls: the upper stage serves as Orleans, Rouen and Bordeaux, with scaling ladders, a torch on a turret and a cannon shot. All the women's parts were played by boys."]],"critics":{"faction":[["E. M. W. Tillyard, 'Shakespeare's History Plays' (1944)","Paraphrased: Tillyard read the Henry VI plays as a lesson in order: England is punished for disorder and division, and the nobles' quarrels are the cause of its losses. He saw the play as dramatising the 'Tudor myth' of history leading to Henry VII."],["Michael Hattaway, introduction to the New Cambridge edition (1990)","Paraphrased: Hattaway argues the play is less about providence than about politics: it shows how institutions break down when the nobles pursue private quarrels, and it invites the audience to judge rather than simply to accept."]],"honour":[["Thomas Nashe, 'Pierce Penniless' (1592)","Paraphrased: Nashe praised the play's Talbot as proof that the theatre could revive the heroes of the past and move huge audiences to tears for their deaths."],["Edward Burns, introduction to the Arden edition (2000)","Paraphrased: Burns sees Talbot as a figure of an older chivalry that the play both celebrates and lays to rest; his heroism belongs to a world already disappearing."]],"women":[["Phyllis Rackin, 'Stages of History' (1990)","Paraphrased: Rackin argues that the history plays are written by and about men, and that women like Joan, who are French, lower-class and sexual, represent everything that threatens the English masculine version of the past."],["Jean E. Howard and Phyllis Rackin, 'Engendering a Nation' (1997)","Paraphrased: Howard and Rackin show how the play links the loss of France with the power of French women over English men, from Joan in battle to Margaret in marriage."]],"witch":[["Gabriele Bernhard Jackson, 'Topical Ideology: Witches, Amazons, and Shakespeare's Joan of Arc' (1988)","Paraphrased: Jackson argues that Joan combines the images of the Amazon, the witch and the holy virgin, and that the play shifts between them because its audience was itself uncertain about powerful women."],["Leah Marcus, 'Puzzling Shakespeare' (1988)","Paraphrased: Marcus suggests that Joan, a virgin warrior who leads her nation, would inevitably have reminded audiences of Elizabeth I, and that the play's attack on Joan carries anxieties about female rule."]],"kingship":[["Jan Kott, 'Shakespeare Our Contemporary' (1964)","Paraphrased: Kott described the history plays as showing a 'Grand Mechanism' of power in which kings and nobles climb and fall in an endless cycle; Henry VI, gentle and pious, is crushed by it."],["Michael Taylor, introduction to the Oxford edition (2003)","Paraphrased: Taylor stresses the play's theatrical energy and its shaping of history into patterns of rise and fall, presenting a weak king surrounded by strong, self-serving men."]],"legacy":[["Gary Taylor, 'Shakespeare and Others: The Authorship of Henry the Sixth, Part One' (1995)","Paraphrased: Taylor argued that the play was written by several hands, and that Shakespeare's share includes the Temple Garden and the deaths of the Talbots, where the themes of inherited honour and inherited quarrel are strongest."],["David Riggs, 'Shakespeare's Heroical Histories' (1971)","Paraphrased: Riggs reads the Henry VI plays as tracing the decline of an inherited heroic ideal: Talbot embodies the chivalric honour of the past, and after his death the nobles inherit only its forms, not its spirit."]]},"symbols":{"Rose":"\\broses?\\b","Witch":"\\bwitch\\w*|\\bsorcer\\w*|\\bfiend\\w*|\\bhell\\b|\\bdevil\\w*","Name":"\\bname\\b|\\bfame\\b|\\btitles?\\b","Blood":"\\bblood\\w*","Fire":"\\bfire\\b|\\bflame\\b|\\btorch\\b|\\bburn\\w*","Shame":"\\bshame\\w*|\\bdisgrace\\w*"},"symbols2":[["The roses","White for York, red for Lancaster. Plucked in the Temple Garden, worn by followers, put on by the King, they stand for a quarrel that grows from a point of law to civil war."],["Joan's witchcraft","The English call her witch from the start; in 5.3 the fiends appear. The play keeps asking whether her power is from heaven, hell or her own skill and courage."],["Talbot's name","The French fear his name more than his army; a soldier wins spoils with it; Lucy recites his titles. Name and fame are what survive him."],["Blood","Blood stands for family, inheritance and guilt (York's 'blood' attainted) and for the violence the roses will cause."],["Fire and torches","From comets at the funeral to Joan's signal torch and the stake where she is burned, fire marks omens, trickery and punishment."],["The Garter","The badge of the highest English knighthood, torn from Fastolfe's leg: honour that must be earned."],["The child King","Henry is young and gentle, a living reminder that his father's strength has gone: 'when sceptres are in children's hands'."],["Fathers and sons","Henry V and Henry VI, Talbot and John, Mortimer and York, the Shepherd and Joan: the play is full of sons living up to, or denying, their fathers."]],"solo":{"38":1,"247":1,"353":1,"423":1,"595":1,"621":1,"731":1,"821":1,"888":1},"why":["Opens the play with the darkness of a world that has lost its king.","Links greatness and early death, the shadow over the whole play.","Sets Henry V as the measure every later ruler fails.","Sums up the legacy the son will lose.","Shows grief turning straight to violence.","Shows the English blaming witchcraft before Joan even appears.","Introduces the fear of a weak king controlled by others.","Shows the quarrel becoming personal, and women blamed even here.","Prophesies the ruined England the plays will show.","States the hope the play will betray.","Refuses the easy excuse of treachery: the problem is England itself.","Makes the link between faction at home and loss abroad explicit.","Calls the nobles to an honour they have forgotten.","Shows the loss of France in the heraldry of England.","Gives the English spirit of defiance in one line.","Shows the French demonising Talbot as the English will demonise Joan.","Names cowardice, not French strength, as the first cause of defeat.","Makes betrayal from behind the image of the war.","Reveals Winchester's ambition in his first soliloquy.","Shows the plan to control the realm through the child King.","Introduces the play's pattern of sudden reversals of fortune.","Shows even the French admitting English courage.","Presents Joan first as holy.","Shows Joan's insight at her first entrance.","Gives Joan's humble origin, which she later denies.","Joan's claim of a divine vision: the key to her character.","Shows Joan as a patriot.","Joan claims to go beyond the limits of her sex.","A double meaning that mixes courage and sex.","Charles reaches for warrior women of legend to explain Joan.","Joan's chastity, the foundation of her holiness.","Shows bawdy jokes undercutting Joan's holiness.","Joan names herself the scourge of England.","A famous image of how glory spreads and vanishes.","Joan sees Henry V's glory dissolving with his death.","Shows Charles worshipping Joan as a goddess.","Shows suspicion and theft at the heart of the state.","Gloucester's insistence on his own authority.","Shows the insults of the Gloucester-Winchester feud.","Winchester calls the Protector a usurper.","Makes civil strife the first murder, brother against brother.","Paints the churchman as a hypocrite.","Each lord accuses the other of plotting against the King.","Gives an ordinary citizen's view of the nobles' pride.","Salisbury as the model of a soldier, killed by a cannon.","Talbot's wordplay degrades the French leaders.","Shows the brutality of Talbot's vengeance.","The shock of a woman leading an army.","Talbot names Joan a witch as he fights her.","Joan seems to know Talbot's fate.","Talbot explains defeat as witchcraft.","National pride turned to shame.","Cowardice betrays England's emblem.","Shame matters more to Talbot than death.","Joan's promise kept.","Charles's praise hints at how fast things fade.","Charles gives all credit to Joan.","Charles makes Joan a saint while she lives.","The English see French reliance on Joan as cowardice.","A crude joke on Joan's virginity.","Talbot's all-or-nothing courage.","The French demonise Talbot as the English demonise Joan.","Charles turns on Joan at the first defeat.","Joan resents being expected to work constant miracles.","Talbot's name alone is a weapon.","Revenge measured in blood.","The Countess wants fame by defeating a great man.","Fame and appearance at odds.","A comic insult that sets up Talbot's answer.","Talbot's riddle: his self is bigger than his body.","A leader's real self is his army.","Fame proved true.","The quarrel begins as a demand for judgement.","The great quarrel starts with a legal point no one can judge.","Each side is sure the truth is plain.","The white rose of York is chosen.","The red rose of Lancaster answers it.","Warwick puns on colour as he takes sides.","Argument becomes a threat of blood.","Each rose accused of hidden corruption.","Thorns: the danger in York's cause.","Somerset denies York's rank.","Warwick defends York's royal descent.","Guilt inherited in the blood.","York's careful defence of his father.","York's patient, remembering grudge.","The white rose as a badge of hatred.","York's ambition hinted: the height of his degree.","Suffolk names York's ambition.","The prophecy of the Wars of the Roses.","The garden scene ends with a threat.","Death as release from injustice.","The original wrong: Richard II's deposition.","The Mortimer claim to the crown.","The cost of challenging Lancaster.","The claim passes to York.","Advice of patience that York follows.","York hides his thoughts.","Calls Lancaster's ambition 'meaner'.","Winchester's charge against Gloucester.","Gloucester throws Winchester's birth at him.","York aware of his lowered status.","The boy King states the play's moral.","Henry pleads rather than commands.","The quarrel spreads absurdly to servants.","The King's suffering, not his power, ends the fight.","The child shames the bishop.","The boy King's moral authority.","The peace is false.","Henry raises the man who will claim his crown.","Somerset's private curse.","Exeter sees through the false peace.","The prophecy that hangs over Henry VI.","Joan wins by cunning.","Joan pictures France as a reunited marriage.","Talbot's fury at being tricked.","Joan's mockery from the walls.","Joan mocks the dying Bedford.","Talbot's alliterative abuse.","Talbot refuses to speak to a woman.","Talbot's oath.","The dying Bedford stays with his men.","Fastolfe chooses life over honour.","A peaceful death after victory.","A stoic epitaph for Bedford.","Joan's practical leadership.","Joan's patriotic appeal.","Civil war as a wound to a mother.","Is Joan's power magic or rhetoric?","Joan mocks French fickleness.","Talbot's loyalty to God and King.","Henry V's praise of Talbot passed to his son.","Talbot strips the coward of his knighthood.","Talbot's ideal of knighthood.","Honour must be earned, not worn.","Henry warns that the French will exploit division.","Henry thinks the roses trivial.","The King's fatal gesture.","Henry's goodness is his weakness.","Exeter's summary of England's danger.","The three servants of conquest.","Even his enemy describes Talbot with awe.","The hourglass prophecy.","Talbot's men trapped like deer.","A grim pun: they will sell their lives dearly.","York's anger at Somerset.","The father and son meet only to die.","Faction devours England's leaders.","The cost of the quarrel.","Somerset's mirror accusation.","Talbot betrayed by his own side.","Rivalry keeps the help away.","The play's verdict on Talbot's death.","Lucy's final rebuke.","John defines himself by his name.","Flight would deny his parentage.","Honour over life.","John's honour tied to his mother's.","Loyalty to his father beyond life.","Father and son choose death together.","Being asked to flee hurts more than wounds.","John's final choice.","The myth of the son who falls.","Talbot's son as his second self.","Death as a mocking clown.","Even dead, John defeats the French.","Talbot's last line.","John refuses to fight a woman.","Charles shows respect for the dead.","English pride, even in defeat.","Joan's brutal mockery of fame.","Hope of rebirth from Talbot's ashes.","With Talbot dead, France triumphs.","Henry's piety favours peace.","A scholar, not a warrior king.","The Church's ambition.","The cardinal's threat.","Joan turns openly to magic.","Her spirits serve the devil.","Joan offers her soul for France.","France submits.","The end of French triumph.","Joan's defiance.","Suffolk makes capture sound like protection.","Suffolk's desire.","A cynical maxim of seduction.","Suffolk's plan to rule through a queen.","Margaret's pride.","The price of the marriage: lands Henry V won.","Suffolk sees the danger and goes on.","The father's love.","Joan denies her father.","The father's curse.","Joan's false claim to royal birth.","Joan's warning to her judges.","A desperate plea.","Mockery of Joan's claimed virginity.","Joan's curse on England.","York's verdict on Joan.","York sees peace as weakness.","York foresees the end.","The truce is made to be broken.","An argument for love that serves Suffolk's plot.","Suffolk plays on Henry V's legacy.","The King retreats to private grief.","Gloucester's foreboding.","Suffolk as Paris: a love that destroys a kingdom.","The play's last lines reveal Suffolk's ambition."],"insults":[{"id":"104","f":"Peel'd priest","who":"Gloucester","n":"Gloucester mocks Winchester's shaven head."},{"id":"105","f":"thou most usurping proditor","who":"Winchester","n":"Winchester calls the Protector a traitor."},{"id":"106","f":"thou manifest conspirator","who":"Gloucester","n":"Gloucester accuses Winchester of plotting against Henry V."},{"id":"112","f":"Winchester goose","who":"Gloucester","n":"A crude jibe about the brothels the Bishop licensed."},{"id":"112","f":"out, scarlet hypocrite","who":"Gloucester","n":"The cardinal's red robes as a hypocrite's costume."},{"id":"125","f":"Abominable Gloucester","who":"Winchester","n":"Winchester's parting threat."},{"id":"156","f":"Pucelle or puzzel, dolphin or dogfish","who":"Talbot","n":"Talbot's punning abuse of Joan and Charles."},{"id":"162","f":"Devil or devil's dam","who":"Talbot","n":"Talbot's first words to Joan."},{"id":"165","f":"this high-minded strumpet","who":"Talbot","n":"Talbot calls Joan a whore."},{"id":"231","f":"the Dauphin and his trull","who":"Burgundy","n":"Burgundy mocks Joan as Charles's whore."},{"id":"252","f":"this weak and writhled shrimp","who":"Countess","n":"The Countess scorns Talbot's size."},{"id":"308","f":"peevish boy","who":"Plantagenet","n":"Plantagenet to Somerset in the Temple Garden."},{"id":"312","f":"We grace the yeoman","who":"Somerset","n":"Somerset denies Plantagenet's noble rank."},{"id":"360","f":"Thou bastard of my grandfather","who":"Gloucester","n":"Gloucester reminds Winchester of his illegitimate birth."},{"id":"451","f":"vile fiend and shameless courtezan","who":"Burgundy","n":"Burgundy abuses Joan on the walls of Rouen."},{"id":"455","f":"Foul fiend of France, and hag of all despite","who":"Talbot","n":"Talbot's alliterative attack on Joan."},{"id":"463","f":"base muleters of France","who":"Talbot","n":"Talbot scorns the French for staying behind their walls."},{"id":"549","f":"thy craven's leg","who":"Talbot","n":"Talbot to Fastolfe as he tears off the Garter."},{"id":"602","f":"Thou ominous and fearful owl of death","who":"General","n":"The French General's grudging description of Talbot."},{"id":"689","f":"Stinking and fly-blown","who":"Joan","n":"Joan mocks the dead Talbot."},{"id":"748","f":"Fell banning hag","who":"York","n":"York silences the cursing Joan."},{"id":"828","f":"Decrepit miser! base ignoble wretch","who":"Joan","n":"Joan disowns her own father."},{"id":"834","f":"cursed drab","who":"Shepherd","n":"Joan's father curses his daughter."},{"id":"846","f":"that notorious Machiavel","who":"York","n":"An anachronistic insult for a schemer."},{"id":"854","f":"Thou foul accursed minister of hell","who":"York","n":"York's last words to Joan."}],"pronouns":[{"id":"61","f":"Reignier is 't thou that thinkest to beguile me?","n":"Joan uses 'thou' to a duke: boldness beyond her rank."},{"id":"71","f":"'tis thou that must help me","n":"Charles uses 'thou' to Joan, intimate and pleading."},{"id":"100","f":"Thou art no friend to God or to the King","n":"Gloucester's 'thou' to Woodvile is contemptuous: a lord to an inferior."},{"id":"253","f":"Madam, I have been bold to trouble you","n":"Talbot answers the Countess's insult with polite 'you': courtesy as a weapon."},{"id":"262","f":"Laughest thou, wretch?","n":"The Countess uses 'thou' as contempt."},{"id":"315","f":"Was not thy father, Richard Earl of Cambridge","n":"Somerset's 'thy' to Plantagenet: scorn."},{"id":"646","f":"and, father, do you fly","n":"John uses respectful 'you' to his father."},{"id":"659","f":"Then here I take my leave of thee, fair son","n":"Talbot uses tender 'thee' to his son."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Bedford wins back France at once","Talbot is freed, but Joan appears to fight for France"],"a1s2":["Joan fails Charles's test","She sees through it and beats Charles in combat"],"a1s3":["Gloucester gets into the Tower","He is shut out and the lords brawl"],"a1s4":["Salisbury takes Orleans","He is killed by a boy's cannon shot"],"a1s5":["Talbot kills Joan","She beats him and enters Orleans"],"a1s6":["The French keep Orleans for good","The English retake it that night"],"a2s1":["The English starve Orleans out","They take it by a surprise attack"],"a2s2":["Talbot ignores the Countess","He accepts, with a hidden plan"],"a2s3":["The Countess captures Talbot","His soldiers turn the trap"],"a2s4":["The lawyers settle the case","It turns into the feud of the roses"],"a2s5":["Mortimer tells York to rebel","He advises patience and dies"],"a3s1":["Gloucester and Winchester are truly reconciled","They make a hollow peace; York is restored"],"a3s2":["Joan holds Rouen","Talbot retakes it and Bedford dies"],"a3s3":["Burgundy stays loyal to England","Joan's speech turns him"],"a3s4":["Talbot is rewarded with peace","He is made an earl, and the rose quarrel spreads"],"a4s1":["The King settles the roses quarrel","He wears a red rose and divides command"],"a4s2":["Bordeaux surrenders","Talbot is trapped outside it"],"a4s3":["York rides to rescue Talbot","He refuses without Somerset's horse"],"a4s4":["Somerset saves Talbot","He sends help too late"],"a4s5":["John Talbot escapes","He refuses to leave his father"],"a4s6":["The Talbots break out","John refuses again to fly"],"a4s7":["Talbot survives","He dies holding his son"],"a5s1":["Henry marries Armagnac's daughter","He agrees, but later breaks it"],"a5s2":["The French win easily","The English unite to fight"],"a5s3":["Joan's spirits save her","They desert her, and York captures her"],"a5s4":["Joan is pardoned","She is condemned, and peace is made"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Talbot and Joan","pts":["Both are champions of their nations who win by force of personality.","Talbot is noble, loyal and chivalric; Joan is low-born and wins by cunning as well as courage.","Each side calls the other's champion a devil.","Both are gone before the peace is made."],"ids":[171,516]},{"t":"Talbot and Fastolfe","pts":["Both are English knights in the same campaigns.","Talbot will not run even to save his life; Fastolfe runs at Patay and Rouen.","Talbot strips Fastolfe of the Garter, the symbol of honour he betrayed."],"ids":[478,551]},{"t":"York and Somerset","pts":["Both are proud, ambitious nobles.","Each blames the other for Talbot's death.","York hides his ambition; Somerset shows his contempt openly."],"ids":[615,625]},{"t":"Gloucester and Winchester","pts":["Both are the King's uncles and both claim to serve him.","Gloucester is hot-tempered but loyal; Winchester is ambitious and plots in private.","Their quarrel spreads to their servants."],"ids":[95,713]},{"t":"Joan and Margaret","pts":["Both are French women who capture English men.","Joan leads armies; Margaret will rule through marriage.","As Joan is led to death, Margaret is led to the English throne."],"ids":[63,784]}],"analysis":{"TALBOT":["Talbot is the play's hero and its conscience. He is introduced through report: a messenger describes his wonders at Patay and his betrayal by Fastolfe, and in captivity he is 'the scarecrow that affrights our children'. His name is a weapon: a common soldier loots the French with nothing but the cry of 'A Talbot!'","He embodies the chivalric code. He swears revenge for Salisbury and keeps it; he strips the Garter from Fastolfe because knights must be 'valiant and virtuous'; he kneels to the King and gives the glory 'first to my God and next unto your grace'. His riddle to the Countess, that he is only 'shadow of myself', says that a lord is his army and his service.","Yet he cannot defeat Joan, and he explains her success only as witchcraft. His world of loyalty depends on the nobles at home, and they fail him. Trapped at Bordeaux, he dies not by French force but by 'the fraud of England'.","His last scenes with his son, in rhyming couplets, turn him from a warrior into a father. Both want the other to live; both choose honour. 'Now my old arms are young John Talbot's grave' closes the line of heroic English chivalry."],"PUCELLE":["Joan is the play's most theatrical figure and its most disputed. She enters claiming a vision from 'God's mother', sees through Charles's disguise, beats him in combat and refuses his love. To the French she is a saint; to the English a witch and a strumpet.","She is clever and practical. She enters Rouen with sacks of corn, mocks the English from the walls, and wins Burgundy back with a moving speech about wounded France, before mocking his fickleness aside. Her power is often in words.","In Act 5 the play seems to answer its question: she calls up fiends, offers her blood and soul, and they desert her. Captured, she denies her shepherd father, claims royal birth and virginity, then pregnancy by three men. The English version of Joan triumphs.","Modern audiences often see her treatment as shaped by English propaganda and by fear of powerful women. Her final curse on England, wishing it darkness and despair, comes true in the plays that follow."],"KING":["Henry VI is a child when the play opens; he does not appear until Act 3. He is pious, gentle and eager for peace, and his first words are a plea to his uncles to stop fighting.","His goodness is his weakness. He makes peace between Gloucester and Winchester by tears, not authority, and the peace is hollow. He restores York, strengthening his future rival.","In Paris he puts on a red rose to show it means nothing, without seeing how York will read it, and he divides command between the two enemies. Exeter's verdict is that it is dangerous 'when sceptres are in children's hands'.","In Act 5 he agrees to marry for peace, then breaks that contract after hearing Suffolk describe Margaret. He prefers books to love and rule, but is easily swayed: a king who will be ruled by others."],"YORK":["Richard Plantagenet begins the play stripped of rank because his father was executed for treason. In the Temple Garden he plucks the white rose and, taunted by Somerset, vows to wear it 'until it wither with me to my grave, or flourish to the height of my degree'.","His dying uncle Mortimer explains that the family has the better claim to the crown, and advises patience. York listens and keeps his thoughts to himself: 'what I do imagine, let that rest.'","Restored as Duke of York and made Regent, he is effective in battle but refuses to help Talbot without Somerset's cavalry. His fury at Somerset is real, but so is his pride.","He captures and condemns Joan and scorns the peace. Throughout, his ambition is held back, waiting; in the next plays it bursts out in civil war."],"SUFFOLK":["Suffolk is a minor figure until Act 5. He takes the red rose with Somerset and calls York ambitious.","Capturing Margaret, he is overwhelmed by her beauty. Being married, he decides to win her for the King. The courtship, full of asides and cross-purposes, is comic, but its price is Anjou and Maine.","He persuades Henry with extravagant praise and an argument for love over money that serves only his own plans.","His last speech compares him to Paris and announces that Margaret will rule the King and he will rule her, the King and the realm. The play ends with a new, more dangerous faction."],"GLOUCESTER":["Humphrey, Duke of Gloucester, is Lord Protector and the King's uncle. He is proud, quick to anger and fiercely loyal to the dead King's memory.","His feud with Winchester begins at the funeral and erupts at the Tower gates and in Parliament. He makes peace for the King's sake, though privately he fears Winchester's 'hollow heart'.","He supports York's restoration and arranges the Armagnac marriage. He is the only lord who opposes the marriage to Margaret on grounds of honour.","His last line, 'grief, I fear me, both at first and last', looks forward to his own fall in Part 2."],"WINCHESTER":["Henry Beaufort, Bishop of Winchester and later Cardinal, is the King's great-uncle and a worldly churchman. At the funeral he is already accused of wanting a weak king.","He shuts Gloucester out of the Tower and swears to have his head. His men fight Gloucester's in the street.","He shakes hands with Gloucester while vowing privately to break the peace, and buys a cardinal's hat.","His last speech threatens to wreck the country rather than stoop to Gloucester, showing that the Church too is part of England's division."]},"motifs":[["The roses","Plucked in the Temple Garden, worn by followers, and put on by the King, the roses turn a legal quarrel into the badges of civil war.",[75,76,78,89,137]],["Witchcraft and holiness","Joan claims a holy vision; the English call her witch; in Act 5 fiends appear. The play keeps asking where her power comes from.",[25,48,50,128,177,179]],["Names and fame","Talbot's name terrifies the French; Lucy recites his titles; Joan mocks them. Fame is what remains of a hero.",[64,69,70,170,171]],["Fathers and sons","Henry V and his son, Talbot and John, Mortimer and York, the Shepherd and Joan: inheritance shapes every character.",[2,154,159,166,191]],["Fire and light","Comets, torches, a circle in water and Joan's curse of darkness mark omens, glory and doom.",[0,33,114,197]],["Faction","Blue coats and tawny, white and red: badges show how the lords divide England.",[11,102,111,147,152]]],"bigq":[["Is Joan a saint or a witch?",["She claims a vision of the Virgin and wins as she promised","The English call her witch, and in 5.3 she calls up fiends","Her power is often in persuasion, not magic","Her final denial of her father and shifting claims undermine her"],[25,50,128,177,191,195]],["Who is to blame for Talbot's death?",["York refuses to move without Somerset's cavalry","Somerset withholds the horse out of pride","The King divided the command and wore a red rose","Lucy: 'the fraud of England, not the force of France'"],[137,145,149,152]],["Is Henry VI a good king?",["He is pious and peaceful and sees the danger of division","He cannot command his nobles and makes peace by tears","He restores York and breaks his marriage contract","Exeter: it is dangerous 'when sceptres are in children's hands'"],[102,105,109,139,174]],["Is Talbot a hero or a relic?",["He embodies loyalty, courage and honour","He cannot defeat Joan or understand her","His chivalry depends on nobles who no longer share it","His death closes an age"],[60,133,152,166]],["How does the play present women?",["Joan, the Countess and Margaret all threaten English men","Each is linked to France and to deceit","Each is also clever and articulate","The play ends with one dangerous woman burned and another crowned"],[27,66,187,207]],["Does the play glorify war?",["Talbot's heroism is celebrated","War is also brutal: Salisbury's death, Bordeaux, the Talbots","Its causes and losses come from faction at home","The ending makes peace look like defeat"],[46,65,140,199]]],"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present the theme of division in Henry VI, Part 1?",[11,75,89,102,111,152]],["Explore the presentation of Joan la Pucelle in the play.",[25,27,50,128,177,195]],["How does Shakespeare present Talbot as a hero?",[60,64,70,133,159,166]],["How is the relationship between fathers and sons presented?",[2,154,159,163,166,191]],["Explore how Shakespeare presents kingship in the play.",[3,102,109,137,139,204]],["How does Shakespeare present women and power?",[27,66,183,187,207]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (soliloquy, imagery, rhyming couplets, irony, structure, parallel scenes) and its effect","Linked to context where it helps the argument (Hall's chronicle, the real Joan, Elizabeth I)","Considered more than one interpretation, especially of Joan","Referred to the beginning, middle and end of the play","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"models":[[89,"Warwick's prophecy turns a private quarrel into national history.","The roses, plucked in a garden, will 'send between the red rose and the white a thousand souls to death'.","The colours of flowers become the colours of blood.","The audience, knowing the Wars of the Roses, feels the irony of a quarrel about a legal point."],[166,"Talbot's death holding his son is the emotional climax of the play.","'Now my old arms are young John Talbot's grave' makes the father's embrace a tomb.","The contrast of 'old' and 'young' shows the end of a line, not just a man.","He dies because York and Somerset failed him, so his grief is also an accusation."],[128,"Burgundy's response to Joan shows the play's uncertainty about her power.","'Either she hath bewitch'd me with her words, or nature makes me suddenly relent.'","The line offers two explanations, magic or natural feeling, and does not choose.","Joan's real weapon here is rhetoric: a vivid image of France as a mother wounded by her own son."],[137,"Henry's red rose shows how innocence can be dangerous in a king.","He insists he sees 'no reason' why wearing it should suggest he favours Somerset.","York sees exactly that reason, and the gesture deepens the feud.","Henry's goodness lacks the political sense his father had."],[152,"Lucy's verdict sums up the play's view of England's defeat.","'The fraud of England, not the force of France' uses alliteration to set two causes against each other.","The blame falls on English nobles, not French enemies.","The line connects the loss of France with the coming civil war."],[207,"Suffolk's final couplet reveals a new threat to England.","He boasts that Margaret will 'rule the king', and he will rule her, the King and the realm.","The rising list of what he will rule shows his ambition.","The play ends not with peace but with the start of the next conflict."]],"lang":{"un":[["81","Glory is like a circle in the water,","Fame spreads like a ripple until it disappears."],["324","A thousand souls to death and deadly night.","The quarrel will kill a thousand men."],["633","The fraud of England, not the force of France,","English treachery, not French strength."],["674","Now my old arms are young John Talbot's grave.","My son dies in my arms; they hold his body like a grave."]],"pun":[["156","Pucelle or puzzel, dolphin or dogfish,","puzzel","pun","Pucelle (maid) and puzzel (slut); Dauphin and dolphin."],["288","I love no colours, and without all colour","colour","pun","Colours (badges, flags) and colour (pretence)."],["368","Roam thither, then.","Roam","pun","Rome and roam sounded alike."],["428","Our sacks shall be a mean to sack the city,","sack","pun","Corn sacks and sacking a city."],["606","And they shall find dear deer of us, my friends.","dear deer","pun","Costly (dear) and deer: the English will sell their lives dearly."],["772","He talks of wood: it is some carpenter.","wood","pun","Margaret hears Suffolk's 'wooden' and thinks of carpentry; 'wood' also meant mad."]],"st":[["674","Now my old arms are young John Talbot's grave.",[["old","Set against 'young': the end of a line."],["grave","The final word of Talbot's life."]]],["633","The fraud of England, not the force of France,",[["fraud","Alliteration sets fraud against force."],["England","The blame lies at home."]]],["324","Shall send between the red rose and the white",[["red","The colour of Lancaster and of blood."],["white","York's colour, soon stained."]]]]},"dir":[["2","Hung be the heavens with black","How should Bedford begin the play?",[["Slowly and solemnly, over the coffin","The funeral sets a tone of mourning for an age."],["Loudly, almost angrily, to the sky","Grief already shading into rage."],["Broken, close to tears","Personal loss rather than state ceremony."]]],["63","Dauphin, I am by birth a shepherd's daughter","How should Joan deliver her account of her vision?",[["With quiet, simple sincerity","Make the audience half believe her."],["Confidently, like a performer","Suggest she knows how to win a court."],["Ecstatically, as if seeing it again","Stress the supernatural."]]],["286","pluck a white rose with me","How should Plantagenet pluck the rose?",[["Calmly, as a legal gesture","The future war begins almost casually."],["Defiantly, staring at Somerset","The personal hatred is already there."],["Slowly, looking at the audience","Invite us to see the history to come."]]],["586","I see no reason, if I wear this rose,","How should Henry put on the red rose?",[["Innocently, smiling, to show it means nothing","His goodness is the problem."],["Carelessly, without looking at York","Show his blindness to its effect."],["Hesitantly, aware of the risk","Suggest he knows more than he says."]]],["674","Now my old arms are young John Talbot's grave","How should Talbot speak his last line?",[["Tenderly, to the body","Death as a father's embrace."],["Proudly, to the soldiers","Honour fulfilled."],["Faintly, barely audible","The end of an age, quietly."]]],["852","May never glorious sun reflex his beams","How should Joan deliver her curse?",[["With terrible power, as a prophetess","Her curse will come true in the later plays."],["Hysterically, as a broken prisoner","Stress her defeat."],["Coldly, with contempt","She is still in control of her words."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["286","301"],["286","324","674"],["343","347","313"],["26","167","469"],["179","837","852"],["549","674","694"],["286","288","301"],["343","771","888"],["160","606","633"],["69","771","888"],["179","439","469"]],"cr":{"faction":[["305","324","586"],["586","633","104"]],"honour":[["252","549","674"],["478","549","659"]],"women":[["69","179","841"],["888","771","179"]],"witch":[["69","837","841"],["837","852","841"]],"kingship":[["586","771","888"],["415","586","704"]],"legacy":[["324","328","674"],["343","659","674"]]}}};
